      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>AN epitaph for a faithful tram-car conductor—he took his last fare well. IF you value the friendship of your friend, never agree with him when he calls himself a fool. ON ONE CONDrrioN.—" Do you believe in dreams ? " "Yes—if they come true." THE desire of some men to wobble round in a big place rather than fill a small one, accounts for many reversals of fortune. MEN are frequently like tea—their real strength and goodness are not properly drawn out until they have been in hot water. FIRST Gossip: "Do you believe that awful story they are telling about Miss Prim ? " Second gossip; "Yes. What is it?" A POLITE TRAMP.—Judge : "What is your name?" Tramp: "Allow me to exchange cards with your honour." " BRIDGET, did you hear me call?" "Yes, mum, but you told me the other day never to answer you hack." A COLLEGE Student is reported as saying that on the whole he rather enjoys his studies. They furnish a needed relaxation from his athletic work. "WHAT kind of a memory have you?" "Bad —very bad." "In what particular?" "It remembers things that were better forgotten." : "Mabel, tell me, do you love me ? " She: "Yes, darling; I would die for you." He: "No, dear ; I prefer your hair as it is at present." PARENT : "This is your birthday, Tommy. 'What can I do for you that will cause you pleanure ?" Tommy : "Spank Johnny!" WIGGLE: "How long did you know your wife before you married her, Satupon ? " Satupon (mournfully) : "I didn't know her at all till I married her."</p>
        <p>-- - - - - - - - - - IT is astonishing how much scorn, indignation, and contempt a woman can put into two words. If you do not believe it, just listen while she speaks of someone she dislikes us "that man." -</p>
        <p>- TOM: "has Harry paid you that money he borrowed? ' Dick: "No, and I don't care if he never does" Tom: "You don't?" Dick: "No. I have the fun of seeing him trying to avoid meeting me." A BICYCLE dealer has utilised the happy thought of presenting a pair of crutches and a box of court plaster to each purchaser of a bicycle. He is monopolising the trade. A GOOD story is told of the Indians, who replied 'when a missionary asked them if they were willing to abstain from work on Sunday: "Yes, and not only on Sunday, but on all other days as well." ACCORDING to an old-superstition of the mediwval Church, whenever a cock crows a lie is being told. The reason that cocks crow so persistently in the early morning is because the morning papers are 'being set up. "THERE are two things," remarked Fogg, in a contemplative mood, "that I don't understand. Ons of these is, how the world got along before I came into it; and the other, how it is going to get along after I have left it." DAWKINS : "I was a great friend of your late husband. Have you any little thing of his you could let me have to remind me of him?" Discon- solate widow: "What's the matter with me?" Exit Dawkins. MOTHER: "Johnny on your way home from school stop at the shop and get me a sugarstick and a bar of soap." Father: "What do you want with a sugarstick ? " Mother: " That's so he'll remember the soap ! " THE trouble with a young fellow in love is, that he imagines everybody else is in love with the girl too; while, very likely, no one but himself ever</p>
        <p>looks at her. ALGY : " Weginald, what did you say, you know, when you were pwesented to the Pwinee of Wales?" Reginald (American dude, who'd been presented to H.R. : "Why, deah boy, the first thing I did was to apologise for the Amewican Wevolution." FRANK: "I hear that young Simpkins started to commit suicide when his girl jilted him. Did he carry out his intention ? ' Jack: "No. He met another girl on the way, and fell in love with her."</p>
        <p>UNDERSTOOD THE Sic.—Hostess: "Why do the Chinese cripple the feet of their girl babies so inhumanly ? " Traveller : "To save their darlings lifelong suffering." " How can it?" "When Chinese girls grow up they are able to wear small shoes without torture." A SMALL Boys VlEWS.—Mother: "There goeS another one of those dirty tramps. I can't see why they don't kevp clean." Small Son: " Mebby w'en they was little their mothers made them wash so often they got tired of it." JAKE : "Here's an advertisement in th' paper fer that dog you found. The man wot owns him offers a reward." Jim: "how d'ye know it's a man?" Jake " Th' paper says no questions asked." A COUNTRY lady, who was recently in London, astounded a female pickpocket, who, in an omni- bus, had thrust her hand into the lady's pocket, by turning and coolly inquiring, "Why do you put your hand in my pocket, when I have my purse here in my hand ?" NEVER WAS WEST.—Veteran : "I presume you never heard an Indian war-whoop ? " Civilian: " No ; but I've heard boys coming out of school." "WILLIE," said the young mother to her abject slave, "you must go to work and raise a beard right away." " What for ? " asked the bewildered husband. "To amuse baby. His grandpa was here this afternoon, and it would have done your heart good to see how the little darling enjoyed pulling the old man's whiskers. He pulled some of them clean out." "YES, I'm in the lecture business," said the long-haired passenger, "and I'm making money. I've got a scheme, I have, and it works to a charm. Big houses wherever I go." "A scheme?" "Yes. I always advertise that my lectures are especially for women under thirty years of age and men out of debt. You just ought to see the way the people come trooping in." TITUS Titmouse was infuriated, but the editor of the "Western Wind" shut him up in two seconds. "Is this the newspaper office ? ' inquired Mr. Titmouse. "it is," responded the ma!n at the desk. "Didn't this paper say I was a liar?" and • he began prancing. "it did not." "Didn't it say I was a scoundtel ? "It did not." "Well, some paper said it." "Possibly it was our contemporary ,down the street," suggested the editor, as he picked up a paper-weight. "This piper never prints stale news. ' " You and Freddie don't seem as friendly as formerly," said one young man to another. "Oh, we are just as fwientll2,' as evah, I assure you." "But you never go about together any more." "We cawn't, don't you know; we both have new suits of clothes, and the colours don't hahmonise. Walking down the stweet togethah we'd be a per- fect fwialit."</p>
        <p>A MINUTE WITH YOU PLEASE</p>
        <p>Sufferers from Gr:x nbgo, Piles, Pains in the Back Dropsy Wind d r ilphiUt. Diseas aot Kidneys 331 odder Stone S,.' I•ll,,niatimi t,.nd Clout wili find a positiv? Cure i„ ravel Pills. Try a small box and J( not satisfied !, Ibe returned. Priees Is. 10, If all Chernii ,:3 rl2 stamps, iron-, iloLottoYD's Medical Hall, Yorxs. iton't be put oft, If you (umnot .:-t ; nen; writ, the Proprietor and a box will seat, next fienit..--Adt,t.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>WIT DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I notice that ladies are still wearing walking pelisses as well as cape. 4 for visiting; the former are extremely elegant on tall figures. They can be made either in tight, semi, or entirely loose shapes. Coloured cloths and coarse ribbed crepon make very smart pelisses; they should be finished with trimmings of velvet and passementerie, as it is a little late to procure new furs. The new capes are cut long enough to just cover the waist. A pretty model in black velvet fits fiat over the shoulders. The cape is completed with a Medici collar, edged with ostrich tips. Another cape of bright red cloth has a square yoke of black braid- ing worked on the material. This is bordered, together with the skirt, with a narrow edging of black fur. Tailor coats with short full ba,sques arc made with the full number of seams, each one heavily strapped; they cannot be described as pretty, but nevertheless they are being worn. A very pretty coat and skirt can be made in grey tweed. The coat, which has a sacque back, i 3 lined with brocade and has a small yoke strapped</p>
        <p>across back and front. There is a very loose, double-breasted front, fastening at the left side with two large smoked pearl buttons, and Bishop sleeves. High roll over collar, lined with green velvet, with cuffs to match. The popularity of large hats seems likely to con- tinue, in spite of their inconvenience in bad weather; they are frequently composed of felt or velvet, trimmed with ribbon, feathers, and flowers. We are in no way restricted in the matter of trim- ming large hats, bows and feathers can be placed symmetrically at the front, or the trimming may be massed at the side or the back. Neat little toque-shaped hats are as pretty as anything for smart occasions, sonic with crowns of bright coloured velvet have a network of jet beads spread over them, other are studded with spark- ling beads or sequins. These hats are usually finished with a full scarf-like border of the Fame velvet, and groups of velvet flowers, tied in little posies, and laid on either side of the front brim. with a high bow of white lace, or an aigrette to stand erect on one side. As for bonnets, an</p>
        <p>exceedingly pretty one is in electric-blue velvet with black plumes and steel ornaments. These latter enrich the crown.</p>
        <p>Capital dresses for girls for every day wear are made in coarse diagonals, hopsacks and faced cloths with Louis velveteen in some rich deep colour. The yoke portion is made on a firm lining, and the frock is attached to this, the widths gored up- wards, to reduce the bulk at the top. If 40-inch material is employed, there should be three widths, or two and a half widths for a small girL The velvet collar and shaped piece below are cut out and laid on the firm lining, but the revers, which are the same at bask and front, are made separately, on stiff union or an ordinary stout canvas, and only attached at the inside edge. The sleeve is first out in coat style, but with a wide top, and then faced to the elbow, and the seams sewn and wrists finished. The puff is joined up and gathered top and bottom, then turned inside out, and sewn over the part below the elbow,, then turned again, and gathered along to the top of the sleeve, setting most of the fulness over the top of the arm. If thin material is used, the lower part should be lined throughout with linen or silesia. For a girl</p>
        <p>of some fifteen or sixteen summers and winters is a frock designed and made with a skirt of golden brown and blue crepon, and a bodice of shot silk, with pointed bretelles of velvet hemmed with bronze sequin trimming. Your loving JENNIL</p>
