      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The presence of Prince Albrecht at the funeral of Prince Henry of Battenberg, and Count Hateeldt at the obsequies of Lord Leighton are courtesies which may perhaps be taken to show that the Kaiser is encleavour- ing to do his best to counteract the unfavourable im- pression produced upon our minds by his tactless tele- gram to President Kruger. Prince .Albrecht who is frequently employed on such ceremonial missions is the tallest of the Hoherzollerns, standing about six feet,four inches, and is a fine looking, genial, scldierly figure. The date fixed fcr the christening of the see,:md little son of tho Duke and Duchess of Ycik is tke 17th of this I month, the ceremony having been postponed owing to</p>
        <p>TO'3IIOTRERS.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers for theirchildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the peon sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste it producer natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and ths little cherub awakes "as bright as kbutton." Of all chemists, 18.1. d. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>the death of Prince Henry of Battenberg. It is now more than probable that one of the names bestowed on the baby will be Henry,alid ag in the Cciti6 6f his brother, little Prince Edward, the font, or rather the christening bowl will be surrounded with white heather. That used at the White Lodge came from Lancashire, but as none is to be found in bloom at this time of the year, the greenhouse and not the moors will have to be depended on. The white heather and the myrtle find much favor with the Royal family and at Osborne there is a cellec- tion of plants grown from slips taken from the bridal bouquets of the Queen's daughters and daughters-in- law.</p>
        <p>Speaking of the christening of the infant Prince re- I minds me that the vicinity of Balmoral is peopled with Alberts and Victorias, the Queen being always very ready to allow the elder children of any of her tenants to bear these names. Albert Brown's sister is named Vic- toria, the head gamekeeper has a daughter Victoria Stewart, a late forester left a daughter Victoria Grant, who is now housekeeper at Balmoral, and a son Albert; Mrs. Macdonald,the Queen's tead-dresser has a daughter Victoria, and the postmaster of the little Crathie office is one Albert Thomson. Apropos, a little item of statis- tics which may prove interesting to those of my readers who are not altogether acquainted with it is,that during the year in which the Prince and Princess of Wales were married, about 1,500 girl-children were baptized Alexandra and about 3,000 boss were named Albert Edward.</p>
        <p>Court gowns are seriously occupying the fashionable woman's mind at the present moment, and there is very little doubt that each year these particular creatione grow less and less becoming. The actual court bodice suits very few women now they take so much exercise, for their shoulders are too square to lend themselves with grace to this old-fashioned style of garment, and it is quite the exception now-a-days to see sloping shoulders. Although it is impossible to wear high dresces at the Drawing-room without permission, and a doctor's certi- ficate has to accompany the regret.there are, at the pre- sent time much easier rules in actual force,and medium low dresses and even square bodices are occasionally seen as well as elbow sleeves. However, they are not indulged in by the very smartest women. It appears, by the way,the Tsaritza of Russia has ordained that for the future all women received by tier shall kiss her hand presentations being made alter the English fashion. This is rather a blow to the Russian women, who infinitely preferred the Dowager Empress's gracious manner of dispersing with the more ceremonious hand-kiss and be- stowing a friendly hand-shake instead. There is little doubt that a new and splendid industry is opening up in Ireland, for the excellent peat rugs which made their first appearance in London quite re- cently are gaining approval in many quarters. Not only rugs,but men's suits can be made of this peat, and the Duchess of York has already added to her wardrobe several peat gowns.</p>
        <p>The very newest corsets are composed of ribbons, which are cleverly crossed and manipulated to the exact moulding required ,and here and there where needed they have a soft padding of plush. A pair which I saw last week, and immensely admired,were made of pale Parma violet ribbon,lined with pink,and scented with violets, while the stocking-suspenders were made of ribbons to match.</p>
        <p>Now that all things are preferably white and black, white feathers are in considerable request for ornament- ing our head-gear. These feathers are worn standing up high and stiff,a little to the left side, but although this particular style may be the very smartest in Paris, one could hope for some modification here as it is by no means becoming to the majority of us. A bishop on a bicycle has not yet been seen, but it is coming. One of the latest interesting items of news from New Zealand—the land of the lady mayor, female suffr- age, and the incipient prohibition of intoxicants—is the announcement that the Rev. Dr. Churchill Julius,Bishop of Christchurch, has been seen propelling a tricycle. Bishop Julius had already acquired the reputation of being th( most unconventional prelate in the Anglican communion. His Quixotic feat of laying the topmost brick of his restored cathedral spire will be fresh in our recollection as will also a photograph of his courageous lordship seated in a chair at the end of a rope and sus- pended in mid-air,which was reproduced in the London illustrated weeklies. Dr. Julius, who was formerly an Islington vicar, is a vigorous and at times sarcastic preacher. At a town he visited during a recent tour he thus addressed his congregation :—" I never saw so much bad coin in all my life. To offer to the Church— the cause of God—money that the butcher and the baker refuse to accept, shocks me. The man who would do that wants the grace of God very badly—or three , months."</p>
        <p>Why in the name of red tape and all that is most in- comprehensible to the ordinary intelligence, has not the absurd rule of turning out tbe cyclists in Hyde Park at twelve o'clock been abolished P—lt has been proved to be quite an unnecessary rigour, for, although the carriages with which they are supposed to interfere are mustered in great force whilst the cycling is going on, they dis- appear as if by magic directly it is over. Now at this time of yearstwelve o'clock is the nicest hour of the day, and from twelve to one is the time which most people choose for a stroll in the Park. Nobody wants to cycle in the afternoon but everyone seems to wish for unlimited cycling in the morning, and there seems no reason why they should not have their wish. When taking one's constitutional, to notice the expression on the faces of the policemen placed to guard this up-to-date traffic, is really intensely amusing—they look so indulgent, like stalwart nursemaida surveying the gambols of their charges. Asa matter of fact the antics indulged in by some of the fair wheelwomen are a little peculiar, and the way in which numbers of them calmly precipitate themselves from their machines, when they see anyone they wish to speak to, without the smallest warning to the three or four dnzPn coming behind. is productive, _ _ .</p>
        <p>fretpiently, of an epidemic of wild wobbliness. Another funny thing to witness is the deep look of doubt with which the less experienced survey the turnings at each end, ere they take them, and as these are the points at which most tumbles are sustained, a student of human nature will not need to be told that at them are always assembled the largest numbers of onlookers. However there is no doubt, that whatever may be the humorous incidents, the wheeling brigade seem greatly to enjoy themselves.</p>
        <p>Speaking of red tape "reminds me of a good story, told by Sir John Adye. One day at Simla an old artil- lery officer called and requested Sir John to give him a certificate as to his being alive,as the Audit Office refusecl to give him his pay without it. He seemed to be well and lively, and Sir John therefore complied at once, and as his visit was in August, dated it accordingly. On looking a% it however, the officer remarked. "Oh ! you have dated it August This is of no use. I have already _ _</p>
        <p>sent them one of that kincl,but what they require is a cer- tificate that I was alive in "July." This opened out a new aspect of the case, but after consideration Sir John certi- fied that to the best of his belief the nsan was living the previous month.</p>
        <p>It seems quite a long time since Sir Walter Besant or Mr. James Payn ran a serial through one of the month- lies, and it is welcome news that the present year is to see both these established favorites reverting to this form of publication. The author of "All Sorts and Conditions of Men "is contributine "The Master Craftsman "to the journal of Messrs. Chambers' while Mr. Paynissues shortly "The Disappearance of George Driffell "in the periodical of which he has had charge since the retire- ment of Mr. Leslie Stephen (Thackeray's successor) in 1832.</p>
        <p>"The Sign of the Cross," which by the way is wonder- fully put upon the stage, continues to draw large audiences to the Lyric. Each scene is like an Alma Tadema picture. One beautiful dress is pale blue em- brni,,ered with big .emeralds, a pale yellow dress is studded with turqunsies and has draperiea of turquoise blue. The Empress Norma wears primrose and rose coloured silk, with jewels, rubies and diamonds on her neck and her flaxen hair, and Marcus Superhus is magnificent in his armour ablaze with jewels. In the revels in Marcus's house colour runs riot, and all the splendour forms a perfect setting to the central figure— the heroine in simplest white with her pretty pale face her dark eyes, and dark hair unbound. The Avenue is another very fashionable theatre jpist now,but Michael's beautiful" *Lost Angel" is withdrewn. On Saturday evening the last performance was given at the Opera Comique of the " Trilby" burlesque, and Miss Nellie Farren has retired from the cares of menagement. The end has come unexpectedly, for Miss Earren had hoped to appear herself in a little piece expressly written for her by Mr. William Yardly,who had provided her with a part in which she WU to have been accommodated with a seat on the bench in the quality of presiding judge in a woman's court of law. Fish Pie is a delicious way of using up cold fish. Take half a pound of fish that has been previously cooked,re. move it from the bones, flake it and put it in a pie dish —with pepper,salt, and one tablespoonful of milk or cold fish sauce. Put half a pound of cold potatoes through a wire seive, or mash smoothly with a fork. Melt half an ounce of dripping or butter in a saucepan. add to it pepner and salt and rile tablespoonful of milk, acid to the potatoes and stir until smooth and hot. Place them on the top ef the fish, smooth over with a knife, mark round neatly and rough the top with a fork. Place on a baking-sheet in a hot oven for ten minutes to brown. Serve hot.—" Madge."</p>
        <p>Immersion in salt water is said to make wood hard and more durable. -</p>
        <p>The quill pens now used in England come from Ger- many and the Netherlands. German children in the mountains of Silesia devoutly believe that beetles are both deaf and blind. The East Indian ship worm will in a few months des- troy any vessel by eating out the interior of the beams Fn d planks. They will be left a mere shell that can be shattered by the f,st.</p>
        <p>RUPTURES.—REYNOLDS' Patent Imperceptible Indiarubber Stem Pad TRUSS. Suitable in all cases. Perfect Fit Ensured. Write or call for particulars.— REYNOLDS, 10, Slater-treet (a few doors off Bold-street), Liverpool. Open on Saturday until? p.ni.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY FEBRUARY 8, 1896</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY FEBRUARY 8, 1896</p>
        <p>In Dalmatia it Is an exceedingly auspicious 'omen for a cat to sneeze when a bride enters her new home. Parrots are to be put to a praT.tical use in Germany. They have been introduced into the railway stations and trained to call out the name while the train stands there, and thus save people the trouble of making inquiries. It is claimed that the perfume of flowers disappears as soon as the starch in the petals is exhausted ; and it may, it is said, be restored by placine the flower in a solution of sugar, when the formation of starch and the emission of fragrance will be at once resumed. How little the population of France moves about is shown by the last census. Out of 38 million inhabitants, 21 millions live in the town or village in which they were born,and thirty and a half millions have not moved out of their native departments. Only a million and a half have emigrated to France from colonies or foreign countries.</p>
        <p>Individual plates for table use were unknown to the ancients,who held their meat in their hands or employed the fiat wheaten cakes then made on which to hold their victuals. They are first mentioned in A.D. 600, as used by the luxurious on the Continent,and in the ninth century they had come into common use both in Eng- land and on the Continent. They were made of wood or some kind of earthenware, the former material being preferred because it did not dull the knives.</p>
        <p>Care of the Tooth Brush.</p>
        <p>While on the subject of the teeth, we should like to point out that the care of tooth brushes is not suffi- ciently observed. They are placed in their receptacles or hung on their racks day and night, absorbing any disease germs that may be floating abut. They should be washed frequPntly—at least about twice a week—in some antiseptic solution, strong salt and water or bicar- bonate of sodium and water being two good and readily provided cleansers. Tooth washes and pastes should also be kept carefully covered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Sugar and the Teeth.</head>
        <p>Sugar and the Teeth.</p>
        <p>Dr. Vaughan Harley, in the Physiology Section of the British Medical Association, read a paper upon "Sugar as a Food." In addition to praising its virtues be denied some of the vices generally ascribed to this substance. The popular view that sugar ruins the teeth is not borne out by observation. People accustomed to take large quantities of sugar are not more liable to have bad teeth than their more abstemious neighbours, but on the con- trary, have in many cases exceptionally fine ones; in support of this contention Dr. Flarley cited the negroes of the West Indies, who are in the habit of taking large quantities of sugar and:whose teeth are unexcelled as to soundness ad whiteness.</p>
        <p>Curious Charge Against Cycles. The bicycle, which is made responsible for all kinds of things good, bad and indifferent, is now charged by a tnbaee.o journal with having enslaved,at low circulation 500,000 males who were formerly addicted to the smoking habit, and who have now abandoned it because it is difficult to ride and smoke at the same time. There is, of course, a corresponding loss to the tobacco trade. The journal argues that if these 500,000 males of the bika craze have weaned themselves from smoking only two cigars less a day—and this is a moderate computa- tion—then the consumption of cigars is decreasing at the rate of 700,000,000 in a year. These figures may be exaggerated, but there is nevertheless a perceptible di- crease in tobacco consumption, and it is no discredit to the wheel that in that direction it 15 encouraging economy. It has also assisted the cause of temperance by diminishing the cc nsumption of intoxicants. rho rider is obliged to keep a cool head, and cannot, there- fore, !drink much ; the amusement, moreover, is so healthful that instead of creating a :desire for artificial stimulants, it inclines the rider to innocent draughts,and best of all, to fresh and cool water.</p>
        <p>Fruit for Health. Dr. Ballard tells us that as a medicine he looks upon fruit as a most valuable ally. When the body is in that breaking-up condition known as scurvy, the whole medical profession look upon fruit aud fresh vegetables as the one and only known remedy. The day will come when science will use it very much more largely than .it does now in the treatment of many of the every-day ail- ments. Observations in scurvy prove that it exerts a very powerful influence on the blood. But "the blood is the life " ; pcor blood means poor spirits,poor strength, poor breath, and poor circulation. Impure blood means gout. rheumatism, skin diseases, rickets, and other troubles. As it is proved that fruit will purify and improve the quality of the blood, it must follow fruit is both food and medicine combined. In fevers. Dr. Ballard employs grapes and strawberries, giving them to his patients in small but frequent doses, and oranges and baked apples, if the others are not obtainable. For rheumatism, plenty of lemons are invaluable. White girls with miserable pallid complexions want a quart of strawberries a day ; where these are not obtainable, bananas, which contain much iron, are a good substi- tute. Probably, of all fruits,the apple stands unrivalled for general purposes in the household ; either raw or cooked it can be taken by nearly everybody, and it con- tains similar properties to the other more delicate fruits. The pear is more easily digested than the apple, and tor eating uncooked is superior to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LATE PRINCE OF BATTENBERG.</head>
        <p>THE LATE PRINCE OF BATTENBERG.</p>
        <p>The remains of Prince Henry of Battenburg. were re- moved from the Blenheim to the Alberta on Tuesday. The Royal yacht steamed through the Flying Squadron in Cowes roads,the flagship saluting with "minute guns." The Queen went on board the yacht and placed a wreath on the bier. The interment of the remains of the late Prince Henry of Battenberg took place on Wednesday at St. Mildred's Church, Whippingham. The coffin was carried from the Alberta and placed on a gun carriage. A procession was then formed, and, to the dull booming, of the minute guns fired by the ships of the Flying Squadron, moved slowly through the streets of Cowes. The Queen, the Princess Beatrice and her children. the Prince and Prin- cess of Wales, and most of the other members of the Royal Family followed the gun carriage. The brief but impressive ceremony in the church was conductod by the Bishop of Winchester, and at the close a firing party fired three volleys. Funeral services were held at the English Church in Paris and at St. George's Church in Berlin. The German Emperor, the Empress, and the Empress Frederick were present at the service in Berlin. A service was also held in Westminster Abbey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>MOTHER'S PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>A MOTHER'S PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>The mother watches the growth of her with wonder. She studies all the intricate problems of health her child represents as it grows up, but how often does she stop to think that these problems so near her heart apply to all humanity? I would emphasise the fact that at all ages of life the laws of nature are the same. Childhood, I will admit, is the most interesting period, for then is presented the problem of growth; but in general terms, life may be said to be no more than a constant strife between supply and waste. You eat so that your body may be supplied with nourishment. When you begin to lose flesh, in spite of care and food, you may depend upon it your system needs your flesh to increase your vitality. If you do not take a remedy that creates vitality, you will keep on losing flesh until none is left. Then disease finds an easy victim. Consumption, Scrofula, Aniemia and other wasting diseases do not come into a strong system and proceed at once to take possession. No, there are othee steps first which you may be said to encourage by your own neglect. Here 13 an interesting caseof a little baby that suffered from weakness and emaciation : "2s, MONKLAND STRINT, MIDDLKSBORO,' "Sept. 24th, 1895.</p>
        <p>Me9Arg. SCOTT &amp; BROWN% Ltd., " 47, Farrington-street, London, E.C. " DHASI SIRS,—SIX months ago, my little son, who was just then under a year old, was sniffling from the effects of Inflammation of the Lungs. He was very thin, weak, and pale, and seemed to be wasting away. He had no appetite, and I began to think he would not live. He seemed utterly exhausted all the time ; was listless, fretful, and did not seem to have strength enough to keep him alive. " I began a course of treatment of Scott's Emulsion, and I saw an improvement in his condition almost from the start. He gained in strength and flesh and is now quite well again, thanks to your invaluable remedy— Scott's Emulsion. The Emulsion seemed to be just what the child needed to give him flesh, strength and vitality. Yours very truly, SAM EVANS." Now, there are thounands of just such oases. Babies are born with weak tendencies, and are unable to over- come them without the aid of a nourishment like Scott's Emulsion, designed to enrich the blood, make healthy flesh, and give vitality. There are two features of Scott's Emulsion, besides those mentioned, which give it a unique advantage over other preparations as a children's remedy. These are its sweet taste and its material for healthy bone formation. Its sweet taste makes its administration easy, while the form of lime it contains is especially useful during the teething period, and in fact at all stages of growtla,when the bones need lime for solidity. - . .</p>
        <p>_ Scott's Emulsion combines the essential principles of nourishment in an easy form for digestion and assimila- tion. It increases vitality, makes flesh, enriches the blood, and overcomes wasting. It is of great benefit at all ages of life to insure a proper nutrition.</p>
        <p>THE HIDDEN HAND;</p>
        <p>CAPITOLA THE MADCAP. 131 r laus. E. D. E. N. SouTawoßTH.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIII. THE BOY'S LOVE. Endearing! endearing! g, Why so endearing Are those soft, shining eyes, Through their silk fringe peering fl They love thee! they love thee ! Deeply, sincerely; 4nd more than aught else on earth lovest them dearly !"</p>
        <p>—MOTHERWELL. While These eark conspiracies were hatching else- where, all was comfort, peace, and love in the doctor's quiet dwelling. Under Marah Rocke's administration the business of the household went on with the regularity of clock-work. Every one felt the advantage of this</p>
        <p>_ improved condition. The doctor often declared that for his part be</p>
        <p>could not for the life of him think how they had over been able to get along without Mrs. Rocke and</p>
        <p>Traverse. _ Clara affirmed that however the past might have been, the mother and con were a present and future necessity to the doctor's collators and nappy.</p>
        <p>ness. The little woman herself gained rapidly both health and spirits and good looks. Under favour- able circumstances, Marah Rocke, even at thirty- six, would have been esteemed a first-rate beauty; and even now she was pretty, graceful, and attrac- tive to a degree that she herself was far from sus-</p>
        <p>pecting. Traverse advanced rapidly in his studies, to the ardent pursuit of which he was urged by every generous motive that could fire a human bosom: affection for his mother, whose condition be was anxious to elevate; gratitude to his patron, whose great kindness he wished to justify, and admira- tion for Clara, whose esteem he was ambitious to</p>
        <p>secure. He attended his patron in all his professional visits; for the doctor said that actual experimental knowledge formed the most important part of a</p>
        <p>young medical student's education. The mornings were nsually passed in reading, in the liurary ; the middle of the day in attending the doctor in his professional visits, and the even- ings were passed in the drawing-room with the doctor, Clara, and Mrs. Rocke. And if the morn- ing's occupation was the most earnest and the day's tne most active, the evening's relaxation with Clara and music and poetry was certainly the most delightful. In the midst of all this peace and pros- perity, a malady was creeping upon the boy's heart and brain that in his simplicity and inexperience he could r either understand nor conquer.</p>
        <p>Why was it that these evening fireside meetings with the doctor's lovely daughter, once such unalloyed delight, were now only a keenly pleasing pain? Vey did his face burn and his heart beat and his voice falter when obliged to speak to her? Wily could he no longer talk of her to his mother, or write of her to his friend Herbert Greyson Above all, why had his favourite day-dream of having his dear friends Herbert and Clara married together grown so abhorrent as to sicken his very 6011 i TraverF,e himself could not have answered these qnestions. In his ignorance of life he did not lieow that all his strong, ardent, earnest nature .(1,9 tending towards the maiden by a power of at traction ~eated in the deepest principles of being and of destiny. Chra in her simplicity did not suspect the truth; but tried in every innocent way to enliven the silent boy, an said that he worked too hard, and Legged her father not to let him study too much. Whereupon the doctor would laugh and bid her not be uneasy about Traverse—that the boy was all right and would do very well. Evidently the doctor, with all his knowledge of human nature, did not see that his protege was in process of form- ing an unadvisable attachment for his daughter and heiress. Mrs. Rocke, with her woman's tact and mother's forethought, saw all. She saw that in the honest heart of her poor boy unconsciously there was grow- ing up a strong, ardent, earnest passion for the lovely girl with whom he was thrown in such close, intimate, daily association, and who was certainly not indifferent in her feelings towards him; but whom he might never, never hope to possess. She saw this daily growing, and trembled for the peace of both. She wondered at the blindness of the doctor who did not perceive what was so plain to her own vision. Daily site looked to see the eyes of the doctor open and some action taken upon the circumstances ; but they did tot open to the evil ahead, for the girl and boy For morning after morning their hands would be together tying up the same vines, or clearing out the same flower bed; day after day at the doctor's orders Traverse attended Clara on her rides; night after night their blushing faces would be bent over the same sketch book, chess board, or music sheet. " Ohl if the doctor cannot and will not see, what shall I do ? what ought I to do?" said the conscientious little woman to herse f, dreading above all things, and equally for her son and the doctor's daughter, the evils of an unhappy attachment, which she, with her peculiar tem- perament and experience believed to be the worst of sorrows, a misfortune never to be conquered or outlived. " Yes ! it is even better that we should leave the house, than that Traverse should become hopelessly attached, to Clara; or, worse than all, that he should repay the doctor's great bounty by winning the heart of his only daughter," said Marah Rocke to herself; and so" screwing her courage to the sticking place" she took an opportunity one morning early while Traverse and Clara were out riding, to go into the study to speak to the doctor. As usual he looked up with a smile to welcome her as she entered; but her downcast eyes and serious face made him uneasy, and he hastened to inquire if she was not well, or if anything had happened to make her anxious, and at the same time he placed a chair, and made her sit in it. "Yes, I am troubled, Doctor, about a subject that I scarcely know how to break to you," she said, in considerable embarrassment. "Mrs. Rocke, you know I am your friend, anxious to serve you!. Trust in me, and speak out!"</p>
        <p>"Well, sir," said Marah, beginning to roll up the corner of her apron, in her embarrassment, " I should not presume to interfere, but you do not see gentlemen, perhaps, seldom do until it is too late." She paused, and the good doctor turned his head about, listening first with one ear and then with the other, as if he thought by attentive hear- ing he might come to understand her incompre- hensible words. "Miss Clara has the misfortune to be without a mother, or an aunt, or any lady relative—" " Oh ! yes! I know it my dear madam; but then I am sure you conscientiously try to fill the place of a matronly friend and adviser to my daughter," said the doctor, striving after light. "Yes, sir, and it is in view of my duties in this relation that I say—l and Traverse ought to go away." "You and Traverse go away My good little woman you ought to be more cautious how you shock a man at my time of life! fifty is a very apoplectic age to a full blooded man, Mrs. Rocke! But now that I have got over the shock, tell me why you fancy that you and Traverse ought to go away.' "Sir, my son is a well-meaning boy--" "A high-spirited, noble-hearted lad! " put in the doctor. "I have never seen a better!" "But granting all that to be, what I hope and believe it is—true, still Traverse Rocke is not a proper or desirable daily associate for Miss Day." "Why ? " curtly inquired the doctor. "If Miss Clara's mother were living, sir, she would probably tell you that young ladies should never associate with any Except their equals of the opposite sex," said Marah Rocke. " Clara's dear mother, were she on earth, would understand and sympathise with me, and esteem your Traverse as I do, Mrs. Rocke," said the doctor, with moist eyes and a tremulous voice. "But, oh, sir, exceeding k hulas you are to Traverse, I dare not, in duty, look on and see things going the way in which they are, and not speak and ask your consent to withdraw Traverse ! " "My good little friend," said the doctor, rising and looking kindly and benignantly upon Marah. " My good little woman, sufficient unto the day is the evil thereof 1 ' Suppose you and I trust a little in Divine Providence, and mind our own business ? " "But, sir, it seems to me a part of our business to watch over the young and inexperienced, that they fall into no snare." "And also to treat them with 'a little wholesome neglect' that our over officiousness may plunge them into none 1" "I wish you would comprehend me, sir!" "I do, and applaud your motives; but give your- self no further troublei leave the young people to</p>
        <p>their own honest hearts and to frovioence. uiara, with all her softness, is a sensible girl I and as for Traverse, if he is one to break his heart from an unhappy attachment, I have been mistaken in the lad, that is all I " said the doctor, heartily. Mrs. Rocke sighed, and saying—" I deemed it my duty to speak to you, sir; and having done so, I have no more to say," she slightly curtsied and</p>
        <p>withdrew. He does not see! his great benevolence blinds him! In bis wish to serve us he exposes Traverse to the most dreadful misfortune—the misfortune of becoming hopelessly attached to one far above him in station, whom he can never expect to possess!" said Marah Rocke to herself, as she retired from the</p>
        <p>room. "I must speak to Traverse himself, and warn him against this snare," she said, as she afterwards ruminated over the subject. And accordingly that evening, when she had retired to her chamber and heard Traverse enter the little adjoining room where he slept, she called him In and gave him a seat, saying that she must have some serious conversation with him. The boy looked uneasy, but took the offered chair and waited for his mother to speak. "Traverse," she said, "a change has come over you recently that may escape all other eyes but those of your mother; she, Traverse, cannot be blind to anything that seriously affects her boy's happiness." "Mother, I scarcely know what you mean," said the youth in embarrassment. "Traverse, you are beginning to think too much of Miss Day." "Oh, mother!" exclaimed the boy, while a violent blush overspread and empurpled his face! Then in a little while and in faltering tones he inquired—" Have I betrayed in any way, that I do?" "To no one but to me, Traverse, to me whose anxiety for your happiness makes me watchful; and now, dear boy, you must listen to me. I know it is very sweet to you, to sit in a dark corner and gaze on Clara, when no one, not even herself, witnesses your joy, and to lie awake and think and dream of her when no eye nut that of God looks down upon your heart; and to build castles in the air for her and for you; all this I know is very sweet; but, Traverse, it is a sweet poison, fatal if Indulged in, fatal to your peace and integrity." "Oh, ray mother-I —oh, my mother! what are you telling me!" exclaimed Traverse, oitterly. "Unpalatable truths, dear boy, but necessary antidotes to that sweet poison of which you have already tasted too much. "What would you have me to do, my mother ? " "Guard your acts and words, and even thoughts; forbear to look at, or speak to, or think of Clara, except when it is unavoidable—or if you do, regard her as she is—one so far beyond your sphere as to be for ever unattainable!" "Oh, mother. I never once dreamed of such pre- sumption as to think of—of—" The youth paused, and a deep blush again overspread his face. "I know yeti have not indulged presumptuous thoughts as yet, my boy, and it is to warn you against them, while yet your heart is in some mea- sure within your own keeping, that I speak to you. Indulge your imagination in no more sweet reveries about Miss Day, for the end thereof will be bitter humiliation and disappointment. Remember also that in so doing you would indulge a sort of treachery against your patron, who in his great faith in your integrity has received you in the bosom of his family, and admitted you to an almost brotherly intimacy with his daughter. Honour his trust in you, and treat his daughter with the dis- tant respect due to a princess." "I will, mother. It will be hard, but I will! Oh, an hour ago I did not dream how miserable I should be now!" said Traverse, in a choking voice. "Because I have pointed out to you the gulf towards which you were walking blindfold 1" "I know it. I know it now, mother," said Tra- verse, as he arose and pressed his mother's hand and hurried to his own room. The poor youth did his best to follow out the line of conduct prescribed for him by his mother. He devoted himself to his studies and to the active service of his patron. He avoided Clara as much as possible, and, when obliged to be in her company, he treated her with the most respectful reserve. Clara saw and wondered at this change of man- ner, and began to cast about in her own mind for the probable cause of his conduct. "I am the young mistress of the house," said Clara to herself, "and I know I owe to every in- mate of it consideration and courtesy; perhaps I may have been unconsciously lacking in these towards Traverse, whose situation would naturally render him very sensitive to neglect. I must en- deavour to convince him that none was intended." But happiness and comfort seemed for the tune, to have departed from the youth. He saw her generous endeavours to cheer him, and while ador- ing her amiability grew still more reserved. This pained the gentle girl, who, taking herself seriAusly to task, said: "Oh, I must have deeply wounded his feelings in some unconscioui way! and if so how very cruel and thoughtless of me I how could I have done it I cannot imagine; but I know I shall not allow him to continue unhappy if I can preven, it. I will speak to him about it." And then in the candour, innocence and humility of her soul, she followed him to the window where he stood in a moody silence, and said, pleasantly: "Traverse, we do not seem to be so good friends as formerly. If I have done anything to offend you, I know that you will believe me when I say that it was quite unintentional on my part and that I am very so[ry for it, and hope you will forget It."</p>
        <p>"You, yon. Miss Day! you say anything to dis- please—anybyly! Anyone become displeased with you I" excikdined the youth, in a tremulous enthusi- asm that shook his voice and suffused his cheeks.</p>
        <p>"Then if you are not displeased, Traverse, what is the matter, and why do you call me Miss Day Instead of Clara?" "Miss Day, because it is right that I should. You are a young lady—the only daughter and heiress of Doctor Day of Willow Heights, while lam—"</p>
        <p>"His friend," said Clara. "The son of his housekeeper," said Traverse, walking away. Clara looked after him in dismay for a moment, and then sat down and bent thoughtfully over her needle-work. From that day Traverse grew more deeply in love and more reserved than before. How could it be otherwise, domesticated, as he was, with this lovely girl, and becoming, daily more sensible of her beauty, goodness and intelligence? Yet he struggled against his inevitable attachment as a great treachery. Meantime he made rapid progress in his medical studies. It was while affairs were in this state that one morning the doctor entered the study holding the morning paper in his hand. Seating himself in his leathern arm-chair, at the table, he said: see, my dear Traverse, that a full course of lectures is to be commenced at the medical college in Washington, and I think that you are sufficiently far advanced in your studies to attend them with great advantage—what say you 2" "Oh, sir 1" said Traverse, upon whom the proposition had burst qnite unexpectedly—" I should indeed be delighted to go, if that were possible." "There is no if about it, my boy; if you wish to go you shall do so. I have made up my mind to give you a professional education, and shall not stop half-way." "Oh, sir, the obligation the overwhelming Obligation you lay upon me 1" "Nonsense, Traverse! it is only a capital invest- ment of funds 1 If I were a usurer, boy, I could not put out money to a better advantage 1 You will repay me, by and by, with compound interest; so just consider all that I may be able to do for you as a loan to be repaid when you shall have achieved success." "I am afraid, sir, that that time will never--" "No, you are not 1" interrupted the doctor--" and so don't let modesty run into hypocrisy. 'Now pat up your books and go and tell your good little mother to get your clothes all ready for you to go Washington, for you shall start by the next coach." Much surprise it created in the little household by the news that Traverse was going immediately to Washington to attend the medical lectures. There were but two days to prepare his wardrobe for the journey. Mrs. Rocke went cheerfully to work; Clara lent her willing and skilful aid, and at the end of the second day his clothes, in perfect order, were all neatly packed in his travelling trunk. And on the morning of the third day Traverse took leave of his mother and Clara, and for the first time left home to go out into the great world. Doctor Day accompanied him in the old green gig as far as Staunton, where he took the stage. As soon as they had left the house Marah Rocke went away to her own room to drop a few natural tears over this first parting with her son. Very lonely and desolate the mother felt as she stood ' weeping by the window, and straining her eyes to catch a distant view of the old green gig that had already rolled out of sight.</p>
        <p>While the stood thus in her loneliness and deso Con, the door silently opened, a footstep softlf crossed the floor, a pair of arms was put around her neck, and Clara Day dropped her head upon the =other's bosom and wept softly. Ifi.arah Roche pressed that beautiful form to her breast, and felt with dismay that the doctor's svreot daughter already returned her boy's silent love. ( To be ocntinwd.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>RISE OF JAPAN.</head>
        <p>THE RISE OF JAPAN.</p>
        <p>When the history of the present century comes tolbe recorded (which in a sense it never will be, for we have no complete records of anything) not the least interest' bag feature of the narrative will 'es the summary of the progress of our little go-ahead aesthetic rival, Japan,' Steadily has she emerged from chaotic barbarity, aria having begun by putting her own house in order, she startled the world by the plucky way in which she sub' dued her unwieldy neighbour and brought light into the dull eyes of the Chinaman. Japan may " says" Science Siftings "thus fairly be regarded as a pioneer of • movement which seems likely to draw the peoples of the Occident and the Orient into closer communion, and our markets are already beginning to fest her competitiol very keenly. But Japan is imbued with all the fire of, youth, and she aims not only to secure recognition 0, equality with civilised nations, but to take a place in tbe very front rank. With any inferior place she will not be content. Yet, except those who had personally aided her to win the secrets of the West, few foreigners warB prepared for the exhibition which Japan gave the worla of her commercial and military power in 1895. The ' struggle of modern times is a fight for trade. In thiS, with brains and pen, machinery and bayoneth the Japanese have won and are winning. Cotton is still one the kings. Almost the only muslin woven under Tai-kun or Mikado, until 1875, was b 9 women on hand-looms at home. Now in fifty mills,worr ing, by steam or water power, millions of spindles, plug brain and hands, weave acres of snowy cotton cloth, Sending out their ships, wares, and consuls in eyed j direction,the Japanese are now competing with us for $ Ilion-like share of the world's trade. They have alreacli made vast progress in Korea,China,lndia, and Australia. J Over one-half at their commerce is with English-spealg' j ing nations. American raw cotton is now imported bl thousands of bales. In bulk of trade with Japan, the United States still leads all nations, but we still sell nsOel, goods. The island empire, which Was once poor an°, " hardly worth trading with," enjoyed in 1894 a total foreign trade of 230,00'9,000 yen,or about £37,000,000 gold. Old Japan has vanished, and in new Japan life i 5 more than worth living for the average man. Safi popular freedom and advantages were never before known. Instead of the caste, monopoly,cramping 100, repreFsive customs, and cruel government of former days, the common people live in a new world of righto, privileges, and possibilities. New institutions, code,e' and deals have come in. The land is for the most Pal:, owned by the men who till the soil. The courts are opee to everyone, and justice is cheap and easy to obtain. Schools invite all to enter. Once,only samurai could be soldiers; now the army and navy are filled without re: gard to class by enthusiastic conscripts. The men.; well fed, well paid, well taught,and well nursed in OP, of sickness. The old sectional jealousies, eectsriltjt. bigotries, and pclitical hatreds are vanishing. We3cae comfort, happiness, national unity, and popolatiols 7iee, steadily on the increase. With over two thousand of railway, with telegraphs, lighthouses, post offic''' newspapers, savings banks, hospitals, and most of tbed appliances of modern civilisation,life seems very rich a,1,1Q, full to the lad and lass born in the "era of Meiji " 1895). Japanese travellers and enterprising adventurer?. are now found in many countries. Immigrants by thc).u_ sands dwell in Hawaii, the United States, Austral?: l Mexico, Korea, China, J,nd in British, Russian, Dutcp Spanish,and French Asia. With her population increa!: ing at the rate of over half a million a year, it is neee_'A sexy for Japan to expand and colonise. Her desire " ability to do both are manifest. These facts explain 4 part, also, why so small a nation did not hesitate, Nyl'4 peace no longer possible, to go to war with coloes' China, and to reduce their enemy to their own tering.</p>
      </div>
