      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>"CA 1-) ITA.L" CETAL</head>
        <p>"CA 1-) ITA.L" CETAL</p>
        <p>The British and Russian Governments, in spite of the difficulties in Turkey and the doubts and sus- picions arising out of them, are, 1 am informed, on excellent terms. Russia's attitute in regard to the Armenian question is one of expectation, and she Will embroil herself with no other difficulties until her way in Asia Minor is clear. A diplomatist who knews much of Russia policy told me on Monday that Russia never will act with other Powers, least Of all with England, in regard to Armenia. She Will act alone; but she desires to precipitate no conflict,and therefore shrinks from doing that which may expose her to the hostility of other Powers. Hence she is willing to occupy Armenia as the man- datory of the Powers just as England occupies Zgypt,but she will not do so without such mandate. If England chose to take the first step in this direc- tion the other Powers would naturally follow, for England is the only Power whose hostility Russia would anticipate in the event of an occupation of Armenia. England has taken no such step yet, and 1 Russia will not, advance without a mandate,and will allow no one else to interfere by force. She regards herself as the only Power capable of dealing with Armenia. If any foreign Power has the right to deal with that province she has; and the question now is whether Lord Salisbury will be a party to a mandate to Russia to occupy Armenia and to pro- tect its unfortunate people. If so, lam assured, Russia will move early in the spring. If not, the Armenian atrocities will go on to extermination. Hence the Armenian situation is not so hopeless as Lord Salisbury Unionist ban mandate. quet seemed to say at the Nonconformist Russia is willing to act with a Lord Salisbur Will she get that mandate? It is for Y to answer.</p>
        <p>pres-,1 been I"ent of the Board of Agriculture has au considering, in conjunction with his assistants, ifgesti"B Put forward in the latest report of the tie erinary of Department in favour of the promulga- " a general muzzling order, with a view to the extinction ; 0, rabies. The cases of this frightful disease reported to the Board have alarmingly in- creased• nutnber of late, for whereas there were °llll about 40 throughout the country in 1892,there 7,7„1.700 in 1895. It is probable that an announce- irou the subject will be made soon after the -eu'ug of Parliament.</p>
        <p>star, - Idr. et„,f, ,Ig going about the city to the effect that Which—b" ithodes has got an ace up his sleeve on "(3 Will play with very embarrassing effect this arrival in this country. Those who spread of 47),°11t declare that the trump card is the copy Pre •—eatY which was being negotiated between Gertaident Kruger and the Emperor William for a pr° b aabl n protectorate of the Transvaal. This is been ,iST only one of the many canards that have a"at,but although the treaty story may have nundation,there is a shrewd suspicion that 31 ilh°he des knewthrew well what he was doing when UP his Cape Premiership and took early ship or England.</p>
        <p>°lir municipal authorities have something to .°lllll from their transatlantic brethren in recogm- _"on of the claims of the bicyclist. In Toronto the 'inn of the wheel have become so great a voting ?tower in Civic affairs that the new mayor has laid clown at the outset of his career that in the con- Iltruction of streets hereafter the council should R 1137 special attention to the needs of cyclists b. y .t.P,aving a part of each street with the most suit- !"'le material for them." Where asphalt or brick 18 not laid down "strips should be put in first-class 811813. e for bicycle riders." In fact the needs of the cYclists are to be remembered in the grading and Paving of 'all streets in future. We are far behind that city. '</p>
        <p>A Motor Car Club has just been formed. The Club is registered as a limited liability company,but it is in no sense a trade combination, its main Objects being to hold exhibitions, give prizes, and generally protect, control, and further the motor ear movement. The first fruit of the new associa- tion is the arrangement of an exhibition at the Imperial Institute of motor cars and cycles of every (lesCription and from every country. It will be °Pened early in May, and will last all through the B,lll:Qtner. As a preliminary the Motor Car Club will T" one or two receptions, probably during the 46.1 T month, to Members of the House of Lords Win --lie Rouse of Commons, when the motor cars require° tested and their suitability to modern view , nients demonstrated; this of course with a restilebtoivT Ae crte. peal or amendment of the present</p>
        <p>plitl deputation of five or six members of the British 4enological Association paid a visit recently to ,7r. Horace Wilkinson, of Sevenoaks, who is the „..-Wrier of the head which is reputed to be Cro.m-1 Ze,!rel 'with the view of examining it phrenologi- F"LY. It came into the possession of the Wilkinson 4aoaily during the lifetime of the present owner's pandfather, and although visitors are allowed to : 00k at it, Mr. Wilkinson's rule is to let nobody touch it. In the present instance, however, he tads an exception, and allowed his visitors to run eir fingers over it organs, and to measure it. The had, to whomsoever it belonged, is very large, the `lrcumference being 21 7-Sin., and when it is borne In mind that it is not a bare skull but the head of a Mummy, with the shrivelled flesh still upon it, the pkrenologists say that it must have measured 23in. In life. The head is high and enormously broad, and the organs of caution, benevolence, combative- eB9, secretiveness, philoprogenitiveness, and won- der, are very large. The deputation were of opinion that the organs showed it to be the head of a person of great power of intellect, and of a religious char- a- cte- r, and if it was not Cromwell's it was not that any ordinary man. As against. the 23 inches of _9e head in life it was stated that the circumference el Mr. Chamberlain's head is 21 inches.</p>
        <p>Following on the lines of the late Administration, rthe present Board of Agriculture has arranged nth the Custom-house officials to take samples of Lnported butter on its arrival in this country. In tePteraber and October the Work of taking samples gan Somewhat vigorously,and these samples N$ ere ice'atlYsed. Out of 146 samples, 50 were pronounced th;°.re adulterated. Holland and Germany were the Wei Offenders, 34 samples out of 51 exported by tale former being found adulterated, and no fewer tor"; ten out of eleven from the latter. Analyses porn44ter months still glow adulteration, but the eftti'Ziltage is less. I am informed that communi- -14417," have been sent to the importers, and that aseiirt.th°f them have shown their willingness to th --e Glovernment in the matter, realising that sufrAeY buy the article as pure butter they are 'rete by the fraud. This is as it should be.</p>
