      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>DYSPEPTIC TEAMSTER.</head>
        <p>A DYSPEPTIC TEAMSTER.</p>
        <p>There is something incongruous, at first sight, in the idea of an'a3tive, vigorous man being dyspeptic ; but, unfortunately, such an affliction is no joke to many a hale and sturdy man. Mr. Barns, a teamsman, of 33, Albert-street, South Reddish, a place near Stockport, was, but is happily co longer,an example. He is a sturdy-looking man, per- haps some fifty years of age, the picture of health, and with little appearance of having been so near his end only a few months since. _ .</p>
        <p>"Ii was ea rly in July," he said to a "Stockport Advertiser " reporter, "that something went wrong with my inside, and I was told that it was ulceration of the coat of the stomach. I thought it was only indigestion or dyspepsia myself, though it soon got so bad that I saw it was some thing more. I got worse until I had to stop work."</p>
        <p>" Yes," bro ke in Mrs. Barns. " Whenever he ate anything, it was just like a paving-stone on his stomach. We tried no end of remedies, end I got him all sorts of things to eat,fish and so on ,but no matter, he said,what he ate, nothing went down comfortably. He got so weak, he could not lie in bed even, with comfort, and all the while he was at home he was in terrible pain." " And what was it that relieved him ?"</p>
        <p>" Dr. Willia ms' Pink Pills for Pale People," was the reply. "Be would have been dead before now if he had not taken them. They saved his life." "Ay," said the h usband, " I'll never take any other physic again while I can get Dr. Williams' Pills. They are not like other medicine. "It was one Saturday," added Mr.. Barns, "that I bethought me of these Pills which I had read of in all the papers, and my wife went and bought a box. I took some, and the next morning felt a lot better. About two days I was back-at work again." " Will you describe your symptoms—for the benefit of those similarly suffering ? " "Everything I ate turned hard upon my stomach and hurt me terribly. I had a bad cough and used to , spit up great clots of blood, as much as an egg-cupful at a time. It was killing me by inches." "And now you are all right ? " " I am as well as ever I was—thanks to the Pills." With that the reporter took his leave, convinced that Dr. Williams Pink Pills had worked a miracle in this teamsman's home, as they hav3 ;done in many others. The papers are, as Mr. Barnes said,full of cures wrought by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, not merely in cases of bad digestion, but wherever strengthening, tonic medicine is needed. They have cured numerous cases of disease arising from weakness and an im- poverished state of the blood, such as anwmia, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, loss of appetite, palpitation of the heart, shortness of breath. pains in the back, nervous headache, early decay, all forms of female weakness, hysteria,paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica. They invigorate the blood and system when broken down by over-work, worry, diseases, excesses, and indiscretions of living. These Pills are. not a purgative medicine. They act directly on the blood, supply to the latter its life-saving qualities. They are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by chemists and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viadubt, London, E.C., at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 9d. They are especially good for ladies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held at the Town Hall, on Tuesday. The Mayor (Councillor James Fairclough) presided, and there were also present Alder- men Francomb, Harrison, Blackhurst, Sutton, Bolton, Wright, Hutchinson, Greenall ; Councillors P. Davies, Pierpoint, Shaw-Green, Cannell, Smethurst, Langton, Bennett, Roberts, Shaw, Skelton, Evans, Ellison, Lee, Garner, Hepherd, Wilkinson, Shillcock, Baird, Woods, Rogers, Tinnion, Furness, Monks ; Messrs. Whittle (town clerk), Langdin (borough surveyor), Fairhurst (borough treasurer), and Haddock (gas engineer).</p>
        <p>THE LATE PRINCE OF BATTMNBERG.-A VOTE OF CONDOLENCE.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said that before they proceeded with the ordinary business of the Council, it was his painful duty to bring under their notice the lamentable death of the late Prince Henry of Battenberg, who lost his life in the service of his country in a foreign land. As loyal sub- jects of her Majesty, he thought they should exn ress to her, and also to the bereaved widow, Princess Henry of Battenberg, their heartfelt sympathy with them in their great loss. He, therefore, proposed—" That this Council desires to convey to her Majesty the Queen the expression of its constant loyalty and heartfelt sympathy. This Council has heard with sincere sorrow of the death of his Royal Highness Prince Henry of Battenberg, B.G. (her Mapsty's son-in-law), a calamity which occurred under circumstances peculiarly calculated to evcke the sympathy of the whole nation. This Council prays that her Majesty may be vouchsafed peace and consolation under the sad bereavement she and the Royal Family have sustained. That this Council has received with sentiments of deep regret the sad intelligence of the death, while serving the empire in a foreign clime,of his Royal Highness Prince Henry of Battenberg, K.G., and desires to express to her Royal Highness Princess Beatrice (Princess Henry of Battenberg) its profound and heartfelt sympathy in the irreparable loss she has sustained." Alderman HARRISON secended the motion, remarking that Warrington had always had a reputa- tion for chivalry and loyalty, and it was very gratifying to find that they were not to lose that reputation on the present oceasion. The national mind had bcen thoroughly aroused by the lamentable death of the Prince, which had occurred under most painful circum- stances when he was,it might be said, fighting for his adopted country, although his death did not actually take place on the field of battle. Naturally the sym- pathy and kindliness of the nation which had been en-. listed on behalf of the bereaved Princess and her four children had been extended to the Queen, whose life had been interlaced with dark sorrow and sadness almost ever since the death of Prince Albert. Nothing could be more appropriate than the words of the great lexico- grapher, Samuel Johnson, with regard to the death of the Prince—</p>
        <p>Life's a short summer—man a flower— He dies—alas I how soon he dies. The resolution was then carried. LETTER FROM MR. L. GREENING. The MAYOR read a letter which had been received from Mr. b. Greening,expressing his indebtedness to the Council for the vote of thanks accorded to him at the last meeting for his gift to the Museum. He was pleased to find that his gift had met with the approval of the Corporation.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED INCREASES OF SALARY.- A WITHDRAWAL "FOR THE PRESENT."</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved" that the standing orders (Nos. 35 and 38) referring to salaries of officials bc rescinded." These orders read as follows :—" 35. Each of the several committees may, if they consider it advisable, but once only in every year,consider and report to the Council,at the meeting in the month of February, upon the annual salaries ; and also upon the weekly wages (exceeding £2 per week) of the officers and servants of the Corpora- tion, under the direction and control of such committee. And no application for an increase of salary or weekly wages (which exceed £2 per week) shall be entertained by the Council except on the recommendation or report of the respective committees,to be made ss above men- tioned,and every such recommendation or report shall be taken into consideration at the meeting of the Council to be held ia the month of February. or at any special meeting convened for the purpose. 38. The recommen- dation or report of each committee shall be accompanied by a tabulated return showing the length of service of each salaried officer,and of each servant receiving wages of more than £2 per week in the department referred to in the recommendation or report." The Mayor said he had submitted the resolution because he did not think it was fair that the whole of the salaries should come up for consideration at once in February. Alderman HARRISON seconded the proposal. Councillor ?lERPOINT hoped the seconder of the reso- lution would amplify the reasons for the action that was being taken. Councillor SKELTON wished to know whether, if the resolution of the Mayor was passed, it would prevent a diseussion taking place with regard to the proposed in- croase of salary that night? The MAYOR : All the resolutions with reference to the proposed increases of salaries will be withdrawn for the present if my motion is passed. Alderman SUTTON said he took it that the object of the resolution was to enable the Special Committee ap- pointed by the General Purposes Committee to prepare new standing orders for regulating the future salaries of officials, and he thought it was a very wise resolution. The Council ought to deal with this question in a differ- ent altogether. These matters should be settled before they come to the Council Chamber,because it was always Coon t a very unpleasantto to amatter r increase of amseamlabryer: of the Alderman Bolton objectopposed the proposal, and said that if there r were to be increases of salary they should be considered at the same time, especially when the salary amounted to over £2 a week. At one time a salary had to be increased almost at every Council meeting. Every- a themn dn return e toneo sbhoodoyl dwiaaes sgorrurtny bt loi seen g , things. He also objected to the proposal that and he rnwtoastbseattaisfitede, st ate mm of- .f</p>
        <p>tee of six or seven members should deal with this sub- ject. That should be done by the whole Council. Councillor SHAW GREEN said there was no intention to deprive the Council of the right to question every In- crease of salary. Unless some arrangement like that suggested was come to, the whole of the salaries would have to be discussed that evening.</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPOINT : And why not? Councillor SHAW-GREEN said they would gain more in- formation as to the applications it the Mayor's proposal was adopted. He thought that when all the applications were taken at one meeting, the first application was well considered, but the last did not get the consideration it deserved..</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPOINT said he had not heard the least reason given why the standing orders should be rescinded except that Alderman Sutton said that it would be far better to regulate these salaries in private. Alderman SUTTON denied._ haying said this.</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPOINT said that s a matter of fact the meaning of Alderman Sutton's remarks was that this question should be discussed in private and not where the reporters and public were present. (Hear, hear.) Alderman SUTTON: Be is drawing on his imagination. Whatever was done by the Committee would have to come before that Council. Councillor PIERPOINT maintained that these matters should be discussed in public. The standing orders were adopted some years ago because, as Alderman Bolton had pointed out, there were applications for in- creases of salary from month to month from different officials, and the attention of the Council was taken from other matters relating to the welfare of the town. The collective wisdom of the Council had decided that the whole of the applications should be considered at one meeting, namely, in February, and there had been no reasons given why they should not, except that it might be disagreeable to some members of the Council to discuss them in the Council. He did not agree with the agreement of Councillor Shaw-Green that the whole of the applications would not receive a fair considera- tion. Alderman GREENALL supported the proposal of the Mayor. Councillor DAVIES was pleased to hear from the Mayor that if his motion was passed the resolutions on the agenda paper with reference to the proposed increase of salary would be withdrawn. At the same time the Council ought to be very careful before tbey decided to disturb a standing order. He objected to the composition of the Committee, as the General Purposes Committeee should not say, in effect, that they were the only people in Warrington who were fit to decide such a question. There were members outside the General Purpeses Com- mittee who were more fitted to deal with the question than those whose names appeared on the minutes of the General Purposes Committee. Councillor ROBERTS said he thought Councillor Davies had entirely misunderstood the object from the Mayor's moticn. It was that a special committee be appointed —not necessarily the committee which appeared on the agenda paper. Councillor DAVIES : Then lam perfectly satisfied. Councillor ROBERTS : The object is to consider the whole question, and then report to the Council. The MAYOR : You can add names to the committee by amendment. Councillor TINNioN supported the MaYor's proposition, as he thought these continual applications in the month of February caused irritation in the town. If a com- mittee were appointed it could look into the whole thing and say what the maximum amount of the salaries should be. They all knew that there had in the last few years been a decrease all round of incomes which were net fixed, and he pointed to the fact that investments which used to bring in four per cent. now only brought in 21. This fact should be kept in mind in considering the fixed salaries of officials, and the committee should provide for decreases as well as increases of salaries. (Hear, hear.) Councillor HEPHERD said it was intended to go into the question of salaries from that of the Town Clerk down- wards. Councillor ROBERTS observed that applications for in- creases of salary had beea made in February hy officials because that was the time to apply. They bad said, "We -night as well have a shot, whether we get an increase or not." (Laughter.) The result was that they had not only serious applications but others as well. The Mayor's motion was carried, only Alderman Bol- ton voting against it.</p>
        <p>UNDEBATED_ MINUTES.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the following committees—the more important portions of which were given in last week's " Examiner "—were passed without discussion, viz. : Watch Committee, Finance and Estate Committee, Street Improvement Committee, Sanitary Works Com- mittee, School Attendance Committee, Markets Com- mittee, and Gas Committee.</p>
        <p>THE HOSPITAL QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Alderman SurroN,in moving the minutes of the Health Committee, referred to the resolution that a report be prepared by the Borough Surveyor and the Medical Officer of Health, showing the extensions and alterations required at the hospital, with details and estimates of the probable cost. He said that the accommodation at the hospital was utterly insufficient to meet the con- tinual increase of patients. The committee had visited the hospital,and after inspection had unanimously come to the conclusion that there was a necessity for further extension and accommodation. They had, therefore, instructed the borough surveyor and medical officer to go into the question,after which the committee intended to deal with it and if possible to provide the necessary accommodation. Councillor ROBERTS seconded the passing of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB inquired as to the number of patients in the hospital, and Councillor DAVIES remarked that if it was intended to sell the present hospital to the Board of Guardians it was not advisable to spend more money upon it. Alderman SUTTON, in reply, said he could not tell from memory the number of patients in the hospital, but he knew that, generally speaking, the place was full. As to the disposal of the hospital to the Board of Guardians they had not received any reply from that body.</p>
        <p>THE DISUSED RIVER AT WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Commit- tee, Councillor LEE asked if anything was likely to be soon done to prevent a recurrence of the nuisance at the old bed of the river at Wilderspool. • Alderman WRIGHT. in reply, said the Corporation were prepared to go into the work forthwith, but the matter chiefly rested with the Runcorn District Council who would have to obtain the approval of the Local Govern- ment Board.</p>
        <p>MUSEUM, &amp;C., COMMITTEE. -THE FREE ADMISSIONS TO THE</p>
        <p>ART SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee, it was resolved that free admission to the School of Art for the present stssion be offered to all pupils of local elementary schools who obtained cards of merit at the recent examination in drawing, on strict compliance with the rules of the school as to attendance and examination.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS in proposing the passing of the minutes said the above matter had been brought before the notice ot the committee by one of its new members, Councillor Furness, who, he thought, was entitled to the full credit that was merited from the fact of their hav- ing made from 30 to 40 free scholarships. (Hear, hear.) If the results were satisfactory the principle would be carried further, the idea being to bring the elementary schools more into contact with the School of Art and to develope to the fullest extent the talents of the scholars. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL, PARR, AND BATHS COMMITTEE.-THE PROPOSED ALTERATION AT BANS NOOSE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of tbe above committee, it was resolved that the plan of proposed offil/4-:es and alterations at Bank House, now submitted, be approved; and that the Borough Surveyor be rpquested to obtain tenders fol. the work,and that the following charges be made for season tickets to the baths for the ensuing year :—Ordinary season tickets, 12s. 6d. per annum ; members of W.S.C., les. per araum ; members of W.S.C. and others under 16 years of age, 7s. 6d. per annum. Alderman Bouros,in proposing till confirmation of the minutes, produced a plan showing the proposed altera- tions to be made to Bank House. He went on to refer to the two bay windows.and remarked that it was in- tended to obtain tenders for the removal of these, and if they were very low in price to lay them before the Coun- cil. The committee would simply bring before the Council the scheme for the work and its cost together with a plan showing how the house would look when the windows were removed. It would then be for the Council to say whether the windows should or should not be taken down. Councillor Dr. CANNELL seconded. Councillor BEsNzTT expressed the hope that no money would be spent over the removal of the bay windows, as the whole place .was to be pulled down for street widening. Alderman FeaNcoms did not think the bay windows should be interPred with. • Councillor TINNION asked how it was that there was a falling off in the receipts at the bath as compared with last year. Alderman GREENALL thought this was due to the fact that more season tickets had been issued, and Councillor Damns was of a similar opinion, and added that the re- ceipts at the bath fluctuated in the most wonderful</p>
        <p>manner. Councillor ROBERTS thought that it should be under- stood that the alterations at Bank Rouse did not include the cutting down of the two" bays." Alderman BOLTON having replied the minutes were confirmed. RIVERS COMMITTEE.—TTIE SETTLEMENT WITH THE</p>
        <p>thought perfectly reasonable. As to the report of Mr.R. T. Faircicugh, that gentleman had given the committee a very full and accurate account of the action of the Upper Mersey Navigation Commission in buoying the main channel. The committee were very glad of the information he had given them, and were very much obliged to him. As far as his suggestion for representa- tion on the Mersey Docks and Harbour Board was con- cerned, however, the committee thought their power would not enable them to do as Mr. Fairclough sug- gested. Mr. William Diggle at this point interrupted Councillor Roberts. The latter objected to this,and as he was again interrupted by thesame person he resumed his seat. The Town Clerk then went to Mr. Diggle to tell him to re- frain from his interruptions, and Mr, Diggle thereupon left the room. .</p>
        <p>Alderman HurcniNsoN:seconded the minutes, which were carried.</p>
        <p>FIRE ENGINE COMMITTEE—STRONG STATEMENT BY couNcILLoR SKELTON.</p>
        <p>Alderman HuTaingsoN in moving the adoption of the minutes. which we reported last week said that he thought the result:of the inquiry by Mr. F. Bailey would be a source of satisfaction to the Council, so far as the supposed incapability of the fire brigade was concerned.</p>
        <p>Councilior SMETHURST secunded, and was about to refer at length to the result of the inquiry by Mr. Bailey, when the Mayor interposed, and informed him that he would have an opportunity to speak on the same subject when the General Purposes Committee's minutes were proposed. Councillor Smethurst accordingly postponed his remarks. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor SKELTON drew the attention of the Council to the annual report by the Superintendent of the Fire Brigade, in which were the words—" The total estimated damage in the Borough was £9BO exclusive of the dam- age at Messrs. Skelton's, information concerning which ' was refused." Information, continued Councillor Skelton, was not refused, and he declared the words quoted to be "downright and deliberate falsehood." The information was not asked for, and he (Councillor Skelton) would like to know how such :a statement ap- peared in the report, (Hear, hear.) The first applica- tion for an estimate of the damage had been made by the Chief Constable about eight or ten days ago, and to him he (the speaker) had detailed everything required. Why that statement should appear in the Superintend- ant's report he did not know, but he should certainly want to know very soon. (Hear, hear.) Alderman HUTCHINSON said he could not understand what the reason was for putting such a statement in the report. If it would suit Councillor Skelton he would withdraw the words complained of from the report, and in the meantime make an inquiry into the matter. Councillor SKELTON said he would be satisfied with that. The minutes were then put and carried.</p>
        <p>WATER COMMITTEE. The minutes of the above committee were of a routine character.</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB proposed, and Councillor Hep- hcrd seconded, the passing of the Water Committee's minutes,which was agreed to. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>FIRE BRIGADE REPORT, TRANSFER OF GYMNASII72.I. ETC.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved, and the DEPUTY-MAYOR seconded, the confirmation of the minutes of the General Purposes Committee which appeared in our last issue. Councillor BENNETT congratulated the Committee upon the transference of the Gymnasium to the Corpor- tion, and said it was a step in the right direction. With regard to the fire brigade he was inclined to think that from beginning to end the report of Mr. Bailey was one long justification of the complaints which had been made from time to time with reference to the brigade. His report bore out everything which the members of the Town Council had said,and there was " gross incapability." The idea of having an electric call for the firemen was a precau- tion which would be very useful in the event of an out- break of fire in the town. (Hear, hear.) Councillor DAVIES said it was evident that the General Purposes Committee did not intend outsiders to become members of the committee it was proposed to appoint to consider the question of salaries. The resolution of the committee read as follows :—" That the following mem- bers be appointed a special committee to prepare new standing orders as to salaries of officials, and to submit a scheme for regulating all future increases in the salaries of officials, with a maximum to be paid in each case :—The Mayor, Aldermen Francomb, Greenall and Wright, Councillors Shaw Green, Hepherd, Monks, and Roberts." He hoped that if he pressed for additions to be made to the committee it would not be construed in- to any mark of personal disrespect to those at present suggested. There were good business men in the Coun- cil more capable than those whose names appeared,some of whom were antiquated fossils,in the minutes. (Laugh- ter.) He moved that the names of Aldermen Francomb and Greenall, and Councillors Hepherd and Roberts be removed from the list, and the names of Councillors Furness, Skelton. Woods and Bennett be substituted. Alderman FRANCOMB I don't feel inclined to sit here and to be judged by Councillor Davies about my ability. Councillor DAVIES : I said" without disrespect." Alderman FRANCOMB : Talk about antiquated fossils! Why Mr. Roberts is as sharp a business man as any in Warrington. Councillor DAVIES: Quite so. Alderman FRANCOMB (indignantly) : I withdraw from the committee. Councillor DAVIES: lam glad to hear Alderman Francomb withdraw from the committee so gracefully. Alderman FRANCOMB Don't be personal. These per- sonalities are uncalled for. We get nothing but per- sonalities from you. Councillor DAVIES What I said I meant, and I mean what I say, and I say what I mean. (Laughter.) Alderman FRANCOMB : Why not have added some names ? You have no right to select people to be thrown off the committee. It is impudence. Councillor DAVIES: I did not know that I could add any. names.</p>
        <p>The Mayoß Why did you not make additions to the existing committee without seeking to strike off names. • __ _</p>
        <p>_ Councillor DAVIES : I withdraw any rerna,rks that may he considered personal. Alderman WRIGHT: There is no reason why names should not be added to the committee. _ _</p>
        <p>_ Councillor BENwETT said he declined to act, owing to his numerous business engagements.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: He is like me. He goes out in November. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>Councillor BEIVIsTETT • Councl replied that that was not his reason for not desiring to be elected. He was not afraid of his constituents. (Laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS: I don't think I am afraid of my constituents. If any man has got a fair claim to a tolerably safe seat I think I may claim that it is myself. (Hear, hear.) However.that is by the way. If there is a painful thing in a man's life, so I am told, it is when he first realises for the first time that he is getting old. I have not realised it yet. To-night is the first time I have ever been considered an antiquated fossil. (Loud laughter.) In 25 years from now I may be open to such a suggestion, but to be told that my powers have de- creased of late—well, it is a reminder,l suppose, that we are all human and getting on in years. (Renewed laughter.) However, I think I must respectfully de- cline to serve on the committee. _ _</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: For my awn part I won't allow you to retire. (Hear, hear. and laughter.)</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON said he Considered himself as wise and as clever as any other member of the Council, but he did not feel jealous because he had net been selected. (Laughter.) Whoever was proposed was liable to be im- proved upon. It was a farce and an absurdity alto- gether to worry about who were on the committee and who were not. If the Council wished adchtions to be made to the committee let them be made. They might depend upon it that whatever suggestions were brought before them would be fully considered before they would be allowed to pass.</p>
        <p>The Council then decided to elect the committee with the names suggested by Councillor Davies!added. Councillor SMETHURST then proceeded to speak at lengthen the report of Mr. Bailey on the condition of the fire brigade, and added that be quite agreed that the Fire Engine Committee found " nothing in the report wkich would justify the criticism of certain members of the Council or the charge of gross incapability of the brigade." Alluding to the fire at Messrs. Skelton's, he contended that considering the position of the premises, the fire brigade did well during the conflagratien. The top storey was scarcely damaged,and Councillor Skelton had informed him that no less than £2,000 worth of stock was saved. Councillor SKELTON I beg to deny that I ever gave him such authority. I never made any such assertion. (Lauguter.) Councillor SMETHURST : Councillor Skelton told me so himself. Councillor SKELTON : I cannot be responsible for the imagination of the councillor. (Renewed laughter.) I am sure I said nothing of the kind. Councillor SHAW GREEN said that ever since the cor- poration acquired the Water Works they had done their best to provide good hydrants. A great deal of work had been done in that special direction. Councillor Tnmon hoped that the Gymnasium would be made even more useful than it was at the present moment when it was transfered to the Corporation. He hoped an efficient reading-room would be provided. When would the building be taken over ? The TowN CLERK: As from to-night, although the actual management will not be taken over until the order from the Charity Commissioners has been re- ceived. Councillor MONKS asked where the funds would come I from ?</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS replied that the building would be carried on pretty much as it had been carried on by the present committee. It would not be possible to make any serious change. They would immediately have re- ceipts from the fees and hire of the building. Alderman HARRISON thought it was rather humiliating to have to go to Stafford for a gentleman to discover the defects in the fire arrangements which had been pointed out. These defects should have been discovered by the committee. The report said :—" The present hydrants are much neglected, being kept anything but clean; some of them are entirely covered with corrosion and dirt, rendering them unfit for prompt use." A more dis- graceful allegation could hardly have been made. Then there was an insufficient supply of water, and that was a statc, of things which ought not to have been allowed to exist. At Latchford, they could not throw water over a cottage in case of fire, and if a fire took place it must burn itself out. The MAYOR : It is a legacy we have had left to us. Alderman HARRISON: We should have opened our eyes before we bought the Water Works. Councillor MONKS objected to the Gymnasium being taken over unless suitable provision was made for carry- it on. There was an Act of Parliament which might be adopted, namely, "the Museum and Gymnasium Act." The Tows CLERK: It could not be done, because the rules laid down by the Commissioners clash with the conditions laid down by the Act. Alderman Hurcsissos also alluded to the fire brigade reports, and said it was intended to use the electric calls for the firemen, while with regard to the hydrants, in a short time they would have them in every part of the town as required. When all the recommendations had been carried out the fire brigade would be in a much , more efficient condition than it was in at present. The minutes were then passed, and the Council rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 8, _ 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 8, _ 1896.</p>
