      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WHAT BATES WANTED TO KNOW.</head>
        <p>WHAT BATES WANTED TO KNOW.</p>
        <p>I shall be obliged if you can answer me one question," said my friend Bates, as he lay on the couch one day in my room nursing his aching " Why does exposure to wet sr cold bring on an attack of rbeumatism at one time, when a like exposure br a score of times leads to no such result I"' Before I set down in writing the answer I gave him I wish you would read the following letters, as no doubt the authors of them will be interested in the same point. "In November,lB92," says the one, I had an attack of rheumatic fever, and was confined to my bed for four weeks, during which time I suffered fearfully. I had awful pains all over me ; my joints swelled up,and I was so helpless I could not raise my hand to my mouth. After the fever left me I was extremely week, and so emaciated I was little more than skin and bone. A large lump, the size of an egg, formed on my elbow, and my fingers were almost drawn but of joint. I cannot describe the suffering I bad to bear. The doctor ordered me various medicines, and cod liver oil,but they had no effect. In February, 1893, I read in a small book about the remarkable success whi.zh had followed the use of Mother Seigel's Syrup in cases of rheumatism ,and.got a bottle from Messrs. Leverett and Fry, High-st. After taking it two weeks I was better, and in about a month more all rheumatic pains had left me, and I was strong and well as ever. You may pub- lish what I have said. (signed) John H. Kent, 9, Ran- dall-st., Maidstone, Kent, January 30th, 1895." "For many years," says the other,"' I had been sub- ject to liver complaint and indigestion. I was habitu- ally heavy, weak,and weary. My appetite was poor, and all food gave me pain and fulness at the chest and around the sides. I had so much pain and tightness of the chest that I could not endure the pressure of my clothing upon it. Although not laid up, I was seldom free from pain or a sense of discomfort. In the summer of 18931 began to. suffer with rheumatism,which affected my arras and shoulders until I had not the power to lift my hand to my head. I tried all sorts of linaments, embrocations, and rubbing oils, but got no benefit from any of them." "In August, 1893, my friend Mrs. Owen told me how much good Mother Seigel's Syrup had done her for rheu- matism,and I got a bottle from the Drug Stores at St. Ann's Road. In a few days I was much better, and in less than a month afterwards all pain had left me; and 1 am happy to say I have never had any return of the rheumatism since,but have enjoyed the best of health in every respect. In common with thankfulness for my speedy and wonderful deliverance,l willingly consent to the publication of this hurried statement should you wish to make that use of it. (Signed) (Mrs.) L.S. Cole, 6 Albert-road, South Tottenham, London, August 16th, 1898."</p>
        <p>Before answering the question of my friend Bates(who was a chronic rheumatic) I asked him one : " Why does a lighted match, dropped into the road, die out harm.. lessly, but when dropped.into a hayrick, set up a con- flagration P" •</p>
        <p>" Any fool can answer that," he said. "Because in the one case there is nothing for the fire to catch hold of, while in the other there is." " Exactly," I responded. " Now see. Indigestion and liver ~..omplaint (the second consequent on the first) continue to produce a virulent poison in the blood called uric acid, practically insoluble in water. This acid, which is a solid, en ters the tissues, and sets going a hot inflammatory fire. That is rheumatism. It does what a sliver would—only the acid is a poison sliver. " When the indigestion and the liver trouble are not very bad, and the kidneys and sweat glands of the skin are acting fairly well, this acid is carried out of the body about as fast as it is formed. Exposure then brings on no rheumatism. But, per contra, when the stomach and liver are in bad condition, the acids form faster than the kidneys and skin can carry it off. Then expose yourself, get cold or wet, hamper the skin and kidneys still more, and the poison acid spreads through your muscles and joints like the fire in the dry hay. You understand ? Very well. The longer the case persists the more frequent the rheumatic attacks. That is why chronic dyspeptics are apt also to be chronic rheumatics. Fend off dyspepsia, or cure it by the use of Mother Seigel's Syrup, and you and the rheumatism will have no dealings. Neglect it,and suffsr every time you catch cold."</p>
        <p>That was my answer to Bates, and he said there seemed to be sense in it.</p>
        <p>PROSECUTION OF A WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCILLOR.</p>
        <p>PLUMBERS AND CORPORATION WATER PIPES On Wednesday at the Warrington County 'Petty Ses- sions, bofore Messrs. John Crosfield (chairman), and S. R. Armitage' Councillor William James Shaw was charged with having on the 10th January,lB96, in Little Sankey, being the owner of premises supplied by the Corporation with water, unlawfully made an alteration a certain communication pipe without their consent. Mr. A. Browne represented the defendant, who was in court, and on his behalf pleaded not guilty. Mr. J. L. Whittle, town clerk, appeared to prosecute for the Corporation,and said Mr. Shaw was an authorised plumber, carrying on business in Dallam-lane. The offence committed wus under section 19 of the water rates clause act, and the liability for such an offence was £5. The property mentioned was situate in Little Sankey, and owned by Mr. Shaw, and he without get- ting the consent of the Corporation, broke through the highway and connected with the main a new pipe which he conveyed behind the property and connected with the houses. By doing so be cut off the supply of the cottages on the other side of the road, and put their occupiers to great inconvenience. The first intimation they (the Corporation) had of the work was on the 11th January,when Mr. Shaw sent them a notice stating that he had put down water fittings. On the following Moc- day they sent up to make an examination and com- municated with Mr. Shaw himself. Mr. Dees, water engineer, informed him that what he had done was un- lawful and threatened to cut off the water supply. Mr. Shaw sent in return a message which was far from , complimentary. If Mr. Shaw had come forward and expressed his regret at the offence, nothing further would have been done, but he seemed to defy the Corpor- ation, and denied the offence. Mr. Browne: There is no offence. If I had thought for a moment that Mr. Shaw had committed a technical offence I should not proceed with the case. Mr. Armitage : By laying the pipe, does it divert the whole main. Mr. Whittle: It is a branch main supplying the two lots of cottages known as Richardson's Row. Mr. Shaw broke down to the stop-cock and turned off the water. He made a hole in the pipe and connected his property. While doing so he shut off the supply of the other cot- tages. Continuing his statement, the Town Clerk said if Mr. Shaw had a right to tap the pipes, any private owner might do it in Bridge-st., and cut off the water there. It did not matter whether the pipes were his own or not he could not do anything to them without tbe consent of the Corporation, which he had never ap- plied for. The question was here raised as:to whether Mr.Crosfield had a right to interfere with the pipes on his property, say at the stop-cock near the lodge. Contrary to Mr. Crosfield's supposition the Town Clerk informed him t:iat by Act of Parliament he had no riaht to touch the pipes though on his own property. Mr. Deas, water engineer to the Corporation,explained the position of the main which ran down Liverpool-road. In cutting the pipe Mr. Shaw deprived the people living in the houses of water. Mr. Browne : I understand for 15 minutes. Mr. Dees : It could not be done in the time. He had opened the ground and found the new-made joint and had told Mr. Shaw about it. It was the main lead ser- vice pipe which was cut. Mr. Browne: Has it not been the custom during the time ot the last engineer to allow the work to be done first and the notice sent after ?—Not as far as I know. Mr. Browne: You wish to stop that practice ?—Yes, certainly. Mr. Browne: This is the first time proceedings have been taken ?—Yes. Mr. Browne: Whose land is the stop-cock on P—l don't know. Mr. Browne: It is Mr. Shaw's private land, and only the London and North-Western Railway people have a right to come over it. I think you knew that Mr. Shaw believed that all the fittings belonged to him?—l didn't. Mr. Browne : Were not the pipes supplying the cot- tages in the front part of the houses P—Some were and some were not. Mr. Browne: There were no gullies or slepstones there. Did not Mr. Shaw do it to improve the sanitary condition of the house by taking the pipes to the back and providing slopstones P—l don't know. Mr.Crostield : Does he claim that the pipe is his from the stop-cock ? Mr. Browne: Yes. Mr. Whittle : I say it is not. These pipes were all put in be the Corporation and 2s. per annum paid as rent by the previous owner of the property. Mr. Browne to Mr. Deas: Have you seen the slopstones which have been put up.—Yes, but they have been put up only recently. Mr. Whittle: Mr. Dias is not a sanitary inspector. Mr. Browne: Is not all that Mr. Shaw has done been to remove the pipe from the front of the houses to the back, on his side of the stop-cock. Mr. Whittle: I believe Mr. Shaw has really improved the cottages, but that is not the point. Mr. Crosfield : Did Mr. Ross permit plumbers to do this work without giving notice? Mr. Whittle: I don't know sir. Addressing Mr. Dees:: Have you had occasion to speak to Mr. Shaw about the manner in which you intended to carry out the regu- lations. Mr. Deas: I have informed all plumbers, and Mr. Shaw personally, that I intended to work this department according to the true letter of the law. Mr. Browne: The pipes are our property. Mr. Whittle: The previous owner paid 23. a year rent for them. Mr. Woodcock, collector, said that Mr. Shaw called upon him on the 18th December. They had sent him a demand note for the water rate of the property,allowing a slight discount if the bill was paid by the 21st Decem- ber. When Mr. Shaw called he asked if he might post- pone the payment of it as he was making some alteration in the property and some of the cottages were empty. Witness told him he might, ana also that the pipes be- longed to the Corporation and that they were paid 2s. per annum for them. Witness advised him to see the water engineer before making any alteration. Mr. Shaw niade a note in his pocket book to the effect that he would see Mr. Deas. Mr. Browns: Did not he tell you that he was making alterations. Mr. Woodcock: He did not; he said he was glad we had told him about the pipes. He did not say he would not pay it, but that he would see the water engineer. He did not pay it. Mr. Browne in his statement for the defence said that the Act was passed for the purpose of preventing a mis- usage or wasting of the water and was intended to pre- vented people interfering with the pipes of the Corpora- tion so as to cause a waste of water. With regard to the matter in question they said that the stop-cock was on their property. They had the title deeds showing that it was on Mr. Shaw's land. Until Mr. Shaw heard from , Mr. Woodcock he never dreamt but what the pipes belonged to him, and he said now he knew nothing to the contrary. They contended that he bought them and there was nothing proving that they belonged to the Corporation except that some man had paid 2s. per year for them. He said, therefore, that the pipes were theirs and that they had not interfered with any pipe of the Corporation and were not liable under that section. Mr. Crosfield : You claim these pipes as yours only up to the stop-cock. Mr. Browne: Yes. Mr. Shaw has done the same thing over and over again and other plumbers too. A question of right having been raised he submitted that the juris- diction of the bench had been ousted. They turned off the water for 15 minutes believing that the pipes were their own property. Mr. Crosfield : I don't think our jurisdiction is ousted from what Mr. Whittle has said. Mr. Browne :* I was under the impression that what was on the house Bide of the stop-cock beldnged to the owner of the property and could be altered by him. Mr. Whittle: It is not so. No pipes can be altered without the permission of the Corporation. Mr. Browne (who had been re-examining the plans) : I find on examining the plans that the stop-cock is not even on our land. It is only fair to say so. Mr. Cresfield : We both consider that we have juris- diction in the matter. Mr. Browne: In that case my defence breaks down. If a plumber has not right to interfere with any portion of the pipes it has always been the custom of the plum- bers.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield : I can understand the waterworks' people being particular about interference with th a pipes. You do not argue that custom makes law ? Mr. Browne, Oh, dear no! It is quite evident now that my client has committed a technical offence in gnorance. Mr. Whittle: I will not press the case against Mr. Shaw. We only want it to be known that this sort of thing will not be allowed. Mr. Crosfteld : We consider that the Town Clerk has proved his case.and we think that Mr. Shaw has touched the pipes in ignor Arica of doing wrong, and we under- stand from the Town Clerk that they only wanted this case proved to stop other plumbers in the ...own from meddling with the pipes of the Corporation without per- mission. We shall therefore inflict a nominal penalty of is. and costs.</p>
        <p>William James Morgan was hanged in Wandswbrth Gaol on Tuesday for the murder of his wife. The crime for which he suffered was committed with the coolest deliberation, alai the murderer interrupted the proceed- ings at his trial by tendering a plea of" guilty" and de- claring that he " would rather die than live." An amusing story is going the rounds in connec- tion with Lord Lonsdale and his hero, the German Emperor. It is mentioned that a few days ago the noble earl had occasion to find fault with some huntsmen who persisted in overriding the hounds. His manner of expostulating was resented, where- upon Lord Lonsdale exercised his authority as Maseer of the Hounds, and ordered the dogs to ken- nel. The addendum is that a day or two afterwards the following telegram was received by the Earl:— " Quite approve of your action.—William the Second."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>STOCKTON HEA.TH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEA.TH.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFF AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HoPE.—The. fortnightly meeting of the above Society was held on Monday night in the Ebenezer Chapel, which was crowded, and an interesting programme was well maintained. The president occupied the chair. The following took part in the programme :—Reciters, Misses Sarah Whitfield, Jessie Ellison, Lizzie Proud, Ada Smith, Annie Houghton, Jane Houghton, Sarah Robin- son, Helena Grounds ; Masters Walter and William Gerrard, Bradford and L. A. Kenworthy, Fred and Charley Brereton, Fred Jackson and Arthur Dunn. A solo was given by Mr. Whitlow, and a quartette by Misses H, aud Eva Grounds and Messrs. Grounds. The Hill Cliffe boys also gave a concerted recitatioe,and sang a Hindoo hymn called " Wakka," bringing a very pleas- ant evening to a close. THE PARISH COUNCIL—A meeting of this Council was held on Monday night in the National Schools, Stockton Heath, Mr. C. W. Davenport presiding. There were also present—Messrs. Richardson, Pearson, Eyes, Britch, Hedgcock, Coppock and Ellison (clerk). The allezed enclosure of a plot of land near London Bridge by Mr. Warburton again came up for discussion, and the Clerk read a letter from the agent of Mr. Warburton—in reply to the Council's letter requesting him to restore the land —which stated that the father of Mr. Warburton had enclosed the land about 221- years. This was then done in response to a request by many parishioners, who stated that it was being used as a repositore for filth and I refuse, and 119 (Mr. Warburton) did not see why he should restore it. If it was a question of obtaining a footpath along that side of the road, then it might be open tor consideration. The letter was lengthily dis- cussed by the Council, who finally decided to again request Mr. Warburton to restore the land.—Mr. Stais'ev Wilson, assistant town clerk of Warrington, ()resented a petition (which it was intended to present to Parliament) on behalf of the Appleton Parochial Committee,in favour of the borough extension, with a request that the Parish Council would show their favour to the scheme by sign- ing it. The Chairman and two members of the Council attached their signatures to the document.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>TIIE LICENSE OF THE SPREAD EAGLE HOTEL.—At the Ahrincham Petty Sessions on Monday, before Mr. R. 11. Joynson and other magistrates, the license of the Spread Eagle Hotel, Lymm„ was transferred from Sarah E. Hughes to Sidney George Collins, of Irlam. ACCIDENT.—On Saturday night, about seven o'clock, as a gentleman (whose name did not transpire) was driving a horse and trap down Agden Brow, the animal slipped and fell. The driver was thrown trom his seat into the roadway, but luckily escaped serious injory. One of the traces and also one of the shafts were broken, and the horse ran away. When secured near the Jolly Thrasher, the trap was much damaged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>IS LIFE WORTH LIVING?</head>
        <p>IS LIFE WORTH LIVING?</p>
        <p>It entirely depends on what you call life. Some lives are not worth hying, but the fault rests with the vic- tim. The drunkard's life is not worth living; the blas- phemer's life is not worth living ; the miser's life is not worth living ; the thief's life is not worth living ; but it is their own fault. "Let us eat and drink,fer to-morrow we die." Is such a life worth living? Such is the philosophy of the brutes. There are two essentials that make life worth living ; regard for the Creator's moral and physical law's. That person whose body breeds disease, weakness and ailments cannot enjoy life,cannot attain that success in life that is so necessary to happi- ness. Such a one becomes a burden to himself and friends. A little more study into these matters; a little less bigotry ; a little more care and a little more observ- ance, would brighten and enlighten many lives that are now one dreary monotonous desert waste. Neglect, procrastination, fill more graves than dreaded epidemics. The morning headache, the feeling of unusual lifelessness and lack of energy, irritability, depression and nervousness ; the occa- sional shooting pains under the shoulders or in the small of the back, are warnings of liver weakness or kidney disease, easily prevented and cured if taken in time—not so easily if allowed to fully develop. But there is a way to ;weld these dangers. By building up ' the systern,restoring healthy action to the liver and kid- neys, freeing the blood from impurities, and thus re- gaining new strength. To do this a suitable remedy must be taken,and there ia nothing for the purpose that can be compared with Warner's Safe Cure, because its effects are immediate and lasting. How many important steps we take—just too late. The letter that was never written ; the apology that was withheld until death— the great reconciler—came, gave no comfort; the kind acts towards others are too often left to be applied to the purchase of an elegant tombstone. The cruelty of heed- lessness and procrastination is often greater than ties cruelty of crime ; and to make life worth living be guided by the advice of those around you who are happy. Mr. J. J. Mines, 122, Rye Lane, Peckham Rye, in telling you that he can safely recommend Warner's Safe Cure to all—men, women, and children—speaks from personal experience, having been rescued from kidney and liver complaint, when all the previous treat- ments prescribed by doctors had given no relief. After taking the second bottle of Warner's Safe Cure he found great relief, and a little perseverance fully restored him to health. This was nine months ago, and he has, up to the present, never felt any return of the disease. He furthermore states that a few weeks ago his wife was suffering from general debility and weakness, and after taking a few bottles of Warner's Safe Cure she enjoyed her former good health.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CF[URCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance of the members of this society at the Wycliffe Church last Sunday afternoon,the Chairman being Mr. H. M. Ashton, of Sankey-st., War- rington, and the speaker Mr. Johnson, of Padgate. The Chairman during his short address expressed a desire to see a band in connection with the Wycliffe P.S.A., and the suggestion appeared to meet with the hearty appro- val of the members present. Dlr. Johnson gave an ad- dress upon the character of Samson of the Old Testa- ment. In the course of his remarks he gave the reasons why Samson did not do the amount of good in this world that his strength and opportunities warranted. Be attributed Sampson's downfall to his unhappy marriage and its rest Its ; and he warned the young men present to be careful in their choice of a wife ; for said he " a man is what a woman makes him," and therefore,if they were Christian men they should take care they married a Christian woman and not a Pnilistine. The President, Mr. Yonge, made a few remarks upon the progress the society was making and on the good work that it had done in the past. He gave the total membership at 1,153 members, and stated that they were within one month of the completion of their third year. He also drew at- tention to the Temperance Society which they were forming, and urgently appealed to all teetotalers to give them aid and support in trying td spread the temperance movement in Warrington. A solo was ably rendered by Mr. Whitehouse, and the 60 minutes were, as usual, heartily enjoyed.</p>
        <p>Electric motors for winding, hauling and pumping in coal mines are estimated by a South Wales engineer to sava three-fourths of the fuel used for power, or a value equal to nearly four per cent, of the coal produced in the mines.</p>
        <p>A contrivance for quickly stopping machinery—as in case some person is being drawn between cogs or rollers —has been divised by a French electrician. On touching one of a series of push-buttons, placed at convenient points, the power is shut off and a powerful brahe ap- plied to fly-wheels. A twenty-horse power engine,work- ing at ninety revolutions, was stopped in two-thirds of a second.</p>
      </div>
