        <p>Th ACCORDING to an apparently well- Ir ish Pe arty. informed writer in the "Daily Chronicl9" there is at the present time a great chance of unity amongst Irish mem- bers of Parliament. He declares that there has been a growing disgust in Ireland against the divi- sions in the party and that strong pressure will be brought to bear upon the different sections. Pro- posals for re-union have been put forward from several unexpected quarters, and it is said that even Mr. &amp;nix REDMOND recognises that combina- tion of some kind or other will be necessary in face of the heavy adverse majority in Parliament. Mr. JUSTIN MCCARTEIY having resigned his posi- tion of leader of the Irish National Party a good deal of interest is now centred upon the election ot a successor, and it is needless to say that the pros- pects of unity largely depend upon the selection that will be made. The men most prominently mentioned in connection with the leadership are Mr. DILLoN, Mr. BLAKE, and Mr. SEXTON. In the opinion of the writer referred to, Mr. SEXTON would be decidedly the best choice. :" Everyone knows," I we.are told, "what would happen if Mr. DILLoN was proposed -as chairman of the Irish policy. Mr. HEALY would immediately be put forward against him, and the old tussle would recar in its most acute form." Mr. BLAKE, it is said, is equally impossible. "He has never won his spurs as an Irish national poli- tician of the first class. He is still a Canadian Irishman, with a long distinguished career behind him, but not racy of the soil,' or likely to fascin- nate the Irish people." By this process of elimina- tion Mr. SEXTON is left, and it is said that" the opinion is growing in the ranks of the Parliamen- tarp party that all hopes of unity centre in him." It is certain that Mr. SEXTON has won a high reputation as a Parliamentarian,and if,as the writer in the " Chronicle " assumes,his election as leader will tend towards unity nothing better could hap- pen for Ireland. By the way, to those people who religiously believe that all Irish members of Par- liament are "selfish agitators," it will be some- thing like a ','revelation to lestrn that Mr. JUSTIN MCCARTHY has suffered both in health and for- tunes by his tenure of the office of Parliamentary leader.</p>
