        <p>The application made to the Warrington Board of Guardians by the Free Church Council for permis- sion to bold Nonconformist services at the Work- house was a very reasonable one, and the Board undoubtedly did a fair thing in granting it. At the same time Mr. Knowles was perfectly consistent in opposing the application. He is a believer in "old customs." It may, perhaps, be suggested that such a staunch:and unfaltering adherent of " old cus- toms" is slightly out of place on a representative body elected at the end of this nineteenth century on the one-man-one-vote principle. As with a fly in amber, one wonders how ',.;)t there. But still he is there, and is entitled to -.;pre. s his opinion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cecil Rhodes, the late Cape Premier and the head of the Chartered Company, arrived in London on Tuesday, and great was the anxiety of pressmen and others to hear what the South African "Colossus "had to say anent the Jameson raid But Mr. Rhodes was impervious to the interviewer and not a scrap ot " copy " could be got from him. Some of the passengers by the same steamer were more communicative. One gallant member of Dr. Jameson's force was very positively of opinion that Jameson had been inveigled over the frontier by forged messages from President Kruger, stating that the women and children of Johannesberg were in serious danger. This is a theory of the invasion which will hardly stand the brunt of criticism. President Kruger may not be quite so saintly and so free from guile as he is sometimes represented to be, but it is not likely that he would invite the in- vasion of his own country by a force of disciplined troops armed with all the latest weapons of destruc- tion, including Maxims. The true explanation of the invasion of the Transvaal will not be quite so sweetly simple as this.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN GAS COMPANY.</p>
        <p>The ordinary general meeting of the shareholders ol the Runcorn Gas Company was held at the Gas Office, Runcorn,on Wednesday,for the purpose of receiving the report of the directors and the accounts of the company. Mr. F. Rigby occupied the chair.and amongst those pre- sent were :—Major Hazlehurst, Meagre. James Handley, Edwin Imison, William Whittaker, William Handley, James Pritchard, J. R. Frith (secretary and manager), J. M. Frith, and J. S. Frith. The directors in their report express regret that the continued depression of trade in the town had had a prejudicial effect upon the consump- tion of gas. The revenue account shewed a profit of £3,715 10s. sd. for the year, and it was pointed out that it would be necessary in order to pay the interest on de- bentures and to declare a dividend of 10 per cent. which the directors recommended, to appropriate the credit balance of £528 3s. sd. at profit and loss account, and to withdraw £194 17s. .Id. from the reserve fund. To provide for the rapidly-increasing use of ** Penny i— the Slot " meters, to facilitate the disposal of residuals, and to maintain the increased stock of coal it has been found expedient to re- duce the amount invested in consols by £1,250, leaving £2,283 Bs. 2d. in that security.—The Chairman moved the adoption of the report and mentioned the satisfac- tory way the "penny in the slot" meters were being taken up,stating that already 300 had been issued.—Mr. James Handley (vice-president), seconded the adoption of the report, which was unanimously approved of. A vote of thanks having been passed to the Chairman and directors on the proposition of Mr. Pritchard, seconded by Mr. Imison, the meeting terminated. FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. RAVENSCROFT, RUNCORN. The remains of Mr. Samuel Ravenscroft, of 8, Water- loo-road, whose death we reported last week, were interred in Runcorn Cemetery, on Sunday afternoon. The procession was headed by the Revs. T. Brackenbury, and W. George; and among those present were Dr. Robinson, Messrs. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., T. Simister, R. Norman, F. J. Norman, J.P., A. R. Norman, R. H. Posnett, D. Bisbrown, Greenway Millington, R. Rawlin- son, J. Dykes. R. Morris, B. liniveton, C. Littler, B. Crosby, R. Abel. Arnold Unsworth, Jabez Unsworth, James Gregory, P. Rigby, I. Bowyer, I. Hayes, Arthur Riley, James Littler, William Rowland, James Mort, T. Boote, A. Anderson, W. Graham, Robert Yates, C. Findlow, L Scragg, J. S. Davies, D. H. Jones, J. H. Once, Joseph Leadstone, J. Pennington, Hampson, William Jeffries, J. H. Darlington, James Higgins, William Rushton; Captains Le Couteur, E. Davies, H. Hill, and James Abrams. The bearers were four of the workmen of the deceased, namely, Messrs. Edward Pritchard, sen., and Pritchard, jnn. Thomas Malinger, and F. Groome. Following the coffin were the mourners and friends : Messrs. Blair (son-in-law), Albert Ravens- croft, Arthur Blair, Herbert Blair (grandsons), George Tracy, (St. Helens), William Fowles, William Hough, Arthur Hough, Frederick Hayes, George A. Hayes, Bottonsley (Widnes), Smith (Liverpool). E. Marston (Liverpool), Alfred Tracy, and John Tracy (Widnes). The funeral obsequies were performed by the Revs. T. Brackenbury and W. George. In the chapel the congregation sang the hymn "Come let us join our friends above,"and at the grave side "Rock of ages cleft for me" was sung. The duties of undertaker were earned out by Mr. R. Morris, High-street. HOLY TRINITY CHURCH TEA MEETING. The annual tea meeting in connection with Holy Trinity Church was held on Friday, when about 250 per- sons tat down to tea. A conversazione afterwards took plate. A quantity of armour including swords used at the battle of Waterloo, was exhibited by the Rev. Dr. Preston ; microsconic lenses were in charge of Messrs. Cunningham and Nickson ; stereoscopic views were lent by Mr. Hankinson ; and a number of photographs were forwarded by Mr. Charles Timmins. The schoolroom had been neely decorated, a number of choice plants kindly lent by -Mr. S. B. Chadwick, J.P.,of Higher Run- corn, and Mr. Nancollis, adding greatly to the effective- ness of the scene. The arrangements throughout reflected credit upon the various committees, namely, refresh- ment, Meselames Meadowcroft,Timmins,Robinson,Hors- field, West, Turner, Lea, Cunningham and Annett ; Misses Andrews, Tyrie,Mattison and Burrows ; Decora- tion Committee, Messrs. Hankinson, Timmins,Horsfield, Annett, Lancaster, Abrams,Nickson,?earson,Hazlehurst, Gleave and Garratt ; amusements, Messrs. Pritchard, Cunningham, John Turner, Lea, Lancaster, Savage, Burrows, Pe trson and Nickson. The Rev. Dr. Preston delivered an address in which he reviewed the work of the church during the east year. At the close he gave two. handsome volumes to E. Fen- wick, who took the girl's prize,and W.Cooper, the boy's. During the evening an interesting programme was gone through. It consisted of duets on the piano by Mrs. Darbyshire and Miss Preston ; selections on the piano and violins by the Misses Horsfleld, Mr. 0. Timmins and Sons; trio, 'Messrs. Lancaster Nickson, and Pearson ; songs. Mrs. Hulme, Miss Johnson,Messrs. Newman,Nick- sin, Pearssn, and F. Timmins. Votm of thanks were accorded to the subscribers, to the committee, and to those who had taken part in the programme.</p>
        <p>President Cleveland does not have it all his own way in the matter of plain speaking in the United States. Ile has this week been the subject of an extraordinary denunciation by Senator Tillman, of South Carolina, who made himself famous a few months ago by leading the movement for disfran- chising the negro voters. According to this Senator the President has followed the advice of "bootlicks and sycophants who have crawled on their knees for crumbs of patronage, and betrayed their con- stituents for the offices in his gift. In the entire history of the country the high office of President has never been 80 prostituted, and Mr. Cleveland has only heeded the interests of his moneyed friends—his owners or partners." This extraor- dinary outburst was, it may be explained, not incited by the President's action in connection with the Venezuelan question, but by his opposition to the bi-metallist or " silver " party.</p>
