        <p>ASK U YOR GROUBR FOR DOTIGALL'S PATENT SELFRAISING FLOUR THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, FEBRUARY. 8, 1896 RUNCORN AND WIDNES. --a THE CRIMINAL ASSAULT AT WESTON. At Chester Assizes on Friday before Mr. Justice Day. a youth, 16 years of age, named John Alfred Done, of Weston Point,was sentenced to twelve months imprison- ment with hard labour,for committing a criminal assault on a child four years of age, named Hannah Miles,living with her parents at Weston Point. BETHESDA LITERARY ANDSOCIAL CLASS' The Rev. J. Simpson gave a study of " Hamlet " at the meeting of this class on Tuesday evening. He spoke of the character as being perhaps the best known and least understood of Shakespeare's creations. Hamlet seemed to have been a young man with good purposes of heart, swayed with a high ideal, but of rather a weak and vacillating soul. The question, was Hamlet mad? was discussed, the lecturer taking the negative. There was a good attendance. For NAME BROOCEIBS, soil silverd name brooches 100, (any name) Government stamped, Presents or Articles for yourself, you may visit DEBBRTS, 10, CHURCH-STRBBX, LIVBRPOOL, where you can get hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery, Blectro-Plate. Cutlery. Purses, Pocket Knives, etc.. at wholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chron- gmph Watch, 6s. 11d., etc.), on page 7 f this Paper. -Advt MARINERS' CHURCH MISSION, RUNCORN. On Thursday evening last, Mrs. Bartram's Bible Class had their first tea. Upwards of thirty members sat down. A vote of thanks was given on behalf of Mrs. Fenwick, Mrs. Banner, and Mrs. Handles. Mrs. Bertram hoped the class would increase. Songs and games were enjoyed, and a pleasant evening was spent. The • Rev. A. H. Bertram, Messr:. D. Smith, J. Williamson, T. Sweetman, and W. Tunnicliffe were present. Mr. S.nith said he was very pleased with the Way the tea had been get up. He wished to see the class grow. AN UNSUSTAINRD CHARGE. At Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday before Mr. James Handley and Mr. F. J. Norman, two lads about thirteen years of age named Jonathan Stringer and Walter Shaw, of John-street, were charged with stealing the iron-work of a bedstead, tha property of Benjamin Scott, dealer in old metal, Heath-road. Mr. Scott stated that he had seen the lads about his premises on many occasions, and he had driven them away. On the 14th January he found the iron in question which belonged to him had been sold at Mr. Howard's marine store.—William Howard stated that the iron was brought to his premises by three lads but he was not sure that the defendants were amongst them.—The magistrates decided under the circumstances to discharge the defendants. MARINER'S CHTJRCIL—SOCIAL EVENING. A social evening was spent in this place on Monday evening, the proceedings being presided over by Mr. Jones. Refreshments were provided, and an interesting programme was gore through. Selections were givenl by Messrs. S. Temple, W. Lunt, W. Outhwaite, Miss C. , Williamson and the Misses Temple, and songs were rendered by Mr. D. Smith, Mr. J. Nall.Mr. Stansbie,Miss M. Hill, and Mrs. Whitchurch. Mr. D. Smith also gave a sketch, and the Misses Matthews a duet. An interest- ing item was the performance of a dialogue entitled "The Seaside Lodging House "in which Miss L. Bread- man, Miss A. Hill,Messra. Readies, Horton, R. A. Banner and J. Williamson took rart. Mr. Gardner presided at the organ, Games were also engaged in. At the close la vote of thanks was tendered those who had taken part I on the motion of Mr. D. Smith. MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN. There was again a good attendance at the weekly en- I tertainment provided in the Seamen's Institute on Tues- day evening. Mr. Shaw, missionary, presided, and addressed a few words of welcome. Songs were pleas- ingly interpreted by Miss Nellie Wright, Mr. T. Woods, and Mr. S. Cross. The instrumental selections included a violin solo by Mr. C. Tonks, and a pianoforte duet by the Misses Tonks and Dutton. Recitations were given by Miss J. Miles, Miss F. Coahill, and Master Miles. The chief feature of the entertainment was a musical can- tata entitled "The Fairies Visit" given by the members of the Parish Church Band of Hope who had been well I trained by Miss Dutton. The following took part :- 1 Ethel Miles, Isabella Saunders. Nellie Holloway, Sarah Tench, Annie Hunt, Fanny Coxhill, Eliza Darbysbire, Nellie Wright, Kate Hutton, Percy Miles, George Brere- ton, Ralph Gibbs, James Lamb, and Montague Miles. The concluding item was a sketch entitled "A Friend in Need." The usual vote of thanks concluded a pleasant evening's entertainment. RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. A special meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall, Runcorn. on Friday morning. There were i present—Mr. E. Imison, J.P., n the chair; the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. Llghtburn' Grice, Jones, Bisbrown and Sweetman ; Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk), and Mr. E. Marshall (secretary). On the motion of Mr. Grice, se- conded by the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, it was decided to affix the seal of the Council to a petition to the Lords Com- missioners of her Majesty's Treasury pointing out that when the Burial Board was transferred to the Council last March the outstanding debt was £4,884 33. Form- ing part of this were three mortgages for £3,284 33., at £3 10s. per cent. per annum, and the mortgagees being desirous to be repaid, the Council proposed to borrow £3,280 on interest at the rate of £3 ss. per cent. per annum in order to pay them off, and to reduce the rate of interest payable on the debt. The petition was in accordance with the resolution passed at a meeting of the vestry on the previous evening. The Council re- quested the approval of the Treasury to the proposed borrowing.</p>
        <p>THE NURSING EXAMINATION RESULTS AT RUNCORN. The result of the recent examinations of those who attended the series of nursing lectures in the Technical Institute has just been received. Of the eight ladies who presented themselves for examination, seven have been awarded certificates, and five of them have passed with distinction. The successful students are Miss Mack, Mrs. W. H. Grice, Mrs. S. E. Davies, Mrs. Scragg, Miss B. Rowlands, Mrs. Bartram, and Miss Preston. THE CASTNER KNELLER ALICA.LI COMPANY. The statutory meeting of this company (Oldbury, Liverpool, and Manchester) was held at the Cannon-st. Hotel, London, on Thursday afternoon. Mr. E. Mather (of Manchester), who presided, said since the company had been constituted they had been able to secure a very eligible site for new works at Weston Point, an es- tuary on the Mersey. The contract for the land had been completed and he could safely say that so far as the situation was concerned for the purposes of carrying on their business it could not be equalled. The building of the works commenced this week, contracts were placed, aud the erectioa would be pursued with the utmost dili- gence during the next few months. There being no resolution to put to the meeting the proceedings termi- nated with a vote of thanks to the chairman. ST. PAUL'S MEN'S BIBLE CLASS, RUNCORN. The monthly " open " meeting of this class was held on Sunday afternoon, and was largely attended. The Pre- sident, Mr. Kniveton, occupied the chair. An address was delivered by the Rev. W. George. A quartette was sung by Mrs. Temperley, Mrs. Stubbs, Mr. Worrall, and Mr. Pendlebury, and solos were interpreted by Miss Jones, Miss Simkin, Mrs. Temperley, and Mr. Charles Atherton.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN illsnacr COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, on Thursday morning. Mr. E. Imison, J.P.. occupied the chair, and there were also present—Rev. Dr. Gastaldi. Messrs. P. Speakm4n, J.P., C.C., J. Pritehard, H. J. Grice, G. H. Kirkham, M. Sweetman, D. Bisbrown, J. Jones, J. Wilson, T. Dean, R. H. Posnett. and Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk), Mr. E. Marabail (secretary),Dr. McDougall (medical officer),and Mr. Wilding (surveyor). A LOW DEATH-RATE. Dr. McDougall in his monthly report stated that the deaths showed an average death-rate of the 12 months of 10.7, and exclusive of the inquest cases of 904 of the estimated population.—Mr. Bisbrown remarked that it was a remarkably low death rate,w hich he thought beat the record.—The report was adopted. HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS COAIMITTEE...--THE PAVING OF</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>PEESENPATION TO EX-INSPECTOR</head>
        <p>PEESENPATION TO EX-INSPECTOR</p>
        <p>BOWEN.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the cab and bus' drivers of Warrington was held on Thursday, last week, at Mr. Peter Clare's Refreshment Rooms, Bridge-st., for the purpose of presenting Mr. Bowen with a handsome marble clock as a tribute of their appreciation of his abilities while in the execution ' of his duty as hackney carriage inspector, and for the high esteem with which he is held among the men generally. In the absence of Mr. Arthur Browne, the president of the Amalgamated Society of Hackney Carriage Employee, Mr. Clooney occu- pied the chair. Mr. Edwin Plinston, secretary or the society, in making the presentation on behalf of the cab and bus' drivers of Warrington, spoke in high terms of the good feeling existing between the men and Mr. Bowen. The ex-Inspector, in thank- ing the men for their handsome gift,said he was the first Hackney Carriage Inspector in Warrington and had held that office for five years. He had served 25 years altogether in the police force but owing to ill-health he regretted to say he had to retire from duty. Mr. Bowen retired last month on a superannuation allowance. Mr. Bowen said that a better conducted or more respectable body of men he had never met with in any part of the, country than the cab and bus'dri vers of Warrington. A few toasts and songs brought a very pleasant evening to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>ST. HELENS THEATRE ROYAL.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS THEATRE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>I-ANTOMINIE OF "ROBINSON CRUSOE."</p>
        <p>As will be seen from an advertisement in another column, Mr. Wallace Revill, the esteemed proprietor of the Theatre Royal, St. Helens, has made an important engagement to cover the next two weeks. Be has arranged for a visit of the original Leopoldo in their grand comic pantomime of " Robmson Crusoe," which has been produced with such great success at the Theatre Royal, Oldham. The company includes some wellknown pantomime "stars," and is considered one of the strong- est:of its kind in the provinceoln addition to the nightly performances there will be two illuminated day perform- ances on the afternoons of Saturday next and of the following Saturday. The important announcement is also made that there will be no advance in the prices of admission and the special trains and conveyances will run as usual.</p>
        <p>ENVELOPES FROM 2s. PER 15000. THE "EXAMINER" WORKS, BENVISEI STREET</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>NTilso,</head>
        <p>NTilso,</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINE[{.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, February Bth, 1896.</p>
        <p>THE temporary truce which the Political leaders of the political parties seem Speeches. to have tacitly agreed upon is now at an end. During the last few days several im-</p>
        <p>portant political speeches have been made in different parts of the country. At the e.nd of last _</p>
        <p>week Lord SALISBURY addressed the Nonconform-</p>
        <p>ist Unionist Association in London; on Monday</p>
        <p>Mr. BALFOUR spoke at Bristol, and tho Montrose</p>
        <p>election has been the cause of several interesting and eloquent speeches by Mr. MoRLEY. Lord</p>
        <p>SALISBURY is surely one of the most unfortunate of orators. He never speaks now but his friends and his opponents expect to hear of one or more blazing indiscretions,"and his latest speech forms</p>
        <p>no exception to the general rule of his public addresses. At the present time the one disturbed</p>
        <p>portion of the British Empire is South Africa,and it might have been thought that the PRIME MINISTER, above all men, would have bee n</p>
        <p>anxious to say something that would tend to re- move the friction and suspicion that exist in that</p>
        <p>important dependency. But Lord SALISBURY does not construe his public responsibilities in that way. Instead of using words of conciliation he seizes upon the opportunity to make certain observations which, to say the least, were not strictly judicious or</p>
        <p>well-chosen. First of all, he sneers at the</p>
        <p>" patriotic " Government which, fourteen years ago, granted the Transvaal its independence, al- though the Government referred to contained no fewer than three of the leading members of the present Cabinet—namely, the Duke of DEVON- SHIRE, Mr. CHA3IBERLAIN and Mr. GOSCHEN. It is a compliment which these quondam Liberals will no doubt appreciate at its fall value. It is only</p>
        <p>one of the little penalties they have to pay for "going over." In the second place, he makes a comparison between the Home Rure of the Trans- vaal and the Home Rule which is proposed for Ireland,as if they were on all fours. Whether or</p>
        <p>not all comparisons are "odious" as some autho- rities have roundly declared, this particular com- parison was distinctly unnecessary and unpolitic. President KRUGER can fairly claim that he is the chief officer of a country which is entirely inde- pendent in every respect except as to its foreign relations, and he will certainly not admit that the Transvaal holds the same position to this country that Ireland would do in the event of the Home Rule Bill being passed. On the whole, therefore, Lord SALISBURY has not made Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S task any easier— for it must be remembered that there are still</p>
        <p>some delicate questions in need of settlement in South Africa—nor has he helped the Outlanders to the possession of those franchise rights which they justly claim. No compensation for these in- discretions is to be had from the PRIME MINISTER'S references to the Armenian question . We have it now from his own lips that his dip lo macy has, in this respect, been an utter failure. Last November he lectured the SULTAN upon his shortcomings in a fashion that was little short of menacing, and he hinted at the effective action that would be taken,if necessary, by the "Concert of Europe." There were about the same time movements of the British fleet towards the mouth of the Dardanelles, and altogether it seemed as though we were to have some of that "vigorous foreign policy" of which the Tories have talked so long and so often. But it has all onae to nothing. Lord SALISBURY himself now confesses that nothing has been done to protect the Armenians, and that as far as he is concerned nothing can be done. Instead of using strong and minatory language to the SULTAN he now in effect tells the Armenians that they must turn to that potentate for protection. It is undoubtedly a long time since this country has played so poor and impotent a part on the stage of forezgn affairs as it has done during the last few weeks. With reference to Mr. BAL- FOUR'S speech)at Bristol, it was remarkable for the declaration of a new Tory foreign policy. As every one knows the Tory party has, in interna- tional affairs, always had a sttong bias towards the Triple Alliance, but the famous telegram of the EMPEROR of Germany has altered all that. Russia and France are now in favour, and Mr. BALFOUR went out of his way to invite the former country to obtain a commercial port in the Pacific which would not be closed with ice for half the year. This new departure,which is rather startling in its suddenness, was commented upon by Mr. MORLEY in a speech at Brechin, on Wednesday. Mr. MOR- LEY neither cOmmends nor condemns the sudden reversal of policy, but points out that it is almost bound to have serious consequences. Probably the country as a whole will think pretty much as Mr. MORLEY thinks on this subject. Just as for- merly there was a decided disinclination to commit ourselves with the Triple Alliance there will be now RU aversion to rushin to the opposite extreme. It is well to keep on friendly terms with Russia and France, but at the same time there need be no unnecessary hostility toward Germany. On the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>STREETS.</head>
        <p>STREETS.</p>
        <p>This Committee recommended that the Penketh-lane sewer be re-constructed by putting in a 12 inch earthen ware pipe sewer for a length of about 140 yards at an estimated cost of £B5 and that application be made to the Local Government Board for their sanction to a loan for such amount for 30 years; and also that the Urban District Council sewer, level, pave, metal flag, channel, and make good several specified streets, parts of streets and passages under the provisions of the Private Street , Works Act, 1892 ; that application be made to the Local Government Board for their sanction to a loan for ten years for the amount required to carry out the aforcsaid Private Street Works; that the expenses when incurred be repaid by the owners by half- yearly instalments; and that interest at the rate of of 4 per cent. per annum be charged Mr. Taylor moved the confirmation of the minutes. He stated that 27 streets had been completed, and 11 more would be finished an March. The present recommenda- sions were for the completion of 22 streets, which would make about 60 in all. Be did not think there would be many left unmade afterwards.—Mr. Dean seconded the adoption of the committee's recommendations.—Mr. Wilson thought the making of so many streets was an injury to the property owners because many of the streets did not require makieg.—Mr. Taylor said he would like to ask the previous speaker what streets he objected to He thought if the council did not follow up this work o. the making of streets it would be a great injustice to th ratepayers, and especially to the t(nants who had been sufferers for a great many years.—The minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>THE TECHNICAL INSTITUTE. The committee in charge of this institute recom- mended that the application be made to the Department of Science and Art for the loan of objects of science and art ; that the following inscription he put on one of thg stones at the front of the institute :—" This building was opened on the 31st August 1895, by Sir John T. Brunner, Bart., M.P. E. Imison, chairman of the Run- corn Urban District Council, F. Wiswall.chairman of the Technical Instruction Comniittee.—The minutes were</p>
        <p>adopted. THE SUGGESTED GRATING DOCK AT WESTON POINT. A letter was read by the Secretary from Mr. Henry Branch, in which he called attention to the proposed graving bock at Weston Point, and asked for the assist- ance of the Council to bring it to a successful issue. Ile submitted facts showing that such a dock was necessary, and that it could to worked at Weston Point with suc- cess. His reason for writing was to ask the Council to be good enough in addition to using their own personal influence in the matter to send an official letter from the Council to the Weaver Trustees urging their most fav- ourable consideration of the scheme. The dock could for a comparatively small outlay be made into a dry one. He had sent the whole of the information contained in the communication to the engineer of the River Weaver some time ago, and had had a reply from him stating that he would bring the matter before the committee as early as possible. Here was a grand opportunity of re- lieving the present depressed state of the town,for should the powers that be have proof of the unanimous wish of the people of the county—this dock should be made into a dry one,he was sure they would accede to their united request.—The Chairman said they might regard the scheme in question as an urgent matter, and if two- thirds of those present expressed their desire to discuss it they could do so and pass any resolution thereon they might think flt.—Mr.Speakman said he was vary pleased indeed that the matter had been sent to them for support. it was a step in the right direction. He thought they might pass a resolution cordially sup porting the scheme, and he therefore moved" That this Council heartily approve of Mr. Branch's scheme and hopes the Weaver TrUStePS will give the matter theis early and favourable consideration, the resolution to be conveyed to the Weaver Trustees under the seal of the Council."—Mr. Bisbrown said the promoters of the scheme deserved their sympathy and practical assistance. There was an absolute nece3sity far a graving dock and it happened that the one in question was on the right side of Runcorn bridge.—Mr. Taylor supported the motion.—Mr. Pritchard believed that there was a treat necessity for a graving dock.—Mr. Grice also supported the motion which was carried unanimously. THE BLASTING AT THE CEMETERY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilson said he had repeatedly heard blastirg operations going on at the Cemetery. He thought it would be well if this could be avoided, especially close to graves in which bodies were interred.—Mr. Grice said it had been the practice in one portion of the Cemetery where it was rocky. The operations were conducted with the utmost care and with as little annoyance as possible.—The Chairman said.it would be well if the matter were considered at the committee meeting.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BRIDGE FARES. The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi said in August last he intro- duced the subject of the heavy charges made upon work- ingmen who had to walk across Runcorn Bridge, and nothing had yet been done. He thought arrangements might be made to bring about some improvement. The ferry did not pay the railway company, and they might in their negotiations consider it. He urged that a memorial be sent to the L. and N.-W. Railway Company to obtain a reduction of the charge which would be a boon to working people.—Mr. Grice said it would be an advantage to Runcorn and Widnes if the charge were lower. With regard to the ferry he could not say what arrangements could be made.—Mr. Speakman considered the matter rather premature. They had hardly finished their negotiations in asking the company to open the ferry, and if they wanted them to close it what would the people think of them. He might say that the rail- way company had made a very handsome reduction in contract tickets between Liverpool and Runcorn.—Mr. Dean thought there was no reason why they should not approach the railway company .—lt was very kind of the railway company to reduce contract tickets to Liverpool but that affected very few workingmen in the town.—Mr. Speakman said they had had the ques- tion raised previously and deputations had waited upon the directors to induce them to make the reduction when the company gave certain reasons why they could not accede to the request. One was the extraordinary cost of the bridge(laughter), and another was that having got advantages they would not throw them lightly away. They would all be glad if the charge could be reduced to 63.—The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi said it was nine years since repreeentations were made to the Company.—The Chair- man said it seemed unreasonable to approach the Rail- way Company with the stmgestion that they were prepared to abandon the ferry when only six months ago they urged that it should be properly equipped. RUMOURED CLOSING OF WORKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilson said there was a rumour prevailing that the Runcorn Soap and Alkali Works were going to be closed. If so they were going to leave a dangerous legacy in the shape of a huge mountain of alkali waste which was a nuisance, the smell being disagreeable. The Coun- cil ought to see what could be done.—The Chairman thought the subject should be submitted to the Health Committee. The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>W ESTON.</head>
        <p>W ESTON.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATEL—About 11.30 on Sundaynight,Harriet Lewis,domestic servant, aged 17 years,daughter of John Lewis,Sweetman's-row, Weston ,died very suddenly. She had gone to the rervice at the Weston Wesleyan Chapel, but feeling unwell was compelled to leave before its conclqsion. She went home,and was subsequently seized with convulsions. Some time ago deceased suffered from brain fever. No inquest was considered necessary.</p>
        <p>The body of a man, supnosed to be a burglar, was found on Thurcdny in tha chimney of a taproom, at Locks, Fenny Stratford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>CH.A.RGE OF ATTEMPTED MURDER AND</p>
        <p>SITICIDEIAT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>POLICE COURT PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at Runcorn Police Court before Messrs. F. J. Norman and E. Imisen, a middle-aged woman named Catherine Gregory,a married woman of Penketh- lane, was charged with attempting to murder her child, Gertrude, aged two years, by jumping with it in the Ship Canal at Runcorn on the 3rd February ,and she was further charged with attempting to commit suicide with jumping into the canal on the same date. John Ball, Cooper-st., Runcorn, bargeman on the Ship Canal, stated that on the day in question he was in tow with the tug Gvrennie, when he saw the prisoner sitting on the steps opposite the Bridgewater Lock,with a child in her arms. He was proceeding up the canal and his barge giving a sheer on one side caused him to turn round, and thus lose sight of the woman. On again look- ing in that direction he saw that she was in the water. A child was in the arms of the woman. He cried to those on the packet to let go the barge,and they returned to try and rescue the woman. Be also cried "Bridge- water lock ahoy." He could not say whether,she jumped or fell into the water.</p>
        <p>Henry Hardley, gateman at the Bridtvewater lock, stated that he heard an alarm given by the last witness about 11.30 on the date named. He got a small boat and crossed the canal to the woman. Witness held her up and found she had the child in her arms. She did not say anything and had "little help in herself." She made no attempt to move when in the water. He did not know how she got in. Sergeant Lee said he rendered first aid, and in about threequarters of an hour she recovered consciousness. They obtained dry clothes, after which they took her home. Witness asked her what was the reason that she had come down to the docks and she replied that she had come down to the lock with the sole purpose of destroy- ing herself and child. He then asked what was her reason for coming down, and she said it was through the unkindness of her husband who did not give her any money to buy food, and she had scarcely anything in the house. It was through starvation. That morning wit- ness charged her with attempting to murder her child as well as attempting to commit suicide, when she said " I didn't jump into the canal, but fell in." Toe previous night she told him it was a pity she had been saved. She would have sooner died. When witness visited the house there was plenty of food. He was sure she was not short of food.</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman said the charges were of a very serious character and the best plan would be to adjourn the case until that day week. They would like the doctor at S:nutsford to examine her so that they might have some evidencß of her mental condition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY,</p>
        <p>LIMITED.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly meeting of the above-mentioned society was held in Lacy-street schoolroom, Widnes,on Tuesday evening,when there was a good attendance. Mr. Dudley occupied the chair, and he was supported by Messrs. E. Aston, J. Sayle, J. R.Terson, T. Riley, W. Dyer, W. Upton (secretary). Jonah Evans (treasurer), and Chad- wick (auditor). The Chairman said they might congratulate themselves that the memuership had increased, and that £220 had been spent in wages more than in the corresponding period last half-year. The interest from the Co-operative Wholesale Society on loan capital had been reduced dur- ing the six months from 4 per cent. to 3 per cent. Mr. Poulson moved an amendment to the balance sheet, but no one seconded,and the report and statement of accounts were passed, a summary of which was given in our last issue, In reyly to Mr. R. W.H. Clarke, the Chairman tated that the Frodsham branch had not been the success it might have been.- -Mr. Crozier noticed that the penny bank was going down very much.—The Chairman replied that that was owing to the fact that there had been bad trade.—Mr. Poulson moved that £3O out of the £59 3s. 11d, to be devoted to special d ividend reserve fund, should be dis- tributed as follows. £lO to the secretary, £lO to the treasurer, and £lO. o the manager.—The Chairman did not think it feasible to take such a step as granting bonuses. There might be a mee.ing some time to pro- pose that instead of the committee having £2 per half year, they should get £l2.—The matter then dropped. —lt was decided that the annual tea party be held at Runcorn. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Fitzpatrick moved that the secretary convey their sympathy to the family and friends of the late Mr. Royle. —Mi. Poulson seconded and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>FRODSH AM.</head>
        <p>FRODSH AM.</p>
        <p>A WIFE DE4ERTER.—CharIes Hammond Wooldridge, formerly ot Alvanley. and who deserted his wife and family in September, 1894, was committed to prison for one month at Frodsham Police Court on Thursday last week. The prisoner had been arrested at Southampton. —Mr. Riley, relieving officer, proved the lease. GAS AND WATER COMPANY.—The directors of thi3 Com- pany, in their report just issued, state that the revenue account shows a balance of £B2B 103. 2d. and after pay- ment of the usual dividends on the respective shares left an undivided balance of £613 83. 101, and a balance of £2OO to the reserve fund. On the Ist of April the price of the as was reduced 3d. per thousand cub:c feet, and the increase of the consumption continued satisfactory. THE AMBULANCE CLAss.—The report of the recent examination has just been received, and the results are far better than was anticipated. The successful candi- dates were Messrs. John Hancock, Thomas Booth,James Harvey, Samuel Alderman, Thomas Earlam, Francis H. Colson, William H. Booth, William Helsby, Charles H. Sladen, and William Forster. " ROOKS : WHAT TO READ AWD How To READ."—This was the subject of a paper delivered by Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P., of Helsby, before the members of the Mutual linprovement Class on Thursday evening. At the close the paper was fully discussed by the members, and a hearty vote of thanks accorded to Mr. 'Guest. PAROCHIAL CommirrEE.—At the monthly meeting held on Friday evening, Mr.H.E. Heywood in the chair, the Inspector of Nuisances, &amp;c., reported upon the measures taken for the supply of water to the premises ordered at the last meeting. With respect to Mr. Booth's case, it was decided that in the event of this work not being done within fourteen days, the Inspector obtain tenders for consideration at the next meeting. It was also decided that the tender for the work required at Mr. Sharrock's property be put in hand at once. It was arranged that the Inspector obtain tenders without any further delay for works necessary at Mrs. Holland's property at Frodsham Bridge, and that with regard to the house occupied by Mrs. Nickson opposite Trinity Chapel that the owner be dealt with as in other cases. Mn. WILLIAM COOKE'S CONCERT PART2.—At the Town Hall Frodsham a concert was given ,by the above on Wednesday the Town Hall being crowded. The con- cert was opened with a pianoforte duet by Miss Annie M. Cooke, and Mr. William Cooke, which was loudly applauded. Miss E. Alice Cooke, nicely sang" Jack at Sea," and was followed by Mr. Wesley C. Cooke, who sang" Say Au revolt but not goodbye," in a splendid manner, "Kitty of Coleraine" was beautifully sung by Miss Simkin, and was loudly encored, and she responded with "Love on the Raft." Mr. Charles Atherton followed with " The Old Soldier "which he sang in magnificent style. A violin solo was nicely played by Miss Annie Smith, and was loudly applauded. Mrs. Timperley sweetly sang. "The better Land." By special request, Miss Alice Cooke sang. "I persuaded him," and being recalled sang another verse. The first half of the programme was concluded wita a humorous trio "Three nice young gentlemen." by Messrs. C. Atherton, Thomas and Wesley Cooke. This item also had to be repeated. The second half was also very successfully rendered, and the concert as a wholo was quite a sucdess.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN POLICE COURT. FRIDAY.—Befo-e Mr. F. J. Norman and Mr. Imison, CHARGE OF THEFT.—Joseph Hopley, was charged with stealing 5 cwt. of old iron, value 7s. 6d. from the Ship Canal works at Runcorn, between the 18th January and the sth February, the property of the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany.—John Ayres. foreman at the Old Quay engineering department, stated that the bolts, fishplates, and waggon chills (produced) belonged to the Manchester Ship Canal Company. He could not say when they were safe on their works. He identified the chills by the Pattern. He had not missed them. They were lying up and down the works. He valued the iron produced at 15s. He had never seen the prisoner before, and the prisoner harrnt worked for him.— P.C. Gregory stated that he apprehended the prisoner the previous night at his house in Okell-street. On the way to the Police Station he said he thought witness was making a mistake as Mr. Congreve said they could have the iron if they picked it up They were all in it, and " Taff "as well as Mr. Smith said they could have it. He charged him with the offence at the Police Station. and he replied "there was another • chap ' named Taylor that went with me and helped him to push the barrow. It only ran about 3d. and of tobacco each. The lot was not worth more than that." He afterwards said they all had two pints of ale out of the money Mr. Howard (marine store dealer) gave him William Howard, sane of George Howard, broker, Top Locks, Runcorn, deposed that the prisoner brought the bolts produced about January 18th and February Ist. Questioned as to what money he gave the defendant. witness said he had not broughtlhis book, He gave him 2s. on the first date for about 1;, cwts. of bolts. He could not say what he gave him the second time. Questioned by Superintendent Okell wit- ness stated that he had not known the prisoner long. He did not know his character.—Superintendent Okell said if marine store dealers would he a bit more careful from whom they bought there would be less thieves about. The witness would have to be very careful in futine.—Mr. Congreve, resi- dent engineer on the Ship Canal from Eastham to Latchford stated that he had employed the prisoner to gather up the scrap along the canal. He paid him at the rate of les. a ton. The prisoner had worked with a gang pulling up a line of rails, and if he had got permission to take broken bolts it was all right, but the fish plates belonged to the purchasers.— The Chairman said the witness Howard had stated that he had only bought bolts, and in order to give the prisoner a chance to bring witnesses on his behalf, the case would be adjourned. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Harriet Baxter was charged with this offence in High-st. on the sth February.—P.C. Butler proved the case, and she was committed to prieon for one month.</p>
      </div>
