      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WESLEYAN ROME MISSIONARY MEETING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN ROME MISSIONARY MEETING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Special sermons on behalf of Home Missions were preached in St. Paul's Church on Sunday, when the Rev. C. de P. Gliddon preached at the morning service, and the Rev. W. George in the evening. Appeals were made for liberal support for the home mission work carried on by the Wesleyan denomination. There were good con- gregations. On Thursday evening the annual meeting was held in St. Paul's Wesleyan Church, when Mr. T. Simister presided. There were also present on the platform, the Revs. George Jacksen,B.A., of Eninburgh, Sampson Weaver, of Liverpool, T. Brackenbury, W. George, C. de Pliddon, J. Stafford ; Messrs. F. J. Nor- man, IP.. E. Taylor, J. Simpson, R. Rollinson, A. R. Norman, E. Marshall, and others. ' The annual report was presented by the Rey. W. George, and stated that the success of their operations in great towns continued unbroken. There had been contributed from the different districts £27,303, and other sums brought the total to £37,942. From the Liverpool District there had been forwarded £2,120, of which the Runcorn circuit had sent £5O 18s. Bd. (Ap- plause.) The Chairman announced that he had asked Mr. G. L. Davies to occupy the position of Ohairman that night, but being unable to do so, he had given him a subscrip- tion of £5. (Applause.) The Rev. Sarnpson Weaver said he believed that Britain was the greatest mission field in the world. He explained that the report presented gave them a most inadequate idea of the money raised and the work done during the past year. Be believed while it might be true that we were never so far removed from uniformity we were never so near unity as we were to-day. (Ap- pplause.) He referred to the difficulties of home mission work in the villages and stated that the village stations deserved their support. The Rev. G. S. Jackson,of Edinboro', authorof "First Things First," met with a hearty reception. He stated that in their work at Edinboro' they always tried to cul- tivate the assistance of the press. He said the Method- ists did not go to Scotland to make Presbyterians Methodists. Toey had set their faces against prosely- tising, and he was glad to say that they were heartily welcomed by other religious bodies who worked with them. In Edinboro' there were 40,000 to 50,000 persons who never entered a church, and while there were those he did not need to apologise for the presence of Method- ism there. (Hear, hear.) The meeting concluded with the usual vote of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Berore Mr. John Crosfield (chairman), and Mr. S. It. Armitage. INTEMPERANCE. —George Welsby for being guilty of drunkenness and disorderly conduct, was fined 10s. and costs ; James Scarisbrick, same offence was fined a like amount ; this defendant said to the magistrate's clerk Will you give me time mate, I'll pay in a fortnight" ; James Shaw was fined 7s. 6d. and costs ; Joseph Bell was fined ss- including costs ; George Flanaghan and Edward Weston, ss. I and costs. - _</p>
        <p>------- SCHOOL ATTENDANCE. John Andrews, was fined ;Os. in- eluding costs, for neglecting to send Illstwo children to- school ; Mary (Heave, for her child Jotin s irregular attenu- ance was ordered to see that he went in future ; Edwin Daniels, was fined ss. including costs ; George Gerrard, 10s. including costs for two children; George Mills, two children. 10s. including costs. CYCLISTS HIDING ON TSB FOOTPATR.--Mrs. Bellian wife of Mr. William Bellian, Liverpool-road, was charged with riding on the footpath in Penketh. on the 31st January.—She pletuled that the road was in bad condition and that she went on the path for a few yards. The magistrates inflicted penalty of of is. m and costs (11s in all) ; Frederick Appleton, for same offence iu Sankey on the 28th January, was fined_ a like sum. Don AT LARGE.—Thatnas Monaghan for allowing his dog Ito lad at large in Burtonwood on the 22nd January, was fined.. 58. including costs.</p>
