      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. PlUDAY.—Before Alcierman_ Harrison_ (chairman), Dr.</p>
        <p>Gornall, and Mr. S. Wright. OBSCENE LANGUAUE AT BANK QUAY STATION.—Joseph Mather Lowe, a young man, was charged with having on the 27th December made use of obscene language at Bank Quay Station.—He pleaded guilty and expressed his contrition.— Mr. F. Lambert, solicitor to the London and Not th-Westarn Railway at Button, pro mated, and stated the cffence,adding that it was aggravated by defendant being an employe of the .company at Wigan.—A penalty of £1 and costs (30s. In ell) was inflicted. ArPearrricus Summoarrn.,—The following apprentices of Messrs. James Platt and Son were summoned for leaving their work at divers dates and putting their employers I 3 a loss of various sums :—Catherine Birmingham, Annie Pr:ce, ,d Hate Lowe.—Mr. Browne appeared tor the complainants ..nd said theirobject was to deter apprentices from tics kind of conduct.—Each defendant was ordekd to return to work and pay costs. The damp _les were withdrawn. ALLEGED CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—James Quinn and An- erew Quinn, Moss Bank, Widnes, were charged, the foi mer -vith working a horse in an unfit state on the tth January, •• the latter with causing it to he worked.—lnspe .tor • 1, ~ said on the 6th January at 11.20 in the row ning he saw James Quinn in charge of a horse attached to a float containing pigs. The horse was distressed and in a low condition and was sweating profusely and Lembling • om apparent furious driving. It was lame in both forelegs, and there was also a wound on the fetlock which was bleed- • g. The animal was undoubtedly suffering severe pain, and it was an act of cruelty for it to be worked. Andrew Quinn • .11 him elterwards that the horse had been in tha stable some time from the effects of an accident.—Dctective Yonaghan had his attention drawn by the Inspector to the animal. The hind fetlock was bleeding, and its knres loot•ed as if they had been cut some time previously and it looked as i• it had been driven pretty fast.—Mr. Browne denied the yeuelty, and said that the horse had bolted Fin lWidnes 19 days previous to the alleged offence,and had cat its knees and jured the fetlock. It was in perfectly good condit'on. The wouud was caused by the accident and had a scab on when Ihe animal left Widnes which had been knocked off in rriul ning.—James Cliffe, horse dealer, and Mr. Oscar, blacksmith, L 3tlz gave evidence. The first said he had seen the animal on the day in question and had talked to Quinn who was driving for 10 minutes. He saw no lameness or sore.—Mr. Oscar said be bad shod the hoi ie on the 31st Dccember.rand there was nothing wronrc then.—Mr. Hewitt, veterinary surgean. said be examined the animal shortly after the Inspector saw it. It bad a slight wound on the hind fetlock. It was possible for 'the wound to bave been right on leaving Widnes, and he did not think it was cruel to: work till horse.—The magistrates said they had consideredlthe case and thoughtithere was not sufficient evidence to convict, and they dismissedlt. ASSAULT ON A SISTER.—John Muskett, Church-street, who did not appear. was charged with assaulting and beating nis sister, Mary Ann Woodin, on the 7th Jannary.—Mr. Browne represented Complainant,a.nd said Me. Woodin's inother,Mrs. Muskett, had died lately;and th, eldest son had called all the family together so that they could divide various little things which they prized. Complainant selected two small orna- rrents, whereupon defendant became nasty. The day before iefuneral he came again and told her she would need a van for her goods. She replied and he threatened her, and in the presenee of the whole family rushed at her and struck her in •Le face, so that she fell. Since then he was given the op- ,ortunity of apolocising and paying for the summons but he would not do the latter and the case had to go on.—Mrs. 'To Ain. corroborated, and Mr. Woodin and Sergeant Chiatler spoke as to the mark on complainant's face.—Defendant was lined £1 and costs and an advocate's fee allowed Mr. Browne.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Harri- son, Francomb, and Wright, and Messrs. F. Monks, and</p>
        <p>J.*. Hayward. HIS FIRST OFFENCE.—Matthew Winstanlev, 41, Leigh-st.. Harlestown, was charged with being drunk in charge of a horse and trap in Winwick-st. on Saturday—P.O. Leonard proved the offence, and as it was defendant's first offence the magistrates inflicted a penalty of ss. and cost 3 only. Minix AND DISORDERLY—The following persons were dealt with for committing this offence at divers times and places :—William Warburton, 18. Barry-st , fined ss. and costs; Mary Grimes. having ill-health was sent home to Manchester to her friends ; William Higginson, 28, Welling- ton-st. (Ist offence, represented by Mr. Browne), ordered to pay 68. to the poor-box ; Mary Coyne. 21, Dial-st., ss. and costs ; Martin Hart, 14, Friars'-green, adjourned case, sent to gaol for a month. He called several witnesses on his behalf, but none could prove that he was not drunk, an i the magis- trates ultimately decided that they must accept P.C.Turner's version of the case and sentenced defendant as above. KEEPING FOWLS, ETC., A NUISANCE.—Jeseph Bullock, 283, Winwick-street, was summoned forkeeping fowls, rabbits and goats, on his premises, so that they were a nuisance and Injurious to health.—lnspector Norman, said defendant was a provision dealer, and the yard of his shop was covered with pens for poultry and two goats. On his visit on Thursday, the 30th January, he found the goat in the house. Mrs. Bullock said tbeir child was so fond of it that it came in now and then. The yard was dirty and unwholesome--Inspector Powell said he had also visited the house and found two rabbits in abox. The yard was filthy and the stench penetrated into the street.—Defendant was ordered to abate the nuisance in twe days. MORE STA.Tims LOA.FERS.—john Griffin, Thomas Griffin, Bernard Hennigan, Patrick Carroll (all of Percival's Feld), and Michael Scully, Angel-yard, Hill-street, were all charged with obstructing the passage of Winwick-street, on Januar 24th.—P.O. Bell said defendants were loitering on the foot- path near the Central Station and obstructing the free passage of it.—Sculley and Thomas Griffin, were dismissed, and the case against the others adjourned for one month.</p>
        <p>TUESDIY.—Before Mr. R. Ciarnett.(in the chair), :and Mr. B.</p>
        <p>J. Gn eat:</p>
        <p>PEDDLING WITHOUT A. LiccweE.—Patrick Flynn was charged witliacting as a pedlar without certificate on the .3rd Inst., and with its being his second offenee.—Detective Ser- geant Monaghan said that on the night of the day in question he was in Horsemarket-st., and saw the defendant going to shops chiefly occupied by females. He was acting as a ped- lar and was under the influence of drink. Defendant was a man who would not work, and on the 16th of last month he was convicted of a similar offence at Warrington, and had also been tried at courts all over the country. He was fined 20s. and costs or in default one month. MORE DISORDERLINESS AT THE WORM:IOI7SE BY AN OLD OFFENDER.—Annie Smith, an inmate of the Workhouse was charged with being disorderly there on Monday.—Mr. Shearer prosecuted, and said that defendant had used some very obscene language to the assistant master, and it was her usual conduct from day to day ; she was locked up.—Mr. Dobson. assistant master of the Workhouse, gave corrobora- tive evidence.—Detective Barlow stated that he received the defendant into custody and she Used disgusting language then.—This was ber 16th appearance, an,' several times she had beeu brought up for the same offence at the Workhouse. She was be committed for one month's hard labour. INSOBRIETY.—Lawrence Mannion, for being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-st., at 8.25 on Monday night, Was fined ss. and costs —P.O Corbett proved the case. WEDNESDAY.—Before Ms. John Fairclough (chairman), and</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Geddes.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT OFA -liV-Ais--;&amp;;lT.—James Kennedy, licensed as a pedlar, w is chargei with stealing from the shop of Mr. Adam Webster, tailor, Buttermarket-st., a man's vest value be., on Monday.—The Chief Constable applied for a week's remand in order that inquiries as to prisoner's antece- dents might be made. On Monday, Mr. Webster had occa- sion to leave the shop between five and six p.m. At 11 o'clock the following morning he missed the vest and not being able to account for it gave. information to the police. Detective Monaghan made enquiries and found that an hour i and a half after the vest was missed t had been sold at a shop In Mersey-it. Monaghan arrested the prisoner from the des- cription he got, and he had been since identified as the man who sold the vest. Mr. Webster recognised the vest as his property.—Detective Monaghan gave evidence as to the ar- s. rest.—Prisoner was remanded for a week.</p>
        <p>BIRDS, ANIMALS and FISH STUFF !SD, and mounted in Glass Cases of any design. HEADS and HORNS mounted, also for Ss le. 'liras na4 le in',o Hand Screens or Fire Screens. Dealer in British and Foreign Birds and Animals Skins, Glass Shades, Stags' Heads. Cabinets Store Boxes. Nets, &amp;c.' for Butterfly and Egg Collectors,—B. Cooxt, Naturalist and Furrier, 21, Renshaw-street, Liverpool. Established over 21 years.</p>
