        <p>:.^CHOOL A-TENDANCE CASE.—The following parents wee fined for ne: e, ;eg t ) car. 1 heir chi'dren to attendee.. el re',ularlv :--John We ve-, 52. Sh-w-sceet; W Warn Marshall, 42, Gilbe stre • ; Arthu: Haddock, 127 Surrey-street. Mr. T. Bowyer ps o 7e, t 4,lse Cases. ASSAULT ON THE POLK&amp; —JOhn William Holt, a ycung man, was oci with being drunk and disordeey in Devonshire--cm.;re. Runcorn, on the let Feb; nary ; also with assaulting P.C. Gregory whist in the execution of his duty, and he was futhrer charged with assaulting a nun named William Arthur Howard. Mr. Allen, solicitor, repre- sented the ilefendant.—P.C. Gregory bated that on the night of the date named he was going in the direction of Devonshire-square when he saw a crowd around the prisoner who was thrashing a boy named Potter. Witness told the prisoner to go home, and let the boy alone. The prisoner thereupon used most abusive language, and refused to go home. Witness closed with him, and whilst on the ground togetiler, the prisoner kicked him. He (witness) called its the assistance of Mr. Howard, who helped him to bring the prisoner to the Police St,tion. When opposite St. Paul's Chapel, the prisoner kicked both of them. In answer to questions by Mr. Allen, witness said that the prisoner was mad drunk at the time. Dr. Meamgall who examined the man did not tell witness that the prisoner had had an epilep- tic ft.—P.C. Lee deposed that the prisoner was in a beastly state of drnnkenness when he went to examine him. • He told witness that he had been to the football match and go'; excited because Runcorn had wun.—lnspector Hicks said the man was very drunk when broug,ot to the Police Station; there were no in:lT:Lions of his having had a fit.—Mr. Arthur Howe. d, 6,2 Byron-street. e•ave evidence as to assist ing Gre or/ and to being kicked by the prisoner.— Willi? m Po. r, 9 year: of age. Balfour-st., stated that he saw the p "%enter d •unk.—For the. defence, Mr. Allen stated t hat as socn re 1