      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NOBLEMAN IN PRISON.</head>
        <p>A NOBLEMAN IN PRISON.</p>
        <p>14 DAYS OR 2s. 9D.</p>
        <p>It seems iticrPrlible that in this age a nobleman or any man of the smalle at matins would spend 14 days in prison rather than pay 2i. 9d. ConEequently it appears to us to be probable that this eobleman did not clearly understand that the hard bed and wretched prison diet might have been avoided at a cost of less than 21. d. per day,when reckoned for the 14 days of his imprisonment. Returning to Tunbridge after 14 days in Maidstone. Prison, this gentleman found hundreds of friends and townsmen had turned out to escort him to a large hall, where a meeting was being held. Preceded by a band they drove to this meeting,ancl there, for the first time, he was addressed as" Lord" Freethy, because, as the sp%ker thought, he had nobly preferred 14 days in prison to allowing his child to run the risk of taking any of the disease of vaccination.</p>
        <p>"How unfortunate" many a mother who reads this will say, and who knows the value of V.V.V., when 2s. 9d. spent in purchasing a bottle of Vaccination Virus. Vanquisher ' or V.V.V. would have quite as effec- tually protected the child, by destroying all the diaease germs that might have been introduced with the lymph if given regularly till the vaccination sore was healed over." This excellent antiseptic remedy can be pur- chased from any chemist at 23. 9d., or from Barclay &amp; Sons, 95. Farringdon-street, London, or from the Vac cination Virus Vanquisher Company, Limited, 31, Snow Hill, London, E.C. This Company, we are informed, also offers to send a pamphlet which fully describes the merits and uses of V.V.V. to all who apply without any charge for pamphlet or postage.</p>
