      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>LUNATIC ASYLUM ON FIRE. MANIACS LEAP INTO THE FLAMES.</head>
        <p>LUNATIC ASYLUM ON FIRE. MANIACS LEAP INTO THE FLAMES.</p>
        <p>Quebec, February 4th.—The insane asylum at Beau port, two miles from here, is on fire, and there is great excitement here, as there are nearly 1,000 patients there, many of whom have relatives in this city. The institu- tion is in charge of the Grey Carmelite Nuns. The entire fire brigade of Quebec, a battery of artillery, and a com- pany of infantry of the volunteers have been sent there from here. The fire began in an outbuilding, and spread thence to the west wing of the asylum. Now it has in- vaded the entire building. The interior presents a terrible scene. Many of the maniacs refused to leave their cells, and fought fiercPly when force was employed to bring them out. Two, after being removed, ran back into the building and leaped into the flames. Others broke through the line of auards and fled to the adjoin- ing woods, some in a state of complete nudity, while others were but partially clothed. Twenty maniacs, men, kept together, and made a determined stand against their rescuers, hurling two soldiers in the flame 3. It required the entire force of military present to subdue them. The women among the insane vied with the men in desperate resistance, and tried to rush into the flames to avoid capture. One wcman patient dashed her I brains out against a wall. Four or five women seized a keeper, dragged him to the floor, and nearly killed him before he was rescued.</p>
        <p>It is reported from Bitlis that there have been 4,300 forced conversions of Armenians to Islam in that vilayet. The legal executions in 1895 were 132, being the same as in 1894, whereas in 1890 they were 102, and 108 in 1885.</p>
        <p>At the inquest on the Rochdale football player who re- ceived a fatal injury to his head on Saturday it was stated that there was no "extraordinary roughness "in the play. A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned A resident at Slough en Wednesday, obtained a verdic for £250 in an action against the Slough Urban Distric• Council for expenses to which he had been put through me as caused by drinking water from a well which ad been contaminated by an overflow from the public ewers.</p>
