      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TFIF TAAMINER, SATUPPAY, FEBRUARY 8, 1896.</head>
        <p>TFIF TAAMINER, SATUPPAY, FEBRUARY 8, 1896.</p>
        <p>FOOTISALi,.</p>
        <p>CFITCKETIIRS should tequue the " Pocket Mary And Companion," issued by the well-known Sports Mrnu- fac,wers, GEociGE G. 131.TssEY dr. Co„ P,cl;barn, London. Ail the information Cricketers require is found therein, as well as the usual Diary, yet It will go in the wais,,oat piOse. Leather bound, gilt edges, 6d. ; pc,stage. fel. If sou are not satisfied, G. G. B. I Co. will return the price.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION. WARRINGTON v. ROCHDALE HORNLTS.</p>
        <p>• The Hornets visited Warrington on Saturday to play their return match,and were witnessed by between 4,000 and 5,000, persons the number not being quite of equal dimensions to the recent home matches. It will be re- membered that at the first meeting of the teams, after Warrington having had almost the whole of the game in their favour, they had to be content wkh a thaw and a try each. It was ther6ore expected by everyone that when the return match was played Warrington would. have an easy vivory. The WarrinV.on team, however, did not play on Saturday wig' their usual dash, and though they bad the best of the play,vet they had not to the same extent as on the .first meeJng. They were, however, fortunate in scoring twice to their oppnntn s' once, anti thus won, this being their four.h succEsive home win. The day was an ideal one for football, and all the conditions were favourable to a fast game. and therefore it was disappointieg to see the teams play in such loose fashion. Warrington st:II lacked the berV.CE.i of Burton who did not feel fit to turn out in first &amp;ASS football as be still felt the efivects of his recent indisposi- tion. The only change in the Hornets' team was the sub- stitution of Melledew from Uttley. The follow_ng were the two teams : _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Warrington : Heesom, full back ; Carey, Cross,Earber, and Broady,threequarters ; Foden and Cia.rk,Hlf: barks ; Nevins, Burrows, Andrews, Thornton, Fairlaurst,Eue..t, Taylor and Swift, forwards. • Rochdale Hornets Rigg, full back ; Egerton,BiacVev, Rangeley, and Wightman, threequarters ; Kele ier and Hanson,half backs ; Gorma n,Higgin, i'l,Leacii,Tetlow. Horsefield, Trevor, and Melledew, forwards. . After the kick off, Rochdale hid the lest oF mitten, and Heesom was soon tested, but he repeled the.atc After a series of scrimmages in the home I:nes, Bur; ows and Carey by individual work,took the pl ty to the centra. Here Foden obtained possession, and dodging F:e cher and Hanson, he made snme headway, and then pa'aed to Barber, who was knocked in touch by Rangeley aa4.e- good run. For a time W arrington held the upper hand slightly, until by a huge kick sent them to the hit about and the Hornets were again very dangerous. From the scriwmage which foLowed, which took place in tile i home 25. Clark dribblei away, and sent n touc'i but Rangely again ran back, and regained all lost ground. Warrington immediately aLerwards had a very narrow escape, for from some loose play, right under the War- rington goal posts, the ball came off Broady into Lee- ton's hands. Had he made his mark, an almost ce:Lain goal would have fallen to his side, but he pre:erred to dash to the line which was only a few Yards awag, and Broady and Barber were enabled to frustrate his attempt. Things still looked ominous, until Clark dribbled the b.(11 out of danger, and by short stages, in which Thorn! on, Burrows, Swift and Nevins were very prominent in the loose dribbles, neutral ground was reached. Here Cross got possession and passing to Carey, the latter made a dashing run along the•touch lino and a score seemed cer- tain, when just as he was about to ground the ball, he was floored by Buckley. Warrington still atzacked, and Thornton followed up a kick of Fodens, tackled Egerton with the ball, and from the following scrim- mage, Fairhurst was almost over. Successive scrimmages followed near the Hornets' quarter flag, and from one of these, Foden pas‘,ed to Cross, who near the line parted to Barbe:, and the lat- ter easily romped over, and scored near the posts. The place was a very easy one, but Foden failed to increase the try to a goal. Buckley kicked out, and Foden re- turned. Barrett and Taylor were early prominent by staying a rush of the Hornets' forwards. A godd round of passing between Foden, Cross, Barber, and Broady, enabled the latter to get away, but he had the misfor- tune of being knocked in touch at the corner flag,, and had hard lines indeed in missing scoring. A line Ce. 4 took place on tne goal line, and Foden throwing far out, Nevins secured and prooptly deposited it over the go .s 1 line. Cross was deputed to kick the goal which again was in an easy position, and although he was allowed a free kick at the goal, owing to the over-eagerness of the Hornets to charge it down, he failed at the place. Ar,.a: the restart, Wightmau made a grand ran right through the thick of his opponents and had almost got home, when Barber tackled him. Buckley made his mark im- mediately afterwards, but Rangeley failed to place a goal. half time soon arrived with Warrington leadieg by two tries to all. Immediately after the restart, Cross dribbled to the line, but failed to score, and Barber had an ineffectual drop at goal. A fine round of passing was flex.-- wt- nosed between Clark, Foden and Barber, and th7s •placed Warrington on the aggressive. Rangeley had now to leave tiie field for a time ow;ng to an it,jury,and this appeared to have a dispiriting effect upon the players, for the play on bath sides deteriorated gi ea.tly, Warrington especially, and Rocticia:3 were speedily at- tacking. Scrimmage after scrimmage took place oa tie Warrington line, and the Wartineton defenders were kept hard at work. Fodenettleoath by a long kick in touch raised the seige, and Carey receiving the u.a.s from Cross,sped along the touch lioe pas ing everybody but Rig,g,but the latter tackled him just on the ILne.Ae.; .t</p>
        <p>the Hornets returned to the attack and S4IIIA Stiff mazes - mages took pl Ica on the Warrington line, and iron ore of them, Fle.cher passed to Er.ferton, whg suceeeje3 in getting over, but Rig could not get the majsr point. Broady' restarted, and after Buckley's return, Ramona mulled Ike ball and almost let his opponents in. E-;erion trled a drop at goal vvith no better result th in a minor. After a considerable spell of atiackin7, the Hornets we"a at length repelled, and successive marks were made tr 7 Broady and Burrows, from the la.tter of which Swt had an itieffectual kick at goal. Nothing; further of note took place, and the game resulted in a win for Warring- ton by two tries to one try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RUNCORN v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS</p>
        <p>The team which did duty for Runcorn, on Saturday, in this engagement, was the strongest the club have turned out this season. The match in question was the return engagement, toe first encounter ending in a draw flinch in favour of the Cestrians. The following com- posed the teams r—Runcorn : S. Houghton, full hack ; T. Warder. H. Myers, J. Butterworth, and B. West, three-quarter Lacks ; G. Robinson and J. Faulkner, half backs ; W. Faulkner, J. Langley, H. Farmer, R. Reece, S. Walker, G. Moores, A. Taylor, 0. Hughes, forwards. Brighouse : Abbey, full back ; Waddington, Caldwell, Wainwright, and Hartley, threequar.:or backs ; Schofield and Blaelrbnin, half backs ; Nicol, Jaggar, Errnshaw, Sag,den, W'hi ley, Marsden, Wood and Armitage, forwards. Woods stared al game of two 40's,and no return being effected scrimmages ensued in the home half. For a brief period play was of an even description, although it was seen that Brighouse were strongest in the scrimmages, and the ball was kicked out on several occasions, but lirrle advantage aerated from this, the home backs being too sharp, whilst Houghton put in some pnnderoos kicks which invariably found touch, and proved of immense advantage to his side. At last the home backs got going, D. Robinson,W. Faulkner and Farmer passing splendidly the latter racing over. An appeal, however, for touch negatived the point, a splendid effert thus coning to naught. More passing by the home backs followed,and unfortunately for Runcorn West had to re ire, W. Faulkner having to come out of the pack and play at threequarter. This weakened the home front rank, but they fairly bent to their work, and by eas7 stares frrced their way to the visitors' quarters, where Robiiivon get- ing possession gave to J. Faulkner, who dashed over at the corner, Farmer making a good attempt at goal. Play cantinued very exciting, and an exhibition of long kiek- ing was given by Houghton, Abbey and Warder.' Clever forward play, in which Walker,Moores and Horthes were prominent again caused pressure on the visitor's lines,- a certain try being prevented by Caldwell in tercepting and running the ball clear. Still Ron- corn were always dangerous, their quick short passing proving too much for the Tykes. Far ner again was seen in a dashing run, and had hard lines for a try, but a moment later some magnificent parsing between Robinson, J. Faulkner, Butterwortit and W. Faulkner ended is the Runcorn captain making a fine run in, Myers landing the major point. Runcorn had a lead at half time of eight points to nil. After the interval Runcorn had a good opening, but a pa.ss from Faulkner wae missed. and Nicol. Jagger and Earnshaw dribbled down, but Houghton effected a fine save. Brig- house now altered their tactics, and went in for the close scrimmaging game, and for about ten minutes a mo3t fierce attack was made on the home lines, where some exciting episodes took place, but as fierce as was the at ack so was the defence of Runcorn, a.n4 some heavy collar work was seen, Houghton, W. Faulkner, La nalev, and 0. Hughes being rno,,t prominent. The d ,oger was averted, but at this juncture the game was very vi2,or- ous, and Runcorn were penalized, from which Wooas placed a goal. Runcorn soon replied, and the ball once more getting amongst the backs, Myers gave the ball to Taylor, who completed the effort, but the kick at goal was a failure. From now up to the call of time the game was of a most desperate character. The find score was —Runcorn, one goal two tries (eleven points); Bt ighouse, one penalty goal (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>LEEDS V. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>LEEDS V. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Played at Beadinglev, before 2,000 spectators. Barber set the ball in motion for %Vidnes, and the Leeds for- wards, headed by Parfitt, were noticeable for a fin p open burst, Walton effecting a clever save. Hainstock cam.e.d the visiting custodian to kick &amp;ad, after a smart psss by Midgley. Leeds were constanly on the attack, and Bainstock experienced hard lines in being pvshed intio touch at the corner flag. From a line out Parfitt scored, which was not improved upon. A mistake by. one of the Widnes backs let in the Leeds forwards, and after a scramble over the line 'niompson scored, Glover con- verting. The visitors were kept on the defence. Walton got in a pass to Summersgill, which the latter gathered</p>
        <p>while running at top speed, and grounded the ball near the corner flag. A pretty bodt of passing Was next seen at the hands ot the Vl'idnr; threequatters, Alford being pushed into touch at half-way, the half-time score being —Leeds, one goal and two tries (11. points) ; Widnes, oil. Pickles re-.3ca, Led for Leeds, Nevitt replying to the centre. Leeds were penal;z.d for off-side play. Scrum- maging ended in favour ot Leeds, and the result of a turn of passing, in which Walton, liainstock, and Midg- ley took part, was the the first-named eluded his oppon- ents and scored a fine try, which Glover failed to convert. Within two minutes of the re-start the Leeds forwards rushed the ball to the Widnes line, and Sowrien placed the fifth try to the credit of the home s;de. f'ckles tailed at goal from a ver easy place. Smith next passed to Walton close up, and the latter gave tile ball to Hain- stpck, who scored. Greig landing a goal. Just liefore the call of time Widnes made a spurt,and Walton was forced to kick dead. Score : Leeds, two goals and four tries (22 points) ; Widnes, nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A "V. ROCHDALE HORNETS "."—The above teat.as played cn the 'erouud of the Rochdale Hor- nets on Saturday, when the following players constituted the teams :—Warrington :Pull back, Spruce; three- quarters, J. Lawless, Scott, Isherwood, and L. Bailey; hali-backs, O'Brien, and Theoinbetham ; forwards, I'. Lawless. Savage. Sanders, Hughes. Seholtza, Proud ,Hill, and E. Bailey. Rochdale Hornets : Full back, Whitwork ; three-quarters, Sitnpson, Collier, Sutcliffe, and Ishe:- wood ; half-backs, Mason and Ogden; fnrwards, Stvzo.t, Nuttall, Fletcher, Dearden, Cook, W. Leach,and J. Leach. Warrington kicked off and forced the ball to the Rochdale quarters, where, however, Isherwood got possession and got to the Warrington quarter flu; berore he was tackled. Mason next made a fine run, but hp lost the ball when tackled, and J. Lawless quickly picked up and carried it well into Rochdale territory, where in attempting ta pass, the. ball went over the line. By a smart piece of dribbling the Rochdale forwards took the ball to the Warrineton quarter, where it remained for some time. The Hornets pressed during the latter part of the half, but when the whistle blew neither side had scored. On continuing the oame, come fine running and passing was exhibited by Ogden, Collier, and Isherwood. Play for sometime was in the Hornets quarters and J. Lawless got away from a scrim- mage and scored for Warringtoti. The goal kick missed. Lawless aga.in broke away, hut was called back and soon afterwards Isherwood (Rochdale) made a good rim but did not get far, hut he frequently repeated his at- tempts to score. The home team made some strong attacks but the Warrinotonians prevented them from getting through. Collier made a dash for the visitors' eaal and on being pressed, he attempted a dron goal hitt tailed. Sutcliffe immedia el! secured tha ball and after taking a few steps,, he took a; cool and deliberate aim at the posts and drooped a neat goal, amidst the cheers ef the cpectators. Things beean to look bad for Warring- ton, but J. Lawless was on the look out for an opportun- ity which was not long in coming. From a scrimmage he took the ball along the field and althouah Wrongly opposed he managed to get over the line and thus won the game for Warrington. The kick for goal was not successful Liavles,3. made another dash for the home goal and after a ‘,OO/3 run he was only checked in time, as he got close to the line. For the last few minutes the play vYas smart and exciting, but !lathing more was scored and the whistle blew with Warrington winning by two tries (six points) to Itc,chdale Hornets a drop goal (foar points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SSOC.I ATION.</head>
