      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WAR El NGTON.</head>
        <p>WAR El NGTON.</p>
        <p>A GE:NITROUS OFF FIR BY MR. J. B. ISAAC</p>
        <p>The" Warrirl-on Weoeyan "ethe--t Magazine" says that Mr. J. B. Isaae, whose inter.ist in ii:lles- Tnere-g...eet MisQinn jc well-known, has offered to give .£5O to meet n ecinal amount from the friends, in wder to I;qnidate the debtor £lOO which remains on the building. It is intended to a. -!ent the offer, and to make the forthcoming. ann;versary very special. wilh a view to raising the stir, revired. NARROW ESCAPE FROM DROWNING.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday prternoon, whikt a boatman named Henry Orme, :34 years of age. and residing at Run- corn, was workirg at erosaeld's quay, het was accidently knocked into the river by •a crane. He was rapidly res.cued from the water blr some bystan- ders and conveyed at once ;1 the Infirmary, where he was admitted in a state bordering on collapse. Medical skill, however, soon brought him round,and he is improving quickly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>CONCERT AT DARKSBURY.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT DARKSBURY.</p>
        <p>A very successrul annual concert was given in the Board school. Daresbury, on Friday evenir.g for the purpose of rai&lt;iino; funds for a scholars' treat, the room herr, •owded to excess. The Vicar pre- sided and, br,rlre introducing the vocalists, said bow pleased he was to see again so many present, thereby showi—, thpt they took an interest ;n the we' rs-resbury school. An e7:nellent programme wag then proce6:ed with, the Following Irdies and geri;',•ngen n'oly taking parr, Whit- -1,17. Miss Ei•en7ll, *3. Owen, Miss Picot, Miss Wakefield. MPr3. Melville, Perm/ candsH A. Grounds, Foreb^w and IT. Booth. Th humorists of the evening wee Viessrs. A. Grounds and H Epoth. A hearty vote or fl,”11;:q was accorded the vocalists on the prono-i 'on of the Rev. G. W. Gilbertson,and after the sinng of she NaLionsl Anthem the room was cleared cor dancing. Mr. Percy randall supplied the music for dancing in his usual good style. LOYAL Or: DER OF ANCIENT ST-TEPHERDS, WARRINGTON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>On Saturday a special meeting of the above dic- trict was held at the Pride of Latchford F,odge. The meeting was presided over by Bro. T. C. Duck- wor ii. pro. chits of the district, who was aupnorted by D.P C.s. Ti"o. Wifljam Eckereley, district treasurer; Bap. James Parr, distriet secretary ; Bro. P. Edger, and 20 other delegates, representing the interests of the ten lodges. The meeting was held for the purnose oF nresent 'lig the annual statement of accounts of lodges and district. The above accounts were presented. to the district by the secretary, and rrom them it was gleaned that, on the whole, the year 1895 has been satisfactory. The Fends of the district and lodge:4 now amount to about E. 3,677. After Paying all disbursements there was a gain of over £76. During the year there had ben admitted in the lodises 85. There had been 2.2 deaths, v'z , eleven members and eleven wives and widows. The nre•ent number of the district stood at OTT. th,, examiners; (Messrs. Weir and Harris) had elven in their report,which was of a flattering nature, comnlimenting the several secretaries mann the manner in which their respec- tive lodge books were kept, the auditors' reports were, after a short discussion, passed. Brothers Haley, Weir, Parris, Taylor, Lowe. and ethers tciok part in the discussion. The usual votes of thanks terminated a pleasant evening. THE DROWNING CASE AT WARRINGTIN%—rSQUEST. In the parade-room at the Police Station,on Satur- day afternoon, Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest on the body of William Mason, who was found in the river Mersey on the previous Wednes- day night. Mr. -vans watched the proceedings on behalf of the Manchester Ship Canal Company, while Mr. Forshaw represented the relatives.—John Mason said deceased was his father, and was 72 years of age. He was a storekeeper in the employ of the Manchester Ship Canal Company, and lived at Howley Cottage. About seven o'clock on Wednes- day night deceased left home with the intention of visiting his daughter in Navigation-st. That was the last time witness saw him alive. He (witness) was a night watchman, and about half-past ten, a dog which he had with him commenced to bark. This aroused his suspicions, and on proceeding to the edge of the river, he saw a young man en- deavouring to take a body from the water. Witness ran to his assis'ance, and was very much shocked when he discovered that it was his own father. ffe was not quite dead when witness arrived on the scene, but expired shortly afterwards. Dr. Fishy had been sent for. Deceased was troubled with " mazy bouts," and when in one of these, he We unconscious of all his surroundings. The night in question was also very foggy,and he (witness) con- jectured that deceased had aceidentally walked into the water,—r. Elizabeth Garnett stated that the deceased. who was her father, visited her house on the night in question, and left about eight o'clock in his usual health.—Questioned by the Coroner, Inspector Cox stated that no trace of the deceased had been found from the time he left his daughter's house milli t:vi time of the sad catastrophe.— Samuel Walker, Howley Quay, said that on Wed- nesday night, abaut half-past ten, he was walking along the river bank when he heard a splash. He ran for a clothes-nron,but on looking round he saw the bsdy floating down the river. He ran and jeroped on a flat, where be procured a boat-hook. 'the body was then recovered. —A verdict, of " Acci- dental death " Was returned, and a vote of ziondol- ence was passed with the relatives, en the motion of the foreman of the jury. Mr..P'orshaw, on behalf of the relatives, briefly thanked the jury, for the sympathy which had been expressed by them;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MAN SCALDED.</head>
        <p>A MAN SCALDED.</p>
        <p>A man named Edward Matthews, aged 02 years, living at 19, Dolman's-lane,was on Sunday admitted to the Warrington infirmary, suffering from very severe scalds about his back and neck, caused by the upsetting of a kettle of boiling water.</p>
