        <p>ALLEGED CONCEALMENT OF BIRTH AT STRETTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. l. Worsley, coroner, held an inquest at the "Cat and Lion," Stretton, on Tuesday, on the body of a child, which was found under mos.; mysterious circumstances on Sunday, lying in a closet at Stretton. Superintendent Okell, of lz,uncorn, watched the proceedings on behalf of the police. Sarah Ann Renshaw, of Spark Hall, Stretton, said that the mother of the child, Ellen Renshaw, was her sister-in-law, and lived with her at the above residence. On Sunday afternoon,about four o'clock, she complained of feeling unwell. Witness ordered her to bed, and called in the assistance of a woman named Blease. She (witness) had no idea that her sister-in-law had given birth to a child. Dr. Bower, of Appleton, stated that he had known the mother about thirteen mouths. Ile was called to the house on Sunday and examined the body, which had been found lying on the floor of the closet. It; was that of a fully-developed female child. fie saw the mother, who was in bed, and afterwards communi- cated with the police. That morning De. Stackey Smith and himself had made a post-mortem exam- ination, and had found that the child had breathed. The right side of the heart was full of blood, but the left side was empty. The stomach was also empty. From these -facts he could not, hovi, ver, swear that the child had had an independent ex- istence, hut from its appearance he was of opinion that if it had had proper attention at birth it would have lived. Annie Blease, of Wilderspool, said she saw the mother of the child on Sunday, and then felt a little suspicious after the latter told her that she had Only had a slight headache. Witnes ex- amined the premises,and underneath the seat in the pit of the closet she found the body, which was then quite cold. She was of opinion that the woman had given birth to the child in that place. Witness then spoke to the mother about the affair, and she replied, " 1- could not help it." The Coroner remarked that had the medical men been able to swear that the child had had a separate existence that inquiry would have assumed a very serious as- pect. As it was there was no other verdict to re- turn than that the child died on the previous Sun- day, but there was no evidence of an independent existence. This verdict was accordingly returned.</p>
        <p>OIL.—GARLAND. Importer, 51. Bridge-street,</p>
        <p>Warr:ngton. b WING MACHINES, Hand £2 18s. 6d. Irealle £4 4s. yin ,ndsome walnut cover.—J. and W. DUTTON 16 and 13, San ke v-et. , Warrington.—Advt. Housv Hon) LINENS, hemmed and marked free of chirge. J. and W. Dutton, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURC El ANNUAL TEA MIIBETING.</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting in connection with the above church was held at the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Monday, when there were about 400 persons present. After tea the room was ar- ranged for the entertainment, which was presided over by Alderman Sutton J.P., and amongst those present we noticed the Rev. H. V. and Mrs. Pigot, the Rev. R. Brierly (curate), Mr. Chantry (people's warden), Captain Chorley,and Messrs. Pigot, Joseph Lawless, A. J. Jolley, James Henry Smethurst, Bough, Bebbington, Braide, and H. Hoult. After some introductory remarks by the Chairman, the entertainment was proceeded with, and proved to be of a very att-active character. The accompanist was the organist of:the church,Mr.J.J.Lythgoe. The first item was a quartet, " aleep Lady, Sleep," which was very tastefully sung by Messrs. ..;cattergood, Wilkes, Ratcliffe and Pownall,whose voices blended in a very harmonious manner. Mr. J. Li. Sherlock, an old favourite of the parish, and a member of the choir, followed with an excellent rendering of "The Wandering Minstrel?' Miss H. Brough was awarded an encore for her singing of "Lock Lomond," to which ehe responded by repeating the last verse,and Messrs. Scattergood, Ratcliffe and Pownall were heard to advantage in the amusing trio, "Jack and Jill." The last verse was repeated in answer to a recall. Miss Brough (in the absence of Miss Law- less) contributed "Robin Adair," and Mr. Wood favoured the audience with " My Sweetheart when a Boy." Miss Brough was heals' for the third time in" The Harbour Bay," a very pretty song,of which 'an excellent interpretation was given. Another equally effective trio, "Little 80-Peep," by Messrs. Scattergood, Ratcliffe, and Pownall, concluded the first part of the programme. The Vicar, during the interval, delivered a short address, saying that theirs was a happy family at St. Paul's, but if they were not united it was not his fault, because it had always been his endeavour to do what he could to promote unity, happiness, and brotherly love.—Mr.Chantry made a financial state- ment, observing that they had spent a great deal of money during the past year, probably more than they ought to have done, but he hoped that when the Easter Vestry meeting was held they would not be much behind the position of last year. The im- provements during the year included lights for the pulpit,and organ,and new curtains for the organist to screen him from the draught. The second part of the entertainment was then commenced,and con- sisted of a series of tableaux,in which the Japanese characters were sustained by the following ladies : —Misses Geddes, 1,. Reid, Pickton, Ward, Annie Smethurst, Minnie Smethurst, Ada Smethurst,Janie Finney.and Ethel Finney. Mrs.G. Braide and Miss Pigot officiated as the vocal soloists,and the charac- ter of "Rule Britannia" was portrayed by Miss Rathbone. Great credit is due to the Misses Geddes, under whose direction this part of the entertain- ment had been prepared. Captain Chorley was in charge of the lantern, and Mr. Brough played the pianoforte accompaniments. The dresses were very pretty. Tne first item was a "song and dance from Japan " (encored) followed by a "military review" in which the performers were a number of young men attired in appropriate costumes. The song and , chorus, "Tommy Atkins, was so well rendered that an encore was the result. The "Family Group from Japan" was an excellent pose. The naval man mu- vres were very amusing, appropriate costumes be- ing again introduced. The last item was entitled " fall° Britannia." and " God save the Queen" hav- ing been sung, a very enjoyable entertainment was brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>GENEI!AL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENEI!AL NEWS.</p>
        <p>At Birmingham on Wednesday, Samuel Horatio Rod- way, strip caster, was committed for trial on a charge of committing a shocking outrage on his wife by stab- bing her in the abdomen with a carving knife. The Paris" Figaro " states that Mr. and Mrs. Glad- stone will leave Biarritz for Cannes on the 10th inst. Mr. Gladstone is. stated to be in excellent health, and daily occupied with literary work. George Blighton, who shot and nearly killed a young woman with whom he had lived at West Ham, was con- victed on Wednesday of the attempt to murder, and sentence to twelve years' penal servitute was passed. Roman Catholic circles at Burnley were on Wednesday much surprised by the announcement that Lady O'Elagan, of Townelay Ball, whose family had for cen- turies been identified with the Catholic religion had joined the committee of the Kentish Town Unitarian Church, London.</p>
        <p>The new Poet Laureate has been caught tripping again. Be has declared that King Alfred, "the greatest of Englishmen." has" never been celebrated by an English poet." But a correspondent writes to say that "a year or two hack a friend of mine dis- covered on a book stall in Whitechapel a small volume, containing a fairly lengthy poem, entitled Alfred,' written by a former Poet Laureate—by name Pye—and which volume is now in possession of .a descendant of that poet." Mr. Austin will therefore have the felicity of shining with Pye—and with about equal lustre. He will certainly never "err with Pope." There is reason to believe that a further honour is in store for Mr. Alfred Austin,says a correspondent. It may not come just at present, but in certain circles it is quite understood that the Poet ['Aureate will, in due course, have a handle to his name. Probably this mark of favour will be reserved for a period when his services have .been called into requisition on an occasion of particular interest to the Royal Family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
