      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Authority was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr. William Heughton presiding. There were also present,Mrs. Clay,the Revs. J.H.Riddette,T. R. Matthews,and Father Moores,Messrs. S. Davies, /. J. Jolley, G. Thompson, Wl' Hickman J. Clarke,J. i Billinge G. Pilling,J. Warburton, A. Jolley:W. Cress, T. B. Malkin, A. Lang, J. Ball, Dr. Patterson, J. W. Knowles. T. Halton, J. Houghton, J. Dignum, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutten (deputy clerk), C. H. Ward (mastbr), and H. M. Mines (superintenient).</p>
        <p>PROPOSED NON-CONFORMIST SERVICES IN THE :WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>The House Committee's minutes contained a letter: from the Rev. G. D. Mason asking for permission to hold Non-conformist services in the workhouse, and it was recommended that three months trial be allowed, and that Non-conformist services be held once a week at such times as would not interfere with the vsual work of the House.—The Clerk said that he had received letter from Mr. Mason that morning as follows : "Dear Sir, I have the pleasure of writing to you re religious services at the Yb orkhouse and receive your courteons reply. As to our Free Church Council meets on Tuesday week, I shall be g!ad if you give me any further reply to the letter before eoreyours trulv,G. D. Mason" :—Mr. Knosvles ; What Sienomime ion is that ?—The Clerk: The letter says " free church."—Mr. Krtowlee : Is it Scotch Free Chutch or what ?—Mr. A. Jolley seconded the pass- ing of the nainutee.—Mr. Knowles said that he did not exactly see what free church meant. He could not understand the appellation of the term, but as he took it at the present time, they ii; 1 0..1y two religious services In the Workhouse, one church, and the other Catholic. Altlioueh he felt that everyone snould have their own method of worship—lie agreed with that—he did not think teat they should deviate from an old custom which hid worked very well for a great many years,and open one day a week to the services of a dissenting body. They would thus render themselves liable to applications from every form of religion which held itself entitled to have its own particular doctrine preached in the workhouse, whether it be Quakers, Jews, or whatever else. He thought that deviating from the old Cir..COM Was a ds neereus preeedent to adopt, and he felt strongly inclined to oppose it simply on those grounds. They would `'.Jon he lieving so many religious services in the Wo.khorise " that it would be turned into so many different chapels." leey would be having services in the House from the Monday morning to Monday morning. Any minister could see any member of his flock in the house, and he would therefore moved that the minutes of the House Committee be p-ssecl with the exception of the one relating to the Free Church services.—The Rev. T. R. MATTHEWS : Can anyone tell us to what Free Church the recommendation refers P—The CHAIRMAN They describe it ee ening to carry out a Nonconformist service. —Mr. PILLING seconded Mr. Knowles' amend- ment to test the feeling of the Board.—Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said that he hoped the members of the Board would not vote in favour of the amendment. The resolution was adopted at the last meeting of the House Committee without a dissentient voice. The Free Church Council was a body which embraced all, or nearly all local Pro- testant Nonconformist &amp;tidies. There were a few people in the Workhouse who were Nonconformists, and the Free Church Council had offered voluntarily to conduct a service once a week, without expense to the Guardians in any way or shape, for the tenefit of their fellow-Non- conformists who happened to be in that place. Alter discussing the question from the different points of view, the committee had come to the conclusion that it was desirable to give the application arial.He hoped that Mr. Knowles would withdraw his amendment, as the aprilic- ants were not irresponsible persons,but werela body °fires- nonsible minimrs of religion.—The CLERK said that the matter was one entirely of having confidence in Mr. Mason. with regard to the descretion he would exer- cise in regard to the services. It had happened that at intervals indiscreet religious matter had been introduced to the poor people who were in the hospital, and had done injury,in the judgment of the medical men. But lie thought that they would have no indiscretion from the body which was represented by Mr.Mason.—Mr. HICK- MAN said that perhaps a little explanation would induce Mr. Knowles to withdraw the amendment. Mr. Mason was a Wesleyan minister, and secretary of the Free Church Council, which had been in existence for three or four years and embraced the ministers and members of the Wesleyan, Baptist, Presbcterian,Primitive Metho- dist, Independent Methodists and, he thought, the Uni- tarian denotninations.The Board would,therefore.clearly understand that they were all responsible persons, and were desirous of holding services in the Workhouse, not on Sunday, because that was already occupied by the Church of England and Church of Rome, but some time in the week, for about three-quarters of and hour or an hour. They would conduct the services free of cost to the Union. He hoped that Mr. Knowles would withdraw his amendment.—Mr.DeviEs said that he was present at the committee meeting on Friday, and he then gave a general support to the application. They were only giv- ing it a three months' trial, and they could revise their decision in three months' titne.—Mr. KNOWLES at this point withdrew his amendment, and the minutes of the 'House Committee were passed.</p>
        <p>THE ILLNESS OF løE-MATRON.</p>
        <p>letter was read from the Matron, stating that she was better now, and would te able again to resume her work.</p>
        <p>SCHOOLS COMMITTEE. -A PERTINENT INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>This committee recommended that for the position of assistant mistress, Miss Mary Ann Goodley, of Warring- ton, Miss E. M. Hassall, of Fearnborough, and Miss Harrison.of Bath, be invited to attend at the next com- mittee meeting,the Board to pay all railway expenses.— Mr. DAVIES mbved,and Mr. J. HOUGHTON seconded, the adoption of the minutes.—The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE asked whether the matters referred to at the last meet- ing of the Boa-d were receiving the attention of the committee.—Mr. DAVIES said that at the meeting of the committee on the previous davithev had made a begin- ning and he was all aid that they would have to proceed further. He did not like to say anything farther at pre- sent.—The mint.tes were passed.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSS'S MOTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. CRO:.:S moved a resolution of which he had given notice, that in future, in all repairs to the workhouse or schools, it should be an instruction that the matters be referrei to the Board and Repairs Committee unless the repairs were ordered by the various committes. The Rev. J. H.RIDDETTE said that he thought that the committee ought to be enlarged,if those powers were added to it.— The CHAIRMAN : Irs a couple of months all committees will be re-formed, and it would be well to wait until then.—Mr. LANG seconded the motion,and said it would be to the advantage of the master and superintendent that they should have one or two men to depend upon to take advice in case of emergency for repairs.—Mr. A. J. JOLLEY raid that he had understood that the Building and Repairs Committee was to he a consultative committee. and if the resolution of Mr. Cnoss was passed, it wc uld take out of the hands of the House and School Commit- i the a large portion of their work. Therefore, without ap- pearing in any way hostile to the motion,he moved that the matter stand over until the annual meeting.—Mr. KNO %, LES seconded the amendment.—Mr. Cnoss said that he had no obj( ction to the committee being en- larged but be thought that it would be to the advantage of the Board that it should be composed of practical men. —Mr. LANG said that there was a clause in the resolution which provided that it did not clash with the other cora- mittees.—Mr. B'CHMAN was of opinion that the matter should lie over until the annual meeting and said be would support the amendment.—Thc amendment was carried by a large majority, and the motion was accord- ingly lost. ALLEGED VISIT OF AMATEUR CASUALS TO !LE wommorss.</p>
        <p>Mr. Biturrou asked Mr. Ward if it was true that a short time ago some men come to the Workhouse repre- senting themselves as vagrants.—Mr. WARD said he did not know.—Mr. KWOWLES : They should be put to an extra amount of stone breaking. (Laughter.)—Mr. BILLINGIE said that he had heard that some persons had visited the place. Mr. WARD: I believe now that Cnrry did say something to me about several gentlemen coming into the werd.—Mr. EILLINOE : It was intimated to me that they saw the bath, the labour, and everything.— Mr. KNOWLES : They shculd have had hot water in their bath.</p>
        <p>A " BREEZE."</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. H. RiDDETTE asked whether the report of the Ladies' Committee had been received.—Tbe CLERIC said that it had, and the suggestions were incorporated in the minutes of the Schools Committee.—Mr.RlDDETTE: Will you read the minutes of the Ladies' CommAtee, please.—Mr. KNOWLES : I don't think that that is neces- sary. I don't want my time wasted.—Mr. RIDDETTE again asked for the minutes to he read.—Mr. KNOWLES : I hope you will do nothing of the sort. lam not going to atop here all morning.—Mr. RIDDETTE : I am only asking what I have a right to do. I will not be insulted by Mr. Knowles.—Mr. KNOWLES: I am not insulting you. but I am not going to have my time wasted.—Mr. RIDDETTE : I ask for the minutes to be read.—Mr. KNOWLES I object to them.—Mr. RIDDETTE : Was not the Ladies' Committee appointed by this Board P—Mr. KNOWLES: I know it was.—Mr. RIDDETTE : It is only right then that its minutes should be read.—The minutes of the committee were then handed to Mr. itiddette to read, and tbe incident cloAed.</p>
        <p>E INFIRMARY. • The CLERIE read the replies he preposed to send to the Local Government Board with regard to the proposed new Infirmary and the Board agreed to them. The Clerk then read an exhaustive account of the negotia- tims for the erection of a new Infirmary.</p>
        <p>EONA-FIDR WORKING MEN. Mr. MALKIN again referred to the question of working men who applied for lodging in the casual ward, and denied that he had raised it in the interests of trades unions. He had simply asked the question so as to call attention to the desirability of distinguishing between bona-fide workingmen and tramps. The trade unions already provided for the:r members when looking about the country for work.</p>
        <p>The Boaid then rose. --,so.—</p>
