        <p>"SONGS AND SONG WItLT, RS."</p>
        <p>INTERESTING LECTURE BY MR. F. H. CROSSLEY. t Mr. F. H. Crossley, the conductor of the Runcorn / Musical Society. read a paper on Monday evening in the Bethesda I.,cture Hall, Runcorn, before a good audience, The proceedings were rendered additionally interestinif by several musical illustrations, the vocalists being Miss Florence Hiller (Manchester), Mr. W. H. Wallington (Warrington), and the lecturer. In the unavoidable absence of the president of tke Runcorn Musical Society (Mr. J. Bruncirit), the secretary, Mr. R. W. Pierpoint,. briefly introduced the lecturer. Mr. Crossley remarhed that if they wished to trace the origin of songs and singing. they would have to go a very long way hack, for it was probable that singing was as olrl as speecii itself. In the earliest ages singing' had its place, either as an individual solace or as a con- • venient means of expressing a common sentiment, either in war cries or in addresses to the deities of idols. These war cries subsequently oecame war songs, and the ad- dresses to the idols developed into chants and hymn. The spread of Christianity requivd that Church music should be purified and put into something like forrn,and it was tolerably certain that they were indebted to the gradual development of Chureh music for laying the foundation of musical art, especially so far as singing' was concerned. This work was commenced by St. Am- brose in the latter part of the fourth century, and was continued and amplified two centuries later by St. Gregory. On the whole it seemed rather strange that the wonderful development of modern times was so long in being brought about. Even the Greeks, with all their lore for and appreciation of the beautifal, and their' power of its reproduction, could not he supposed to have gone far in the cultivation of music. • Most of their modes were unsatisfactory to modern ears, and were not in harmony with cultivated nature. The origin of the modern song might be traced to the time of the troubse- dours, minnesingers and meistersingers. The trouba- dours first appeared about the end of the eleventh cen- tury in the southern province of France, and they in. vented their own verses as well as music, hat it was not long before the function of poet and composer come to be separately recognised. As might have been expected from their routhern temperament and the customs of chivalrous time, their effusions were principally love songs. At this point the lecturer gavel; vOcal illustra- tion of a troubadour's song, which he stated was 1.9X/i years old. Continuing his lecture, he said while the troubadours sang most exquisitely of love. and gallantry, the minnesinger* constantly iv • - I troduced into their songs praises of the --varlet(' beauties of nature. He proceeded to speak of the life and works of such well-known song writers OP Purcell, Schubert, Mendelssohn, Schumenn, Brehm . Grieg, and Sullivan. and examples of their work were sung by the vocalists. In referring to Mendelssohn'e work, the lecturer made a passing reference to 1)4 Elijah whieh is to he performed by the members of the society next month. Mr. Cross4!y expressed his bee lief that a really good song must find an echo in the. heart, and no amount of technical study merely on ate part of a composer sufficed to give the truth and abs, lutely correct tone of expreslsion. It was necessary ti words should possess a completeness of form, and that they should be written in an easy flowing metre, and a pleasant striking, rhythm. As a rule,the narat musically - flowing verses would make the most effective song. n was almost desirable that the verses should not be over- crowded with ideas ; in fact, a poem with only one central idea, such as the stillness of night, or the beauty of snrine, was preferable to the set of verses containjrl. a little history. Some descriptive songs 'Nue successfui• but the frequent change of sentiment in the words, if followed literally in the music, caused a feeling of IT s • lessness, and often gave to a song a sort of patchy chat- acter. Italian was by far the most vocal of all the lan- guages ; it was so full of vowel and c.pen sounds. French and German had advantaees over our own language,. though they were not se easy to sing as Italian. . They had in England, on the contrary, had to study hard te • fore they could arrive at the Italian's starting point. An there was the secret of the reason that so many of theif. sineers failed to make their words understood. Alluding to the present-day singers, Mr. Crossley observed that a number of those who sought public favour as singers wet enormous, and they might readily imagine that these made up only a small percentage of those who professed' to know how to sing. There was a certain glamorr , about the life of a profess'on-d singer which sotnetim induced people to join the vocal ranks whe would be. better emnloved in other ways. (Lauehter.) Sever amusing incidents were recorded, and the speaker com- mented upon the very little attention paid by ordinary folks to the evidences of skill and careful study in sin- gers and players. The public and many press critical were led away by noise,clash and brilliancy. The singer'S style might reek of the music hall and his pronnncia. tion be anything but that set down by Dr. Johnson and, other authorities, but if he would shout the regulation ballads. or" dash " the famous " potter's vessel " (front the" mewah ") in pieces with a final howl on a high A. (which. by the way, Handel never wrote), the public applauded and the press wrote the s'nger up. (Laughter and applause.) But good taste, cultivation of voice,and conscientious study of the music to be performed won slight appreciation, except at the bands of a select for who maintained, and rightly, that it 'was foolish to at - cept a singer for the sake of a good voice. (Hear, bear.)' In conclusion. Mr. Crossley came to 'the consideration of the modern song in our own country, remarking that. the average standard was at present very low indeed, , although he did not wish to infer that all-the songs pub. lishPd within the last few years were bad, but for even, really good song brought out there were probably 50' h singing. That might the fact that they had not maul partly edacwghoiucnhtewdefroernboyt musicians capable of composing genuinely aitistic songs. though there were certainly some, en that that in itself was not a sufficient reason for the enormous productioh of the present style of modern ballads. Demand creata supply, and if there were a demand for a higher class a song he had no doubt that plenty of artistic songs would' be forthcoming. The fault, therefore, lay with the pu' ' lie. The public were apparently sat:sfied with rillyaer timental words and utterly cemmonplare music, al- though there was much consolation to be derived frora the evident love and appreciation by the masses of our old national conga. Here they had music which, while' being simple, was of the right sort, and so long as these songs retainod their popularity it was, perhaps, not toe' much to hope that before long there might be a diffused liking for the true art song. (Applauge'Y The programme of illustrations was as follows and WO Hiller and Mr. Wallington are te he ccneratulated twoe their excellent interpretation of thcm r—Purcell (1658-"" 1695), "The Captice Lover" ; Schubert (1767-1828)t "The Wanderer," " Hark, hark, the Lark:, ci rion Roses"; Mendelssohn (1609-1847), "On Wir"l °.t Song " ; Schumann (181.0-1636), " Inh erolle nicht (" I will rot grieve,") " Widmueg." (" Devotign.c4 Brahms (1833), " Sapphie " ; (Meer (1843). 1.4 liebe dich," ei I love thee ") : Sullivan (1842), " WO" I were a king,"" o:pion's with his Lute." ~1 At the conclusion, a vote of thanks was accord.- , lecturer and artistes on the' motion of Mr. W. Tirrn t i"4 seconded by Mr. Joseph Littler. Mr. Crossley return' thanks, and the meetin9 terminated.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA Ts n gentle stimulant ;it does not irritate the nerves, 00 oothing, refreshing, rouris igorating, and rarely° sg to the day.—Advt.</p>
