      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this committee was held at the Work- house on Friday, Mr. William Houghtoa presiding. There were also present, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, S. Davies, J. Houghton, G. Pilling, J. Billinge, W. Hickman, A. Jolley, Joshua Ball, J.C.Sutton (clerk),W.Owen (valuer), and J. L. Tunstall (assistant overseer). A large number of appeals were heard but none of them were of public interest. Mr.. OWBN reported that since the last meeting he bad made the tollowing valuations :—Manchester Ship Canal Company, Bridgewater canals undertaking, Howley,, an additional floor in the large warehouse, gross £32 103., rateable, £2B. The Hope Foundry Company Limited, Dallam-lane, gross £2OO 103., rateable, £l6B. In this case, seeing that the works are only just getting into operation and cannot be said to be in full use,he thought that for the first six months, the assessment should be gross £134, rateable, £ll2. Warrington Corporation, the old barracks, Wilson Patten-st.yuntenanted positions, building and cells next to the gateway, exclusive of plumber's shop, gross £3O, rateable, £25 103. ; yard and I sheds, gross £25, rateable, £2l 103.; unoccupied portion of the large central building, gross £4O, rateable.£34. In the township of Havdock, there was a branch length of railway of the South Lancashire Railway, from Messrs. Richard Evans and Company's collieries, on to the new railway, the colliery was 1,400 yards and an average width of 11i yards. It was not vet on a complete state. When it was complete, he would report the value to be put upon it—The report was adopted. In reply to Mr. A. J. Jolley, Mr. Owen said that the assessment of tele- phone wires in this district was 7s. 6d. per wire per mile.. Mr. Tunstall recommended for valuation the fol- lowing premises :—Messts. Skelton's new shop ; Messrs. J. and W. Dutton's new shop in Castle-street ; Messrs. Guest, tanners; Flesketh and Dutton's shop, Sankey-st.; and Messrs. Wareing, tanners.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS ASSAULT ON A BOY: SEVERE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>PENALTY.</head>
        <p>PENALTY.</p>
        <p>Before the magistrates at the Warrington Borough Court, on Friday, William Alcock, River-road, a youth of about 18, was charged with assaulting Thomas Jack- son M 029, son of Mr. Samuel Moss, draper, Stockton Heath, on the 15th Oct. There was a cross-summons charging Moss with assaulting Alcock.—Mr. F. R. Roberts appeared for the complainant, and said he thought when the Bench heard the evidence of the pro- secution,they would consider it was a dastardly outrage. The complainant was a school boy and attended Mr. Johnson's School, Froghall-lane, and was 14 years of age. On the 15th Oct. he was returning from school about 5 o'clock in company with a school friend named Pickles, , and when passing Arpley Station, he stopped to watch through the railings an engine shunting. While he was ' looking through the rails he heard Pickles shout" Look out Moss" ! but before he could turn round he was seized by the back of the neck by the defendant who raised his fist in a threatening manner. Moss asked him not to hit him, but defendant struck him and knocked his head against the rails with great force. So violent was the blow that complainant's collar and shirt were saturated with blood and there was also some on the ground. He was so faint that he could not move for half an hour, and fur- thermore his eye was closed by defendant's blow. He was unable to go to school for 10 days and had to remain in bed 24 hours. The boys knew defendant and discovered where he was employed. Since the assault the defendant had used disgraceful language to the boy again. If the bench were satisfied with the proof hi Mr. Roberts) would ask them to inflict a severe pen- alty. The cross-summons was absurd, and was issued merely in order to enable defendant to be sworn. Robert Jackson Moss, 14 years of age, gave corrobora- tive testimony. When defendant seized hold of him he asked him what he had been throwing stones for. He denied doing this, and defendant immediately struck him and knocked his head against the rails. The next thing he remembered was leaning against the rails with Pickles holding him up. He had not seen defendant before he struck him and had never thrown stones at him or given him any provocation whatever. Edwin Pickles, school-fellow with Moss, who was with him at the time of the assault, supported the previous statements. He denied that either of them threw stones. Mr. S. Moss, father of complainant, stated the condi- tion his son was in on reaching home and his subsequent illness. He had seen defendant about the matter and he excused himself by saying that complainant had thrown at bim. Alcock said he was passing the boys on the day in question when they threw some gravel in his face. He had to go home in consequence and change his collar. Mr. Roberts here suggested that it was a case of mis- taken identity, and that if anyone threw it was not complainant. Even if complainant had thrown it did not justify such a violent assault. The magistrates said it was a very serious assault as defendant might have 'killed Moss. They fined him 103. and costs and allowed Mr. Roberts an advocate's fee of , 10s. 6d. Defendant had to pay £1 13s. 6d. in all.</p>
        <p>SALVATION FOR ASTHMATICS. A PASTOR CURING HIS FLOCK.</p>
        <p>That pastor is a pastor indeed who looks to the cure of bodies as well as souls. The Rev. ALFRED JONES, Shawlands,Glasgow.like thousands more, has discovered the blessings of Schiffmann's Asthma Cure. Be says : " I am pleased to say that in two or three instances in which your Asthma Cure was used among my friends,it gave almost instantaneous relief." We hope he will forgive this extract from the letter accompanying the above in which he says—" Where I came across, in my pastoral work, cases of suffering I shall be pleased to make your Asthma Cure known in a practical way." sufferer, JAMES HIGGINS,33, Doon Street, Cornwall-road, Lambeth, S.E.,writes —" I have been a sufferer from bronchial asthma these eight years and have not been able to work for this last twenty-one weeks, but having tried one of your packets of Schiffmann's Asthma Cure, after only using it four times I can say that it has done me more good than any medicine I have had from any doctor. I have tried four doctors and been to three different hospitals for months at a time." Space pre- vents publishing further testimonials but readers are at liberty to communicate with the above if they doubt the genuineness of these statements. In any case they are urged to send post card to Dr. R. SCRIFFMANN'S, British Depot, 32a, Snow Hill, London, 8.C., for a moat convincing list of testimonials. Schiffmann's Asthma Cure is sold by all Chemists at 2s. 61. and 4s. 61. per package, or can be obtained at the Depot as above.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>DRAMATIC PERFORMANCE AT LATCH</head>
        <p>DRAMATIC PERFORMANCE AT LATCH</p>
        <p>FORD.</p>
        <p>On Thursday last week the Lymm Amateur Drama- tic Society gave their services in producing at the Parish Room,Latchford,the pieces" Cousin Zachary," and "The Duchess of Bayswater and Co."An orches- tra composed of Messrs. A. Grounds, E. H. Clare, W. Brown, T. T. Gregson, R. Grounds, and J. Grounds, gave a selectioa of music during the evening and enlivened the proceedings in the interval. In "Cousin Zachary," the first piece given,the charac- ters were taken by Mrs. Ernest Crosfield, as Maggie Marsden; Mr. Ernest Atkin,as Zachary Binks ; and Mr. Tom Hill, as Claude (a young artist). "Cousin Zachary "is a combination of sentiment and fun which has its merits, but it can hardly be called a piece likely to achieve pronounced success. The title role is, of course, pre-eminent, and to this part Mr. Atkin was eminently suited. His interpreta- tion of the drunken scene was most natural, and merited the approval with which it met. A simula- tion of insobriety is always difficult, but in this in- stance the difficulties were surmounted and a realis- tic piece of play was the result. Mrs. Crosfield per- formed very creditably, considering the dearth of attraction which there is in the character of Maggie Marsden, while Mr. Tom Hill, though labouring under the same disadvantage, was a fair success. After a lengthy interval the second half of the pro- gramme was commenced. The characters were as follows ;--Sir Jeremy Joles (a valetudinarian) Mr. Atkin; the Duke of Bayswater, Mr. Tom Hill; Caryl Stubbs (a young millionaire, a son of Stubbs of tinned meat fame), Mr. William Nelson Jenkyns (Sir Jeremy's attendant), Mr. Arthur Nelson; the Duchess of Bayswater (mother to the duke) Mrs. Ridgway; Kathleen (Sir Jeremy's daughter), Mrs. Ernest Crosfield. "The Duchess of Bayswater and Co." is devoted entirely to humour; the Duke and Duchess are the Company and are dealers in all manner of goods from perk pies to sanitary cloth- ing, and they " push " their wares at every oppor- tunity, in public and private. Sir Jeremy, one of the old school, had previous to the advent of the Com- pany strongly objected to his daughter's engage- ment Stubbs, owing to the latter being the son of a man who made his money in tinned meat—Sir Jeremy's pet aversion. As examples of the aristo- cracy the Duke and Duchess so horrify him that he eventually with a fear of the Duke securing Kath- leen. chooses the lesser evil and accepts young Stubbs as a son-in-law. The honours of the piece were shared between Mrs. Crosfield and Mr. Atkin, both of whom acted their characters to perfection, though Mr. Tom dill and Mrs. Ridgway were also, itt should be said, faultless in their roles of mercan- il.e aristocrats. Mr. William Nelson had a less pro- mienrent part, but nevertheless he made an excellent chant prince. The minor part of the piece was that of Jenkyns, who was creditably personated by Mr. Arthur Nelson. The proceedings are to be de- voted to the Parish Room funds.</p>
      </div>
