      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>WARRINGTON FIRE BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>AN EXPERT'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The'Special Committee which was appointed by Warrington Town Council in June last "to consider the gross incapability of the Fire Brigade as at present constituted, and to recommend steps neces- sary either by the appointment of a permanent staff or otherwise to prevent a repetition of the lament- able display exhibited on the occasion of the late fire resulting in the complete destruction of Jubilee Buildings, Ilorsemarket-street," has at length pre- sented its report to the General Purposes Commit- tee, and it will be submitted to the Town Council on Tuesday next. The Special Committee have secured an independent report from Mr. F. Bailey, the superintendent of the Stafford Fire Brigade, who visited the town on September 23rd and 24th. In the course of this report, which is a rather lengthy document, Mr. Bailey says the town is well sup- plied with water for domestic use from two distinct sources, but the pressure in some parts is inadequate for fire extinguishing purposes. The pressure in the mains does not exceed 401bs. in the lower part of the town, and is as low as 20lbs. in the higher parts, which is insuffi- cient to cope with an outbreak of fire. This deficiency entails the brigaJe laying down three and even four lines of hose from different hydrants to supply the small steamer, and five or six lines of hose from different hy- drants to keep the larger one su.pplied. ; this means from 15 to 20 75ft. lengths of hose being laid dewn before the engines can commence to play upon the flames. The hydrants are much too far apart, thus reducing the pres- sure of water and materially hampering your Brigade. The hydrants being of three distinctly different kind. is, to his mind, most confusing; the hydrants throughout the town should be of one uniform patterns The present hydrants are much neglected, being kept anything but clean ; some of them are entirely. covered with corrosion and dirtirendering them unfit for prompt use. The hydrant plates, having two different supplies of water, should be distinctly different from each other, so that the Brigade may, in cate of either one or the other supply being shut off, know which is available. They are much too small, and are fixed too high to be seen after dusk, and many are without plates entirely. Mr. Bailey also reports that in some of the principal streets, for about 300 yards in each, are supplied with Win wick water only; and that in case this should be shut Dff the locality would be practically without water. In conclusion,he says that to bring the fire extinguishing and life-saving appliances in the town to a state of effi- ciency, in his opinion the following is necessary :-1, larger pipes in some of the streets for the water service ; 2, uniformity in the Fire Hydrants, and on account of the low water pressure to have more of them : 3, dis- tinct and clear Hydrant-Plates; 4, a Fire Escape of modern construction ; 5, a Horse Hose Tender ; 6, the Canvas Dams to have side inlets ; 7. Electric calls for the Firemen ; 8, Serge Suits for the Firemen to be kept at their homes ;9, more frequent practice ; 10, four or six additional Firemen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>WARRINGTON WESLEYAN SUNDAY</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WESLEYAN SUNDAY</p>
        <p>SCHOOL UNION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Sunday School Union in connection with the Sunday schools of the Warrington Wesleyan circuit was held in the Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom on Thursday evening. Previous to the tran- saction of business, a tea was provided at which about 30 sat down. A discussion ensued at which representa- tives from the various Sunday schools gave an account of • the work at the places they represented. A very profit- able time was thus spent. At eight o'clock, a public meeting was held, presided over by Mr. R. Garnett. IP., who was accompanied on ; the platform by the Rev. G. D. Mason, the Rev. Mr. ; Goodman, of Bowden, and Mr. C. H. Pigot. After sing- , ' ing and prayer, The CIIAIRMAN, in his opening address, said that the position in which he was placed was not one he was suited for,but one which he occupied with great pleasure. The Suhday school had at all times had his very earnekt and lovieg sympathy. He was glad to see that meeting that night and to see such a large representation from his own place. (Applause.) If it were not for Penketb and Sankey Bridges, he did not know what Warrington would do. (Laughter.) He believed in the movement which was set on foot of visiting the Sabbath schools. They must have the effect of being serviceable.He would like to have the facts about the schools of the circuit. He believed that Bold-st. and Sankey Bridges were fairly well circumstanced with their schools, but they were the only two which were so. They at Penketh were unfor- tunately circumstanced,as they were very much in want of a number of classrooms. To get to the hearts of the echolars, the teachers must have them separated from school. The importance of Sunday sclool work it was impossible ever to estimate. There. was a good number of teachers who did not think the work was of so much importance that it should be absolutely from Sabbath to Sabbath, punctual and regular. But the work of the Sunday School teacher was as important as that of the minister. The teacher was able to work where the iefluence of the ministers and local preachers was comparatively little. To be a successful teacher needed a mental, moral, and spiritual equipment. He should know what he was going to speak about, and not trust to the inspiration of the moment. At Penketh,some years age, they devoted Saturday evenings to the study of the lesson for the Sabbath, but they found that the teachers who most needed to be taught themselves were the least likely to come and partake of the blessing. The teachers best up in the questions were always there. To be a successful teacher one must be an earnest Christian. It was no use trying to take children a way one was net going one's-self. A teacher should also be enthusiastic and earnest in his work, and he need hardly refer to the necessity of punctuality and regularity. A teacher ought to make hislessona interesting as well as profitable, and he should try to take advantage of everything which threw light upon the subject. He himself always found it best to prepare the lesson on Sunday evening for the succeeding Sabbatti,and thus the subject was not then a new one. The Rev. Mr. GOODMAN then gave a very interesting work, accom- paniedysuggestions as to the teresting should be dosie. an manner o qb iuyne many ahnih taddresshe illustrations wupo 9n r k Ss hu oan und The meeting closed with a vote of thanks to Mr. Goo- dman, on the motion of the CHAIRMAN, seconded by Mr. W. sitcom, and the pronouncing of the Benediction by the Rev. Owen Watkins.</p>
        <p>Some idea of the terrible competition for governess. ships may be gained from an advertisement in the "Times." A lady there announces that she "has selected a nursery governess, and begs to inform the 700 other candidates that the photographs with which stamps were sent will be presently returned." Major Ranh, M.P., has announced his intention of in- troducing.in conjunction with Colonel Sir C. H.Vincent, Mr. Radcliffe Cooke, and Mr. John BUMS, a bill to limit speeches in Parliament, allowing Ministers one hour and ordinary members twenty minutes each.</p>
