      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE SHIPPING TRADE OF MANCHESTER. The sea-going traffic upon the Ship Canal during the mouth of January has been of an extremely satisfactory character,and it is probable that when the orficial return of the weight of merchandise handled and gross earn- ings are published they will show an improvement upon all previous records. During the month the first steamers arrived in Manchester from Australia, Boston (Mass), Norfolk (Virginia), and Marseilles, and a stew service to Glasgow was commenced, bringing the number of sailings to that port from Manchester up tr six weekly. 23,347 bales of Amcrican cotton and 13,652 bales of Etzyptian cotton arrived at the docks in Janu- ary—a larger quantity thin has been handled in any tw. months since the opening of the canal—and nearly 6,000 bales of wool—the first imported direct. Last week', traffic,although slightly less than that for the previou• seven days ,was fully up to the average. Amongst th. imports were twenty-one cargoes of general merchan dise,seventeen of pottery materials, Szc., seven of ston, and slate, four of pig iron, fcur of wood pulp, three ot logwood, two of iron ore, one of cotton, one of greet fruit, one of deal ends, and one of bath-brick Twenty two general cargoes were exported,seven of coal,and on, of salt. •</p>
        <p>ESuicide is on the increase. The F uicides in 1895 run tiered 5,750, as ccanpAred with 4,912 in 1891, 2,040 IL 1890, and 978 in 1385.</p>
      </div>
