      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Rettero to tin Obitor.</head>
        <p>Rettero to tin Obitor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. A letter from " Vivian Grey " has been held over.</p>
        <p>THE SCHOOL ATTENDANCE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I, through your valuable columns, call the attention of school managers and teachers, who fre- quently have cause to complain of the poor attendance or lateness of their scholars, to the very successful method to encourage regular attendance in vogue at Fairfield Girls School ? A good prize is given to all girls who have not once been absent or late during the year. The prizes were distributed on January 17th, and it must have been a very pleasing and gratifying function to the managers of the school, and to the excellent mis- tress, Mrs. Stansfield, for no less than twenty-nine girls gained the above prize. Amon.' the twenty-nine, one girl, Maggie Fearneough, has taken it four years in suc- cession, :and five girls, Sarah Darbyshire, Ruth Ellen Sykes,Frances Stockton,Naomi Berry, and Small Morris, three years in succession. If seemed to me that this method might very well be introduced into other schools for, apart from other great advantages, it would confer a great benefit on the scholars by brightening their school life, and encouraging a spirit of healthy rivalry and emulation ; and the cost of its adoption would be more than made up by the increased grant gained by a higher average attendance.—Yours faithfully, SPES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>MR. PEERS ON THE ATONEMENT.</head>
        <p>MR. PEERS ON THE ATONEMENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of January Ilth,you kindly allowed me to correct three mis-statements which Mr. Peers had made in one short letter. Will you now permit me to protest against another error which appears in your issue of January 25th. Mr. Peers describes the Christian doctrine of the Atonement as meaning "the sacrifice of the innocent to procure the safety of the guilty," and asserts that this doctrine rests upon the story of the Fall. This assertion has the support of some of those Christian preachers and writers whom Mr. Peers so scorn- fully denounces, but is, as a matter of fact, baseless and erroneous. The doctrine of the Atonement is one about which there has be2n, and still is, great diversity of opinion, and which has been presented in various forms. The ultimate facts with which it deals, however, will, I sup- pose,scarcely be challenged even by Mr. Peers. These are man's sin and God's righteousness. Whatever we may think about th 3 origin of sin, its existence cannot be disputed ; and Christianity teaches that sin is the barrier which prevents direct personal relationship between God and man. How are these two to be made at one? The possibility of such a making at one, an Atonement, was revealed by Jesus,who is for Christians a living not a dead Christ, and who during His earthly ministry showed how this atonement is to be effected. His teach - kg shines clerrly enough through the refracting media of apostles and evangelists. The difficulties of this "making at one " are not on the Divine but on the human side; and as soon as a man strives by His help, which is freely given to those who seek it, to live in accordance with the Divine will, to sacrifice himself, the atonement is—for that man—effected. The sacrifice of Jesus was not reLtricted to the mere murder on Cal- vary but extended through all " those aimless years be- neath the Syrian blue," and the Atonement is not so much a past historical event as an incident in the life of every true Christian. I think that if Mr. Peers would try to understand the real strength of the Christian position, his letters would gain in value even though they lost somewhat of their spiciness.—Yours, &amp;c., LA I MAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WORD TO " ZA.MINER"</head>
        <p>A WORD TO " ZA.MINER"</p>
        <p>Sir,—Youi-cOirespondent, " Zaminer," says he does I not intend to continue the discussion with me and then goes on to continue it. He rightly conjectures that I allude to the orthodox dogmas of Atonement and Re- demption,and says they are triths taught by Christ and his apostles. But I must demur to the statement that Jesus taught such a dogma as Original Sin and its re- quired atonement. His teaching was mostly in parables by which He illustrated in simple language truths and principles affecting the life and destiny of the human race. Will " Zaminer " tell me which of them contains this doctrine of vicarious atonement? The parable of the Prodigal Son entirely discards it, and his description of the last judgment goes dead against such a doctrine. His condemnation of the wicked and unfaithful is not for lack of acceptance of his " glorious merits." but for their want of loving and helpful sympathy. "I was an hungered and ye gave nae no meat ;" "I was sick and in prison and ye visited me not ;" "Naked and ye clothed me not ;" " Inasmuch as ye did it not to the least of these my brethren ye did it not to me." Character and conduct, not" trusting in the precious , blood," was the constant theme of His teaching. Love to God and man exemplified by deeds of mercy and goodwill He holds up for our imitation. The good Samaritan though a member of an heretical and un- orthodox nation is singled out for approval while the orthodox priest and Levite who are so intent on their religious performances that they cannot stop to do a deed of mercy, have earned the contempt of succeeding ages. " Zaminer "objects to my putting Paul's teaching on a lower level than the Master's,but much as I admire the great apostle I cannot do otherwise. He doubts Paul's leanings to Calvinism. I only know that trained as I was from early years in that bitter faith, I found in the writings of this apostle my strongest arguments for its defence. - -</p>
        <p>With regard to the question as to whether Jesus was known as Jesus Christ, His very words to Peter quoted by " Zaminer," shows that He was not thus knowd.But there is one of Mr. Peer's charges which I tabulated, which your correspondent seems to have ignored (No. 4) —the question of Paul's non-condemnation of slavery. I prelume it was from a dread of admitting that there could be anything defective in the sacred records. Why not accept the fact as I stated it, that the Christian con- science has advanced in light and knowledge, and that whether we like it or not, even the apostolic writers were not infallible. Of what use is the injunction to" prove all things and hold fast that which is good," if we are expected to accept everything because written by good men 1800 years ago. The bible is of priceless value but it requires to be read intelligently—not worshipped as a Fetish. If it was thus more generally read much of the God dishonoured teaching which now disfigures Christianity would vanish like an evil dream.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>A LEARNER.</p>
        <p>MR. JOHN PEERS AND HIS AMENITIES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your correspondent, Mr. Peers should not get so unreasonably angry with those who happen to resent in necessarily strong terms the badinage with which he is in the habit of treating others, and what is worse, the profane assurance with which he choses to handle what most right-minded men deem to be amongst things the most sacred. He is an admirer of "sweet reasonable- ness," but does not hesitate to dub his critics as "salva- tion " hounds or" harriers." His amenities are durably stereotyped, and he has no cause for complaint if his methods are fittingly described. He is grievously hurt, as it would seem with " Zaminer's " mild attack upon one of his idols, and re- minds him that •' his distance, his insignificance and obscurity "alone could shield him" from a well-merited chastisement." Now to one who happens to know something about Ingersoll this is vastly amusing. A pamphlet lies near me,in which this Corypheus of infidelity is handled both in his character and arguments with a scathing power which ought to have evoked a reply at least,if not some kind of " chastisement." But the Colonel's bravery lay in his discretion, and this, let Mr. Peers specially not, although no Atlantic rolled between him and his assail- ant—nor yet any "insignificance or obscurity" to boot; for Father Lambert was too well known in New York. Just listen Mr. Peers how a brave defender of the faith closed his address upon a man whom I could only think of as a" windbag." " We have said enough to convince our readers that Mr. Ingersoll is profligate of statement; that he is not to be trusted ; that he is unscrupulous ; that as a logician and metaphysician he is beneath con- tempt; that he is a mere galvanizer of old objections long ago refuted ; that he is ignorant and superficial ; full of gas and gush ; in a word that be is a philosophical charlatan of the first water, who mistakes ccrious listeners for disciples, and applause for approval." Father L imbnt adds a postscript to his book—" As I anticipated,Mr.lngersoll has publicly declined to reply "; and well he might, for he was, de facto smitten, be- leaE uered, overwhelmed and crushed in an encounter which reminds one for its decisiveness of that " awful squelch at Sedan "twenty-five years ago. And now for a few words on the "Missionary tub." Not first the amenities—Christ's obedient servants are descrit 2d as" intrusive fanatics " ; their work is only "a mania" and a failure—a mere "monument of Pharisaical arrogance." For the present I pass by the untruthfulness of this rhodomentade, and would ask Mr. Peers to aim at a little more exactness in his statements. Who, for instance, are they pray, who attempt to " force "Christianity upon a peciple unwilling to receive it ? There is not a Society in existence that does auc.tht of the kind. Use the right words Mr. Peers. Christians go forth under a divine command to offer a divine bless- ing, and to persuade risen and not to force. Do you pre- sume to tell them they have no right to do this ? Will you tell us then what right you have in almost weekly diatribes to offer your nostrums to an enlightened and a vast majority of an unwilling public? You think that you have a panacea which will restore men to what you call rationality, naturalism and common sense ; and you endeavour to persuade people that the right step towards this is to accept the fads of ac-called scientists, and to reject the teachings of One who has given life to the world. We don't call in question your right to do this —to offer and to persuade if you can, however much we may deny the sanity of such an effort. But we no more attempt to force Christianity upon China and India than we allow you to force upon us a dead stone for living bread. But time and space are up, and I must defer to another paper, Mr. Editor, by your leave, for a further notice of Mr. Peers' bad logic and ignorance of well known facts.—Yours truly, 30th January, 1896</p>
        <p>ZAMINER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 8, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 8, 1896.</p>
        <p>afraid that the works to be executed under the powers to be conferred by the Bill to be submitted to Parlia- ment in the ensuing session would have the effect of raising the waters in the Canal and the river Weaver to such an extent as to continually place t:e outfalls of the sewers under water, and more- over would considerably add to the pollution of the Canal and the river Weaver. The representatives of the Canal Company, however, assured the deputation that the waters would not be raised above the present levels and that the works would have the effect of im- proving rather than otherwise the waters of the canal and the river. The deputation intimated that the Coun- cil would ask to have inserted in the Bill a clause to pro- tect their interests,though they were not prepared at the present time to submit the terms thereof, but referred to the clause which was inserted in the iManchester Ship Canal Act,lB94,"when the representatives oft heCompany stated that they did not think there would be any objec- tion to the insertion in the present Bill of a similar clause. The deputation afterwards met at the Clerk's office in Runcorn,when it was resolved that a consulting engineer be called in to advise the Council.—At a sub- sequent meeting of the Parliamentary Committee on Saturday, the Ist inst., it was resolved on the motion of Mr.Heywood,seconded by Mr.Aaron,that subject to an interview with Messrs. Brandreth, Heywood, Chadwick, and Pilling and Mr. Diegle (surveyor), and such inter- view being satisfactory, Messrs. Beloe and Priest, of Liverpool, be appointed consulting engineers. He (Mr. Brandreth) moved the adoption of the report, and stated that they thought it was better to have an engineer who understood all the technical points connected with levels, &amp;c. ,so as to guide them in the framing of a clause to be inserted in the Bill safeguarding the Council's in- terests.—Mr.Aaron seconded the adoption of the report, and it was carried. COUNTY .COUNCIL ACCOUNTS, &amp;C.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Parliament Committee on Sa.tur- dag, the Ist February, the following resolution of the Malpas Union was approved: "That this Council res- pectfully request the County Council to allow to be pub- lished at a reasonable price the county accountant's annual abstract of accounts in order that any ratepayer may study therein the details of County expenditure and that a copy of this resolution be forwarded to the County Council." Tne committee also resolved on the motion of Mr. Woodward to petition in favour of a bill to be introduced into Parliament by the Nantwich Rural District Council, the object whereof was to give facili- ties to landowners of limited interests and to others to charge their estates with sums expended or with sums subscribed or guaranteed by them for the construction of waterworks and to enter into arrangements with Dis- trict Councils to as3ist such in the construction of works of water supply for their districts.—Mr. Brandreth moved, and Mr. Darbvshire seconded the adoption of the resAutions of the Committee, and they were agreed to.</p>
        <p>WATER ANALYSES.</p>
        <p>The Inspector submitted his annual return of the num- ber of samples of water submitted for analysis during 1895. The total number was 64, of which 21 were re- turned ascot fit for domestic use. Of the latter 15 were sent from Frodsham, together with others of doubtful purity. This in itself was conclusive evidence that a public water supply was greatly needed for such an im- portant place.</p>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT..</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams -reported that there were five cases of scarlet fever at Frodsham ; three at Hatton ; one at Stretton, and two or three fresh cases at Halton. There was an epidemic at Appleton, and he suggested that the schools be closed for three weeks as the disease was chiefly among school children.—The report was adopted. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon, the fortnightly meeting of this Board was held under the presidency of Mr. J. Knowles. There were also present the Revs. Dr. Gastaldi and J. Simpson, Mi.ssrs. S. Taylor, Z.P., C.C., T. Lea, S. Yarwood and A. R. Norman, with the members of the Runcorn Rural District Council. In the absence of Mr. Ashton, clerk, through indisposition, Mr. George Ashton was appointed to the position, •</p>
        <p>MAINTENANCE OP PAUPER LUNATICS. Mr. A. Etornby, clerk to the C)unty Asylum Commit- tee, Chester, notified that the rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics in that asylum for the quarter ending 31st March, was filed at 7s. per head per week.—lt was pointed out that this was an increase of sevenpence per head on the previous quarter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>REMOVAL OF MRS. MARSDEN.</head>
        <p>THE REMOVAL OF MRS. MARSDEN.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Marsden, Astoa Vicarage, Sutton, wrote stating that in consequelaze of her intended removal from Sutton she was sorry to discontinue her visits to the Runcorn Union. It had been a pleasure to her to join with the members of the Ladies Visiting Committee in their en- deavours to promote the comfort and welfare of both women and children. As she was under the necessity of tendering her resignation, she wished to thank the Board of buardians for permitting her to take any part in measures designed for ameliorating, the condition of the poor.—Tt was decided on tho motion of Mr. Wright, , seconded by Mr. Aaron, to accord Mrs. Marsden a vote of thanks for her services,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>VAGRANCY.</head>
        <p>VAGRANCY.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the conference of representatives of Boards of Guardians in Warwickshire, and of the Board of Guardians of the Stratford-on-Avon Union, submitted the following memorial which had been adopted, ana a,sked for the Board's decision " That every Union in England and Wales be asked to join in requesting the Local Government Board to urge the Government to ap- point a special commission for the purpose of revising the vagrancy law in order that uniform treatment may be enforced at all relieving stations." It was decided to support the memorial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PROPOSED GRAVING DOCK AT WESTON.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED GRAVING DOCK AT WESTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Aaron introduced this subject, and in reply to an objection by Mr. Yarwood, contended that the matter could be discussed by the Board of Guardians. He said Runcorn was at one time a shipbuilding port but nothing had been built there since the makinc, of the Ship Canal. The disused dock at Weston belonged to the ratepayers of Cheshire and was under the management of trustees. In support of the proposal to use it as a graying dock, _ . .</p>
        <p>it was stated that there were many timber snips, enter- ing the canal about 20 of which were towed either to Ellesmere Port to the pontoon dry dock there, or to Liverpool dry dock. In many instances those vessels after repair which cost above £5,000 had to be towed back to Saltport or Weston Pointsto load with salt. Con- sequently there was a great amount of delay and ex- pense. The total registered tonnage of foreign vessels alone which traded to Runcorn and Weston Point, in- cluding 80 steamers,was 65,089 tons, represented by 162 vessels. Those alone would almost keep a dry dock going,but in addition to that there were coasting vessels, steam and sail, representing a registered tonnage of 226,520 tons. Nearly the whole of those vessels, both foreign and home, would use the dry dock if one were made. The size of the dry dock which it was proposed should be made out of the wet sue at Weston which was now known as the new basin was 620 feet long by 120 feet wide, and ' would be equal to one of the finest dry docks in Liverpool, if con- structed. The outlay would be very small as so little required doing, and it could be divided into two dock by a centre pair of gates. The length of the one would be 400 feet from large vessels and the other 220 for small ones. The present entrance would require widening to 55 feet and unless the Ship Canal Company obtained permission to close the outlets, the sill would have to be lowered 4ft. 6in.,as there was only 9ft. Gin. of water over the sill when the Ship Canal was at its normal level, but if the Company closed the outlet then the height of the water would be raised perhaps 3 or 4 feet, and if so this would dispense with the necessity of lowering the sill and so keep down the expense of con- struction,and consequently be a greater inducement to the trustees to go on with the work. All those who were directly connected with shippicg at Runcorn and Weston Point had given their word that whatever they might require doing to their vessels should be done in the dry dock when vessels required dry docking to say nothing of the number of accidents which had occurred during</p>
        <p>the past twelve months. He ventured to say the acci- dents to vessels alone had been the means of defraying all the expenses involved in bringing the pJntoon docks into the Ship Canal. If the Weaver trustees thought fit to opcn the basin as a graving dock it would bring a great number of artisans to the district of Weston Point and Runcorn. He moved a resolution that the clerk write to the river Weaver trustees praying, them to con- vert the basin at Weston into a dry dock.—Mr. Baxter said it wonld be a benefit to Runcorn, Weston, and the whole Union.—Dr. Gastaldi seconded the proposition, and said the dock would be of great benefit to v.many people in Runcorn wanting work.—Mr. Eleywood sup- ported, and the proposition was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>DINING ROOM.</head>
        <p>THE DINING ROOM.</p>
        <p>A discussion took place with reference to the proposed enlargement of the dining room. Mr. Norman objected to any expenditure in regard to this matter. He said it was now common property, that one works in Runcorn was to be absolutely closed. He thought it was very inopportune te spend money on the enlargement of a dining room for the members, and he did not think it would meet with the approval of the ratepayers. After considerable discussion it was decided to refer the matter again to the House Committee. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL. DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL. DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This Council ms.t at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. J. Mclntyre, J.P., occupied the chair, and there were also present, Adm'ral Jones Parry, Messrs. H. E. Heywood, J. Brandreth, J. Knowles, S. Woodward, J. M. Williams, T. Baxter, C.C., H. ralbot, J.P.,T. Lewis, E. Aaron, T. Wright, W. Holt, J. Darby- shire, J. Weir, A. Greenway, W. Bradford, T. Darling- ton, S. Horton, J. Stokes, Mr. G. Ashton (clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer), Messrs. Swinton and Diggle (suryeyors),and Mr. J. Farrington (nuisance inspector).</p>
        <p>HELSBY WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Taylor, Ravenscar, Helsby, writing on Jan. 22nd, stated that in consequence of the water in his well aupplyhag his house failing rapidly, he was anxious to connect his house main with that of the Helsby and Dis- trict Water Company, now finished as far as the Robin Hood Inn. He desired to lay a pipe 3ft. deep from the Robin Hood, via Robin Hood-lane as far as the top, a distance of about 105 yards. The same gentlemau writ- ing again on Feb. 3rd, said he found he must have leave to put the pipe in question without further delay,unless the District Council were prepared to find a new source ef water in a few weeks at their expense on his property. The water level in his well had gone down still further and he believed his well was not singular in that res- pect,and it was 93ft. deep. He understood that the Water Company's supply from a bore hole, as shown by analy- sis, was a very good water and free from any impurity. —Mr. J.Crosland,of the Post Office,Helsby,wrote stating that he was purposing having a house built on land situated at the top of Robin Hood-lane, Helsby. He asked for Rermission to have a water pipe laid up the laed named, and perhaps for a short distance along the roan at the top to be connected with the Helsby Water Com- pany's main on the Chester-road.—Messrs. Potts, Potts, and Gardner, solicitors,Chester, in a letter dated Janu- ary 28th,said they were authorised to say that the water company's proposed scale of charges would be is. per thous,.nil gallons, including meter rent, for cottages of a rateable value not exceeding £7 10s. ; is. 3d. per 1,000 ; gallons, inchading meter rent, for cottages of a rateable Ivalue,ooo gallons exceedingonsf orh e otLr pIO and a minimum of is. 81. per r emises.—Mr. Heywood said the reason they did not give permission was because it was a matter over which the Council Should have some corn- trol as to what water should be supplied, and at such prices that the Local Government Board would sanction. They bad asked the company to supply the Council with a plan showing where the pipes were proposed to be laid, and to be willing to pass a Local Government Board enquiry and submit their scale of charges. On these terms they were willing to give permission to the com- pany to lay their pipes along the Council's roads. It appeared that they had not yet submitted a plan, and he did not see how the Council could grand permission with- out one. He proposed that the Clerk write the secretary to that effeet.—Mr. Aaron seconded, and said the Coun- cil were not going to give people permission to cut their roads, and then allow a company to charge just what they liked.—Mr. Woodward supported the proposition, and said the company seemed to be evading the condi- tions which the Council had made for granting permission to the company to lay pipes.—The Clerk stated that the Company had been three months in sending in their scale of charges. Referring to the price to be charged for water he said that if a cottage householder used the maximum quantity stipulated by the Local Government Board, it would amount to 18s. a year, whereas the Bard would not allow them to charge more than Bs. Bd. a year.—The proposition was carried.—Mr. Heywood said the matter affected the County Council, and that authority had granted the company permission to lay pipes on the main road. He (Mr. Heywood) thought it would be well to ask the County Council, when matters affecting the health of the district in which the Rural Council were specially interested, to consult them before coming to a decision. There was no doubt that Helsby should be provided with a proper water supply, but the Council might be landed in a difficulty with theseprivate enterprises. He moved that the Clerk write to the Coun- cil on the matter.—Mr. Woodward seconded the proposi- tion which was carried. THE SHIP CANAL 81LL... - -</p>
        <p>Mr. Brandreth Sl;t;tni-Eed-thn report of the deputation appointed to meet the representatives of the Manchester Ship Canal Company at the 13ridgewater House, Run- corn, on Wednesday the 22nd January. The members present were :—Messrs. Brandreths Heywood, Aaron, Chadwick, Waliingsam, Wright, and Roylance, also Messrs. Diggle and Swinton (surveyors), Mr. Farrington (inspector of nuisanc?), and Mr. J. Ashton (clerk). • The deputation explained that the District Council were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>INTERESTING • MARRIAGE AT</head>
        <p>INTERESTING • MARRIAGE AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Great interest was centred in the marriage of Mr. Barry Harrison, captain of the 2nd Earl of Chester Bat- talion Cheshire Regiment, younger son of the late Mr. Albert Harrison, solicitor, Frodsharn, and grandson of the late Mr. Robert Stevens Harrison. of Borne Abbey, Lincolnshire, to Maud Marc, only daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Henry White, Castle View, Runcorn. When the ceremony was performed at the Runcorn Parish Church on Wednesday noon there was a large• attendance within the precincts of the church. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in a white satin dress with long train, and on her head was a wreath of orange blossoms with the accompanying white veil. She carried a beautiful bouquet of lilies and tulips. There were two prettily attired children of Mr. F. A. Lake, solicitor, for braidesmaids and they carried baskets of flowers. The best man was Captain G. F. Ashton. The Rev. R. Edwards, incumbent of St. Winetred's, Birkenhead, and the Rev. H. B. Firth were the officiating clergymen. At the conclusion of the ceremony, Mr. Johnson, organist,played the well-known " Wedding March," and as the happy couple left the church with the good wishes of many friends and amid showers of rice, the bells played merry peals. Breakfast was partaken of at the residence of the bride's parents when numerous toasts were honoured. An "at home" was afterwards held and attended by many guests. The newly married couple, later in the afternoon, journeyed to Llandudno, where the honeymoon is being spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>ODDFELLOWSHIP IN HALTON AND</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWSHIP IN HALTON AND</p>
        <p>RUNCORN DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the annual meeting of the Halton and Runcorn district Grand United Order of Oddfellows.l comprising the Rose of Sharon lodge, 426 members. sick funds £2.919, Rising Sun, 252 members, sick funds £1,145, Star of Freedom, 277 members' sick funds £757 was held. Bro. W. .Lea presided, and amongst those present were—Brgg. Thomas Booth, George Hazlehurst, and Robert Hugta' trustees ; T. Davies, treasurer; W. Massey, secretary ; William Moston and Walter Moores, auditors ; Bros. James Douglas, George Rutter W. H. Bradbury, J. A. Shepherd and J. H. Douglas, delegates. The Secretary, in his annual statement of accounts, stated that on account of district funeral fund, contri- butions £219 ss. 7d. and interest £42 10s. 7d. had been received. £194 had been paid on account of deaths leaving the fund on January' 20th, £1057 18s. 30. an increase on the year of £67 7s. 7d. on this fund alone. The membership stood at 955. an increase of 13 on the year. The total worth of the district funersal fund and lodge funds was £5,960 an increase of £340 on the year. During the past five years. although £1,254 had been paid for deaths and £3,744 for sickness, the funds had increased by £l,OOO and the membership by 83. The juvenile society had 440 members and funds were rapidly increasing. Bro. Moores,the auditor,stated that he had carefully examined the books and accounts and found them cor- rect. The books were very well kept. The election of officers was proceeded with. Bro. William Lambert was appointed district master ; Bro. Walter Moores, deputy district master ; Bro. James Douglas,auditor ; and the trustees, treasurer and secre- tary were re-elected. The secretary's salary was ad- vanced £2. The consideration of the formation of a female lodge was adjourned as the Board of Dilectors had not been able to supply model rules. Bro.Flughes proposed a vote of thanks to Bro. Lea for his services' as D.M.—Bro. Rutter seconded the proposi- tion, which was carried with acclamation.—ln respond- ing Bro. Lea said the fulfilling of the position had been to him a source of pleasure and a: little education. Be then inuucted Bro. Lambert as D.M. and Bro.Moores as D.D.M., and theiproceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE LIBRA.RY LECTURES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRA.RY LECTURES.</p>
        <p>ADVICE ON BOOKS-</p>
        <p>'1 he last of this series of lectures was given in the Technical Institute on Friday evening by Dr. Bowman who gave some most valuable advice on " Books : their use and abuse." Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P., occupied the chair, and he was supported on the platforrn by Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., E. Imison, J.P., S. Pope, and J. D. Jones (librarian). Dr. Bowman said he found in a library thoughts of the best, the highest, and noblest minds that our race had procured. After alluding to his judgment of meii by the books they read, and the facilities with which men could so easily acquaint themselves with the works of the best writers—especially now that they had a Free Library system, he said the man who had not a taste for any of the books in the library had a mind of ignoble mould. He proceeded to trace the history of literature from the earliest times, alluded to its extensive range, and expressed the mpinion that by a study of history they would find something far more fascinating than a novel. They would see how small causes had led to wonderful results, how the world had gradually been developed, and how history repeated itself. If they were of a scien- tific turn of mind there were ample subjects to engage their attention. Books were food for the mind,and that was one of their great uses. He believed that the feeding of the mind was quite as necessary as that of the body, but the former was fed at the public expense by means of free libraries such as they possessed. He urged them under no circumstances to starve the mind. Some men had mental starvation depicted in their faces. By toil, hard work, and the sparing of neither time, labour, nor training, men had elucidated difficult problems, and in a thousand ways people by their experience could begin on a higher level. The fact that they could transmit experiences was the difference between man and beast. He urged them to avoid per- nicious literature, a flood of which was issuing from the press to-day, which was poisonous to the mind. Whatever they did they should select a few books as their bosom companions, and they would find that the sweetest hours of human life were those spent in the presence of kindred souls. On the motion of Mr. Taylor, seoonded by Mr.lmison, a vote of thanks was accorded to Dr. Bowman for his lecture, and a similar compliment was passed to the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOA.RD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOA.RD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held in the Board-room at the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Tuesday evening. Mr. J. Pritchard occupied the chair,and there were also present —Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Dr. McDougall, Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., E. Imison, J.P., F. J. Norman, J.P., and Mr. G. Moss (clerk).</p>
        <p>DR. MCDOUGALL OBJECTS TO THE MINUTES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the minutes of the previous meeting in which a vote of thanks to Dr. McDougall was recorded for his services as vice-chairman. Dr. McDougall said he was quite sure he should feel exceedingly grate- ful to the members of the Board for passing a vote of thanks for any services he might have rendered in the cause of education. But he was quite content to leave that for the public to decide. If he had rendered any service to the public he was perfectly satisfied. He was quite sure it was not competent for that Board to pass any resolution commending any services he might have rendered during the existence of the old Board which ex- pired at the beginning of January. However grateful he might feel, he asked that that portion be deleted from the minutes. It might have been sincere,but he was satisfied that he had no right to have it passed by the new Board, and placed on the minutes of its first meet ing. Therefore he begged with all respect, that the motion be deleted. He was sure that Dr. Gastaldi who was not present then, would second his proposition. —The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi did not rise to second the pro- position, and Mr. Norman evidently advising him not to second it, Dr. McDougall addressing Mr. Norman said he was not going to take any dictation from him.— Mr. Norman said he was sure he was not going to take dictation from Dr. McDougall, and the sooner he and Dr. McDougall understood each other the better. He (Mr. Norman) was responsible for the resolution passed, and he accepted his full share of the responsibility, and he was sure the other members were prepared to do the same. They were all of one opinion in the matter. They thought that the vote of thanks had been forgotten on the previous occasion, and he did not want to enter into details as to the cause of that forgetfulness. He hoped that Dr. McDougall would not press his objection.—Dr. McDougall said he would not under any consideration allow the minutes to stand as at present. He could not accept it as an expression of the Board, and it had set an extremely bad precedent.—The Chairman said as the minutes were an exact record of the proceedings he Old not see how they could be amended.—Dr.McDougall said he much preferred that the matter sh. uld now be ended, and the minutes altered. He felt it was the proper course to take.—Mr. Imison said he felt that Dr. McDougall's wishes should be consulted, but he hoped Dr. McDougall would accept the vote in the spirit it was given.—Mr. Norman said if the members had to go over the same ground they would do the same again. They were all anxious to pay a tribute to Dr. McDou- gall's services. If he liked to refuse to accept it he could do so. If Dr. McDougall objected to it, he suspected that his protest would appear on the minutes. He (Mr. Norman) did not think they were competent to rescind the present minute.—Dr. Gastaldi said so long as the members had passed the vote of thanks in the spirit which had been explained he thought he would be stul- tifying the Board to second the amendment. —Dr. McDougall said the members had created the difficulty for themselves. There was no precedent for their action. —Mr. Norman said the members took the responsibility of their action. He preferred to take the irregular course than to be subjected to the charge that he had been dis- courteous to Dr. McDougall, i thought which none of them were guilty of.—Dr. McDougall said his protest must be entered ,un the minutes.—The minutes were then passed. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of a meeting of the School Attendance Committee held on the 28th ult, showed that the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi was appointed chairman for the ensuing three years. There were summoned 23 parents, of whom 21 appeared, and it was ordered that three be summoned before the magistrates. The visitor's report for the month ending January 24th showed that the total num- ber of children on the registers of the elementary schools was 3,400, being 30 less than 12 months ago. The atten- dance was eighty per cent. showing a total of 2739, a decrease of 16 on the figures of last January.—Dr. Gas- taldi in moving the adoption of the report said the diminition was chiefly owing to the non-attendance of infants,—Mr. Taylor seconded, and the minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee showed that Mr. F. J. Norman was elected chairman for the ensuing three years. It was also recommended that Miss C.H. Hough, of Winsford be appointed assistant mistress at Greenway- road Board Schools at a salary of £5O per annum; that the accumulated arrears of fees £5 169.11 d. at Granville. street and £l6 93. sd. at Brunswick-street schools be re- mitted;and that in future all scholars be admitted to these schools without payment of fees; that the name of Green- way-road schools be changed to Victoria-road schools.— The Chairman moved the confirmation of the minutes, and Dr. Gastaldi seconded.—Dr. McDougall thought something should be said with reference to the freeing of the Brunswick-street and Granville-street schools. They calculated when they took over the schools that the fees would be somcthing over £lOO. As a matter of fact when giving an estimate to th. overseers as to the pre- cept the Board would require, that sum was taken into account. But it appeared that during December the sums paid in fees dwindled to nil, and the consequence was that the ratepayers would have to pay the fees. He did not think the ratepayers were in a position to pay fees in that way. The schools would now be free, but it did not appear to him that much energy or effort had been made to recover the fees. They were now thrown on the rates, and the responsibility of pay- ing the cost of the schools would fall upon the ratepayers generally, and the £lOO would have to be provided for out of the rates instead of by those who were taking advantage of the education. The Chair- man said it might be quite as well to show the difference between the freeing of the Green- way-road schools and the Wesleyan schools. At the time they rescinded the fees at the former schools the Board was receiving threepence in fees from the children, but the Government came forward and gave them 10s. a-head more which left but a very small sum to be realised from the children—something less than id. per child per week,a sum which they did not think worth the trouble to collect. They did not do so, and it was but a loss of £2O or £3O to the town. With reference to the Wesleyan schools, they took them over as a going concern, and the Board anticipated a revenue of about £lOO a year for school fees. In consequence of an agita- tion the Board had been reluctantly compelled to free the schools. They had thought to continue the fees until the new schools were erected, when under new arrange- ments the burden on the ratepayers would not have been so great. But as it was now,the ratepayers would have to bear this £lOO imposed upon them in consequence of this agitation.—The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>THE WESLEY GUILD. —The monthly devotional meeting was held on Monday night, when there was a very plea- sant and profitable conversation on the subject of "Prayer." The Rev. C. de P. Gliddon presided. The attendance was 21 members and 3 companions. WESLEY GUILD SOIREE.—On Tuesday night, the choir of the Wesleyan Church held a soiree in the schoolroom, in aid of the funds of the Wesley Guild. The Rev. C. de P. Gliddon presided. There was a very large gathering, and the following programme a as much enjoyed :—Glee, "Pull away merrily," choir; solo, "The village black- smith," Mr. T. Barrow; solo, "Comrades," Mr. H. Rowe; duet, "The Gipsy Countess," Miss Barrow and Mr. T. Barrow; glee, " A_ hunting song," choir; comic song, "I can't change it," Mr. H. Rowe; pianoforte solo, "Scotch air," Mrs. Gliddon; glee, "Laugh and grow fat," choir; song, "We'd better bide a wee," Miss IBaguley ; solo, "The mighty deep," Mr. T. Barrow: duet, "The upper ten and lower five," Messrs. C. P. Bradbury and R. Johnson; duet, "Kind words," Rev. C. de P. and Mrs. Gliddon; glee, "Very sleepy," choir. Mrs. Gliddon and Messrs. Sutton and Johnson were the accompanists. Excellent refreshments were partaken of during an interval in the programme. After the for- mal dismissal of the company, the ysunger portion re- mained behind for games. TEE ANNUAL OLD WOMEN'S TEA PARTY.—This party took place in the Girl's School on Friday night. A capital tea was provided, and the guests,who numbered 50, thoroughly enjoyed it. The following ladies presided at the tables :—Mrs. Wray, Mrs. Stokes, Mrs. Collier, Mrs. Haddock, Mrs. Ireland (Shrewsbury), and Miss Newton (Bold). Assistance was also rendered by the following :—Mrs. Wright. Misses L. Wright, E. Wright, Stokes, Baker (Bold), and Jeacock. A good supply of provisions was sent to the houses of those whose in- firmities kept them away. Mrs. Wray gave an interest- ing address to the company. The Vicar, the Rev. G. Wray, proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Wright and his family for the interest they took in the annual gather- ing, and Mr. Wright acknowledze.d the vote. After tea, Professor Miller gave an exhibition of sleight .of-hand tricks, talking dolls, and Punch and Judy show, which the audience greatly enjoyed. Miss Clara Wright sang " The three fishers." Miss A. Shingler tendered " The Queen's Navee "and "The powdered monkey." Mr. G. H. Walton gave "Handel Moses " and " Johnny don't wriggle the baby," and Mr. A. Grounds contributed " My first wife,"" By the sad sea waves," and " Like Pa had yesterday." Miss C. Wright, Miss Jeacock, and Mr. W. Grounds acted as accompanists. The old ladies had a game of musical chairs, and also a dance or two, for the latter of which Mr. G. H. Walton played. Messrs. P. Wright and J. Taylor also did much in various ways to make the time a pleasant one. Before leaving, each of the guests received a Vb. of tea. The proceedings were brought to a close by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>EARLESTOWN CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The third of the Winter series of smoking concerts promoted by the Committee of the Earlestown Con- servative Club, took place in the large billiard room on Friday evening. Mr. James Smith, J.P. presided, and there was a large attendance of members. An excellent programme of songs, glees, and instru- mental selections was given by Messrs. Leyland, Peplow, Jukes, W. Jackson, G. Wright, F. Brierley, W. Kay, C. H. Lea, J. Howard, Appleton, Walsh and S. Swann,</p>
        <p>TEtE DISESTABLISHMENT OF THE PUBLIC HOUSE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LECTURE AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>LECTURE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The above was the subject of a lecture delivered by the Rev. Lloyd Jones, of Rhyl, in the Wesleyan Chapel, Earlestown, on Monday evening. The Rev. J. Bartley presided, and there was a good attend- ance.</p>
        <p>The LECTURER, in dealing with the effects of the drink traffic on the country, and the arguments of the trade in its favour, said the only literature to- day that depended upon the public-house was the Tory newspaper. He thought if the trade would take a tip from him,he could give them one. The trade, before the last election, told us that they would spend millions of pounds rather than that their trade should be destroyed. He believed them—since the last election. But he wanted to show them a depart- ment where their trade was valuable, Why did not they take £lO,OOO and go to Cassell's or some other large publishing house,and say "there is £lO,OOO if you will engage 10 men to write up our trade." He main- tained they could not find ten first class men to write up the trade. Sir D. Duckworth, surgeon to the Prince of Wales,laid down rules for the taking of liquor, but it must be absolutely pure,and then they must not take it during the day, and not more than liozs. of it. If they did it was injurious, and they must always take it with the last meal at night. He would not mind legislating on these lines. Up to the present hour no legislation on the liquor trade had assumed that the liquor trade was a good trade and a necessary one, but all legislation upon it had been upon the as- sumption that it was a bad trade ' and unnecessary. There was no law to determine at all how many milk shops there should be in Earlstown, they could have a whole street of them, and the excise man ' would not interfere, and so with chemists' and tailors' shops, but with regard to liquor shops, the Act of Parliament stepped in and said, "there shall be only so many." What was the difference? The trade was muzzled. The law said the houses should be closed at 11 o'clock at night, but if he was in the House of Parlia- ment, he should go in for their being open at night, and closed in the day. To use a phrase more forcible tifau eloquent "you give yourselves away, if you pass an Act of Parliament to sell drink." It was not a trade for making a country wealthier, but poorer. If they took the barley and put it into the depths of the sea,probably it would improve the mackerel, but if the mackerel would not touch it, it was better at the bottom of the sea than in the men and women. It was not a trade for the makine• of money, but the destroying of money. Then there was the question of the unemployed. In the manufacture, in the sale, in the distribution of liquor, they employed half a million of people. Take the money that employed that half million in drink, and transfer it to other legitimate trades, they would employ three millions and a half with it. Theoretically more than twice the population of the little country of Wales were out of work, because they put their money in the trade which required the least labour to produce. Theoretically they were keeping 3?-, millions out of work. The first reason why this evil was tolerated, was that very few people took the trouble to acquaint themselves with what was going on. 120,000 people lost their Fives in England every year from drink. He had a higher conception of the men in the trade, of publicans and brewers than to think that they would tolerate this if they looked at it. What did it mean ? Every five weeks more people were killed with drink, than lived in that parish. He did not bet eve that because one man was a sober man, and another was a drunkard, that one man was stronger than the other. It only showed that one, man was sus- ceptible to a physical disease that the other man was susceptible to. It was a pitiable disease. If it were a question merely of will force, he ;would not ask Parlia- ment to legislate for it. But as it was a disease equally as much as smallpox, he thought it was a question in which Parliament could interfere. No one hated drink, more than the man who took it. They c.)uld see it was a national evil eating at the very foundation of a man's will force. He was glad that England was more willing to-day that something should be done than ever becore. Of all people that ought to learn a lessen from this, teetotalers were the first that ought to learn it,but he was afraid they were the last that would. There were no men in England who could do so much for the temperance question as the clergy, if they would do it. Let the bishop spot the brewer in his corner pew and say," We shall not have your cursed money to build churches with." If any man told him that the Queen's government could not be carried on without killing 120,000 people a year, he should say curse the Queen's Government. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT CO UCH,.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT CO UCH,.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday afternoon,the chair be- ing occupied by Mr. William Houghton, and there were also present Messrs.-J. Houghton, G. Thompson, J.John- son, J. Dignum, T. Halton, J. W. Knowles, J. Ball, J. Warburton, S. Sutton (clerk), W. Owen (surveyor), F. Kerfoot (nuisance inspector), and Dr. Sephton (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>MR. LANGHAM'S CASE.</head>
        <p>MR. LANGHAM'S CASE.</p>
        <p>During the reading of the minutes,the Clerk said with reference to the case of Mr. Langham's son, that he had seen the Town Clerk and it was agreed not to publish the letters suggested. The agreement between the Cor- poration and the Council with regard to the hospital ac- commodation of patients was at an end.</p>
        <p>THE RECENT FEVER EPIDEMIC AT FA.DGATE.-HEAVY</p>
        <p>LIABILITIES OF THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC then read the bills from the Corporation for the patients who bad recently been discharged from the Fever Hospital. The bills in all amounted to £9l 103. 7d.—Mr.lixowLEs said it was time that the Council had a hospital of their own.</p>
        <p>THH TRREATENED ACTION BY MR. W. D. PIOUGHTON</p>
        <p>AGAINST THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said that in accordance with the wish of the Council, he had seen Mr.Wallhead with reference to the threatened action of Mr. W. D. Houghton for the alleged pollution of a brook in Penketh from which Mr.W.D.Houghton claimed to have the right to abstract water for his reservoir. Mr. Wellhead had said that he thGught the action would have to go on,but promised to see Mr.Houghton on the subject. Nothing had,however, been beard since from Mr. Wallhead. In the meantime Mr. Owen and Mr. Kerfoot had kindly gone through the correspondence, &amp;c., with regard to the case.—The CRAIRMAN : Then practically the case stands where it did at our last meeting.—The CLERK : Yes, except that we are in a state of preparedness.</p>
        <p>A COMPLAINT FROM THE LANCASHIRE ASYLUMS BOARD,</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. J.IP. Muspratt stating. that he was instructed by the Lancashire Asylum Board to call the attention of the Warrington Rural District Coun- cil to the public drain leading from Croft and Golborne, which went along the main road at Winwick, and dis- charged itself on land which would form part el the site of the new asylum building. He hoped that the Council would take steps to take away the sewerage and prevent its coming on the land. The Clerk said that this was a matter for the Inspector to report upon., and the Council decided to wait until their next meetingtfor a report. MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.—THE LONGFORD DEPOT</p>
        <p>NIIISANCE.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPHTON reported that during the month there had been 19 bil-hs and 13 deaths. The deaths showed an average death rate of 112 per 1,000, against 129, the average of the last three months, and 146, the corres- ponding month last year. The death rate from zymotic diseases was equal to 14 per 1,000, against 1.6, the average of the last three months and 3.2 the correspond- ing month last year. The two fatal cases of zymotic diseases were of enteric fever. During the month, the following cases of ithectious diseases had been reported: scarlet fever, six cases ; enteric fever, three cases ; and diphtheria, one case. He had heard many complaints of Longford depot, and the stench was said to he unbear- able and could be tasted as well as smelt.—Mr. Knowles ; I could smell it as far as my house.—The Chairman said that they had repeatedly gone to the Cor- poration, and they had as repeatedly promised to do something. Was there no improvement ?—The Nuisance Inspector: None whatever. It is worse than ever.— The Clerk : If you approach the Corporation they will tell you there is no smell.—Mr. Ball : I wish some men- ber of the Corporation had passed it this morning.—Mr. Knowles: The smell is very very bad. They are ruining the country round there. The town will never grow in that direction. They are ruining our rateable value.— The Clerk said that he would suggest that a deputation should go to the Corporation, but they had done that so repeatedly—Mr. Ball thought that they might try once more, and moved that a deputation be formed.—Mr. Halton seconded, and the motion was passed. The de- putation was composed of Messrs. Knowles, Ball, Halton and Thompson.</p>
        <p>THE DRAINAGE OF PADGATE.----DEPUTATION FROM THE</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL. The CHAIRMAN said that a deputation from the Poulton-with-Fearnhead Parish Council was waiting, out- side and as he happened to be a member of that Council, he was in rather a curious position. The members of the Board, however, requested him to retain his position as chairman during the time the deputation were pre- sent.—The deputation was then called in, and consisted of Messrs. Royston, Mather, Bennett,and Granger.—Mr. Royston, speaking on behalf of the deputution,said that they had come to meet the District Council because the Parish Council were not satisfied with the arrangements with regard to the drainage of the township. i"The ex- penditure by the plan proposed would be £2,700, a very large sum,and it would only drain a small portion of the parish,round Fearnhead Cross. If they took advantage of Warrington Corporation sewers, there would always be the cost of pumping, owing to the Warrington sewers being on the higher level than the township. The Parish Council thought that something could be done which would be of less expenditure by settling tanks. They would be very glad if the surveyor would report upon the matter.—lt was decided to request Mt.. Owen to re- port to the next meeting,</p>
        <p>NEWTON-LE-WILLO WS HORTICUL- TURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of this society was beld iU the Town Flail, Newton, on Tuesday evening. Mr. J. Randall presided, and tnere was a fair attendance of members.</p>
        <p>THE BALANCE SFIEET.</p>
        <p>The balance sheet for the past year showed receipts,io. eluding a balance of us. sd.,amounting to £B4. 15s.,and expenditure £73 16s. Old., leaving a balance of £lO. 18s. 111-d. The receipts for 1895 were much in excess of 1894, but there were several subscriptions still out, and several lar.ie subscribers he had written to but they had nct remitted to him yet. If they paid he thought it would increase the balance by about £s.—Mr.A.Smitl,. treasurer, moved that the balance sheet as read Ws, adopted. Everybody would agree that having regard to the very unfavourable day they had for their sports and show, the report they had had submitted to thera was a 'very satisfactory one indeed. They were much( obliged to those gentlemen,who had won prizes,for fore.. going the whole of the prize money they were entitled,to.- and by this means helping the society in its hour of need, thus stimulating them to go forward in the future to greater success. (Applause.)—Mr. Rutter seconded' and the accounts were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS</p>
        <p>The whole of the patrons were re-elected with the addition of the names of Mr. Thorpe (Mere liouse,)New• ton, and Mr. T. Molyneux, Earlestown. Mr. Smith feel: inglY alluded to the loss sustained by the death otr Colonel McCorquodale, and of Mr. T. H. Pearson, who had removed from the district, but it was some consol- tion to know that they had another patron in Mr.Thorpt,, who, he was sure, would he of the greatest assistance to them in the future. Mr. Smith was re-elected treasurer, and Mr. r. Williamson as secretary. On the motion of Mr. Schofield, it was decided to ask Mr. G. Boyer to be assistant secretary. The following were elected as a committee :—Messrs. Randall, Ackerley, Anderson, BeeS• ton, T. Cooper, jun., Jackson, Liptrott, C. V. Naylor, Schofield, Rutter, Senhton, Roscoe, Thompson, Cotterell, James, James Randall, E. Grace, with the power to add to their number. The alteration of rules and schedule were left in the hands of the committee. Mr. Smith moved that the thanks of the soeiety be tendered to the donors of prizes, to the Earlestown Foot bail Club for the loan of the field, and to the Baptist Cricket Club, who were kind enough to let them have access to the field a few days before the show, and at to the judges.—Mr.James seconded, and the motion waS unanimously carried. Thanks to the Chairman termin ated the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>FINANCIAL ANT) MINING NEWS</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL ANT) MINING NEWS</p>
        <p>ACHILLES GOLD.</p>
        <p>It is evident that at :the present time on the Lond Stock Exchange intrinsic worth is overlooked and that some securities are now to be bought at about one-hall their real value. One of the most notable instances illus. trating this fact is that of the shares of the AchilleS GAdtields Limited, which can be purchased at 3s, per. stare, fully paid up, which is about one half of the price at which they were selling on the London Stock Ex- change sme few months ago. This is somewhat remark- able in view of the excellent reports, both by cable and letter, received from the Managing Director at the mine in New Zealand ; and the shares cannot remain long at their present low quAation. He cablqs that the first crushing of 720 tons of ore produced 725 onnces gold,and that the 30 stamp mill already on tha property in treat' log the next parcel of quartz ; amounting to 200 tongs obtained 214 ounces gold from same. This is highlY satisfactory information; but intelligence has since come to hand that, as sinking is proceeded with, the mine' shows greater richness. At the 200rc. level, ore is being obtained, yielding on an average 10 oz. of gold to tbq ton; and at the 300 ft. level, the ore prodUces 20 ozs. or gold to the ton. That the shares must advance far ahoie their present price is beyond doubt,taking into considers" tion the above good news, and to investors, with spare cash at hand, this is an opportunity to pick up shares at low prices, which offer many attractions as an invest. merit, with every probability of their very shortlr I doubling in price.</p>
        <p>- Warringt on.—Printed and Publithed by JOHN WALKER &amp; q: at The Examiner General Pr,ntin7, Works, Bewsey-streed and Publishc 1 also at r.e Exa,ni:n,r Offices, Victoria-roV, , WIDNES ; Hieh-street. RUNCORN; 32. George-street. I-Yelens ; and 33, Market-street, EARLESTOWN.—FEBRITP. 8, 180.</p>
